,\"105\",{\"_197\":541,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":34,\"_527\":531},\"106\",{\"_197\":540,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},\"107\",{\"_197\":539,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},\"108\",{\"_197\":538,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},\"109\",{\"_197\":537,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},\"110\",{\"_197\":536,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},\"111\",{\"_197\":535,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},\"112\",{\"_197\":534,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":34,\"_527\":531},\"113\",{\"_197\":533,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},\"114\",{\"_197\":532,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},\"115\",{\"_197\":530,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":34,\"_527\":531},\"116\",{\"_197\":529,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},\"117\",{\"_197\":528,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},\"118\",{\"_197\":524,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},\"4lm8kr\",\"edited\",\"previewable\",\"preview_language\",\"nb5xo8\",\"1fc19z\",\"bk512f\",\"html\",\"w2b6ft\",\"07yawy\",\"q8ywls\",\"lhc07d\",\"lxtqda\",\"a6w3d4\",\"dhr3ip\",\"9unnf7\",\"opfym6\",\"9bdo2e\",\"cra6xz\",\"8eufbg\",\"oe9ayz\",\"qw38y9\",\"eqv2yu\",\"hxx155\",\"4k7bhe\",\"ein7rm\",\"smo8zy\",\"sr109m\",\"g1td9n\",\"yhj7mz\",\"58upug\",\"99zazp\",\"t1x8qc\",\"xf9nr3\",\"o82qw7\",\"59krh0\",\"9q5j27\",\"1fv47g\",\"xyqdiu\",\"qvbkft\",\"9ynnwa\",\"vw9rfp\",\"o0oi1b\",\"j3qxd7\",\"jexuy7\",\"y703yw\",\"rnvago\",\"70asbk\",\"m2vg38\",\"ej55vj\",\"vbcv0g\",\"fzkpw6\",\"1rn87w\",\"14322y\",\"zbhqk0\",\"41uct1\",\"dzelb4\",\"zjfy9v\",\"unkkf3\",\"vr9aon\",\"fhxai6\",\"gtwkex\",\"eaoo3s\",\"opqw8y\",\"xfas1d\",\"xzu8z4\",\"u0bn3n\",\"8yk3h3\",\"pqu8kj\",\"8ypp6j\",\"j7swzv\",\"tn5cvw\",\"74p5mk\",\"8rjyrd\",\"9utg6o\",\"t3xwjj\",\"ykqckn\",\"67tti9\",\"4suwwt\",\"1fc5b7\",\"fhs4gi\",\"ca7kf8\",\"qj454x\",\"1ucj1c\",\"1wvbn2\",\"3i7h22\",\"pih70v\",\"7qfrg3\",\"pgaw0j\",\"hxfe5h\",\"3ua592\",\"rqxhsg\",\"5xs8hl\",\"u8ddnv\",\"av1j3t\",\"7stw81\",\"g2scj2\",\"rngenx\",\"qvdgmj\",\"ieagtv\",\"ts5ru8\",\"go2fso\",\"pbupv4\",\"5qbjqx\",\"pilyjo\",\"pcuhsn\",\"fb8dn9\",\"al7y5w\",\"g6pe9v\",\"l5rp7f\",\"xpaj6w\",\"8l6htx\",\"3kcsa6\",\"oxojbz\",\"9ulp5k\",\"zrejkp\",\"q72xen\",\"6462t1\",\"mll5o6\",\"26ez5w\",\"e4f4yc\",\"ksjnr4\",\"t018yb\",\"stop\",\"stop_tokens\",[650],200002,\"content_type\",\"text\",\"parts\",[655],\"# Phase 13 — Events \u0026 Event Handling in JavaScript\\n\\nYou already know the **DOM**. Now we need to learn how JavaScript actually **responds to user actions**.\\n\\nThink:\\n\\n\u003e **DOM = what exists on the page**  \\n\u003e **Events = what happens on the page**  \\n\u003e **Event listeners = how JavaScript reacts**\\n\\nThis is extremely important for React later.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1. What is an Event?\\n\\nAn **event** is something that happens in the browser.\\n\\nExamples:\\n\\n- User clicks a button → `click`\\n- User types → `input`\\n- User submits a form → `submit`\\n- User presses a keyboard key → `keydown`\\n- Mouse enters an element → `mouseover`\\n- Input gets focus → `focus`\\n- Page finishes loading → `load`\\n\\nExample HTML:\\n\\n```html\\n\u003cbutton id=\\\"btn\\\"\u003eClick Me\u003c/button\u003e\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst btn = document.getElementById(\\\"btn\\\");\\n\\nbtn.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", function() {\\n    console.log(\\\"Button clicked\\\");\\n});\\n```\\n\\nWhen the user clicks:\\n\\n```text\\nUser clicks\\n     ↓\\nBrowser detects click\\n     ↓\\n\\\"click\\\" event occurs\\n     ↓\\nJavaScript callback executes\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 2. `addEventListener()`\\n\\nThis is the most important method in this phase.\\n\\nSyntax:\\n\\n```javascript\\nelement.addEventListener(eventType, function);\\n```\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```javascript\\nbutton.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", handleClick);\\n```\\n\\nWhere:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction handleClick() {\\n    console.log(\\\"Clicked\\\");\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Java comparison\\n\\nThis is conceptually similar to Java GUI event listeners.\\n\\nFor example, in Swing:\\n\\n```java\\nbutton.addActionListener(e -\u003e {\\n    System.out.println(\\\"Clicked\\\");\\n});\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```javascript\\nbutton.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", () =\u003e {\\n    console.log(\\\"Clicked\\\");\\n});\\n```\\n\\nThe syntax is different, but the concept is similar:\\n\\n\u003e **Register a function that should run when an event occurs.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3. Callback Function\\n\\nThe function passed to `addEventListener()` is a **callback**.\\n\\n```javascript\\nbutton.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", function() {\\n    console.log(\\\"Hello\\\");\\n});\\n```\\n\\nThis function isn't executed immediately.\\n\\nYou are telling the browser:\\n\\n\u003e \\\"When a click happens, call this function.\\\"\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```javascript\\nbutton.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", handleClick);\\n```\\n\\ndoes **not** mean:\\n\\n```javascript\\nhandleClick();\\n```\\n\\nIt means:\\n\\n```text\\nStore handleClick\\n       ↓\\nWait for click\\n       ↓\\nClick occurs\\n       ↓\\nCall handleClick\\n```\\n\\n### Important interview question\\n\\nWhy don't we write:\\n\\n```javascript\\nbutton.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", handleClick());\\n```\\n\\nBecause that **calls the function immediately**.\\n\\nCorrect:\\n\\n```javascript\\nbutton.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", handleClick);\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 4. Event Object\\n\\nWhen an event occurs, the browser provides information about that event.\\n\\n```javascript\\nbutton.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", function(event) {\\n    console.log(event);\\n});\\n```\\n\\n`event` is an object containing information about the event.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n```javascript\\nbutton.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", function(event) {\\n    console.log(event.type);\\n});\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nclick\\n```\\n\\nYou can also inspect:\\n\\n```javascript\\nevent.target\\nevent.currentTarget\\nevent.type\\nevent.key\\nevent.code\\n```\\n\\ndepending on the event.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5. `event.target`\\n\\n`target` tells you:\\n\\n\u003e **Which element actually triggered the event?**\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```html\\n\u003cbutton id=\\\"btn\\\"\u003eClick Me\u003c/button\u003e\\n```\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst btn = document.getElementById(\\\"btn\\\");\\n\\nbtn.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", function(event) {\\n    console.log(event.target);\\n});\\n```\\n\\nThe target will be the button.\\n\\nYou can do:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconsole.log(event.target.textContent);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nClick Me\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 6. `event.currentTarget`\\n\\nThis is slightly different.\\n\\n```javascript\\nbutton.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", function(event) {\\n    console.log(event.target);\\n    console.log(event.currentTarget);\\n});\\n```\\n\\n### `target`\\n\\nThe element where the event **originated**.\\n\\n### `currentTarget`\\n\\nThe element whose event listener is **currently executing**.\\n\\nThis difference becomes very important with **event bubbling and delegation**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 7. Example: target vs currentTarget\\n\\nHTML:\\n\\n```html\\n\u003cdiv id=\\\"parent\\\"\u003e\\n    \u003cbutton id=\\\"child\\\"\u003eClick\u003c/button\u003e\\n\u003c/div\u003e\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst parent = document.getElementById(\\\"parent\\\");\\n\\nparent.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", function(event) {\\n    console.log(\\\"target:\\\", event.target);\\n    console.log(\\\"currentTarget:\\\", event.currentTarget);\\n});\\n```\\n\\nClick the button.\\n\\nYou may get:\\n\\n```text\\ntarget: button\\ncurrentTarget: div\\n```\\n\\nWhy?\\n\\nBecause:\\n\\n```text\\nClick happened on button\\n          ↓\\ntarget = button\\n          ↓\\nEvent bubbled to div\\n          ↓\\ndiv's listener executes\\n          ↓\\ncurrentTarget = div\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8. Common Events\\n\\nYou should know these very well.\\n\\n| Event | Meaning |\\n|---|---|\\n| `click` | Mouse click |\\n| `dblclick` | Double click |\\n| `input` | Value changes while typing |\\n| `change` | Value committed/changed |\\n| `submit` | Form submitted |\\n| `keydown` | Key pressed |\\n| `keyup` | Key released |\\n| `mouseover` | Mouse enters/over element |\\n| `mouseout` | Mouse leaves element |\\n| `mouseenter` | Mouse enters element |\\n| `mouseleave` | Mouse leaves element |\\n| `focus` | Element receives focus |\\n| `blur` | Element loses focus |\\n| `load` | Resource/page loaded |\\n| `DOMContentLoaded` | HTML parsed and DOM ready |\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9. `click`\\n\\n```javascript\\nbutton.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", function() {\\n    console.log(\\\"Clicked\\\");\\n});\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10. `input`\\n\\nVery important for forms.\\n\\nHTML:\\n\\n```html\\n\u003cinput id=\\\"username\\\"\u003e\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst input = document.getElementById(\\\"username\\\");\\n\\ninput.addEventListener(\\\"input\\\", function(event) {\\n    console.log(event.target.value);\\n});\\n```\\n\\nIf user types:\\n\\n```text\\nDevjeet\\n```\\n\\nYou might see:\\n\\n```text\\nD\\nDe\\nDev\\nDevj\\nDevje\\nDevjee\\nDevjeet\\n```\\n\\nThis is useful for:\\n\\n- Search boxes\\n- Live validation\\n- Autocomplete\\n- React controlled inputs\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 11. `change`\\n\\n```javascript\\ninput.addEventListener(\\\"change\\\", function(event) {\\n    console.log(event.target.value);\\n});\\n```\\n\\nA useful distinction:\\n\\n```text\\ninput\\n ↓\\nfires as value changes\\n\\nchange\\n ↓\\nfires when the change is committed\\n```\\n\\nFor text inputs, `change` commonly fires after the user changes the value and then leaves the field.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 12. Keyboard Events\\n\\nThere are three commonly discussed keyboard events:\\n\\n```text\\nkeydown\\nkeypress\\nkeyup\\n```\\n\\nFor modern code, focus mainly on:\\n\\n```javascript\\nkeydown\\nkeyup\\n```\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```javascript\\ndocument.addEventListener(\\\"keydown\\\", function(event) {\\n    console.log(event.key);\\n});\\n```\\n\\nPress:\\n\\n```text\\nA\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\na\\n```\\n\\nPress Enter:\\n\\n```text\\nEnter\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 13. `event.key` vs `event.code`\\n\\nThis is an interview-worthy distinction.\\n\\n```javascript\\ndocument.addEventListener(\\\"keydown\\\", function(event) {\\n    console.log(event.key);\\n    console.log(event.code);\\n});\\n```\\n\\nFor the A key:\\n\\n```text\\nkey  = \\\"a\\\"\\ncode = \\\"KeyA\\\"\\n```\\n\\nFor Enter:\\n\\n```text\\nkey  = \\\"Enter\\\"\\ncode = \\\"Enter\\\"\\n```\\n\\n`key` describes the character/key value.\\n\\n`code` identifies the physical keyboard key.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 14. Detecting Enter\\n\\n```javascript\\ndocument.addEventListener(\\\"keydown\\\", function(event) {\\n\\n    if (event.key === \\\"Enter\\\") {\\n        console.log(\\\"Enter pressed\\\");\\n    }\\n\\n});\\n```\\n\\nVery common in interviews and frontend applications.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 15. `preventDefault()`\\n\\nSome browser actions have default behavior.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n```html\\n\u003cform id=\\\"form\\\"\u003e\\n    \u003cinput\u003e\\n    \u003cbutton type=\\\"submit\\\"\u003eSubmit\u003c/button\u003e\\n\u003c/form\u003e\\n```\\n\\nNormally submitting the form can cause browser navigation/reload.\\n\\nWe can prevent it:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst form = document.getElementById(\\\"form\\\");\\n\\nform.addEventListener(\\\"submit\\\", function(event) {\\n\\n    event.preventDefault();\\n\\n    console.log(\\\"Form submitted without page reload\\\");\\n\\n});\\n```\\n\\nMental model:\\n\\n```text\\nEvent happens\\n     ↓\\nBrowser's default behavior\\n     ↓\\npreventDefault()\\n     ↓\\nCancel default behavior\\n```\\n\\n### Important\\n\\n`preventDefault()` does **not** stop the event from propagating.\\n\\nIt only prevents the browser's default action.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 16. `stopPropagation()`\\n\\nThis is different.\\n\\nSuppose:\\n\\n```html\\n\u003cdiv id=\\\"parent\\\"\u003e\\n    \u003cbutton id=\\\"child\\\"\u003eClick\u003c/button\u003e\\n\u003c/div\u003e\\n```\\n\\nBoth have listeners:\\n\\n```javascript\\nparent.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", function() {\\n    console.log(\\\"Parent\\\");\\n});\\n\\nchild.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", function(event) {\\n    console.log(\\\"Child\\\");\\n});\\n```\\n\\nClick button.\\n\\nYou may see:\\n\\n```text\\nChild\\nParent\\n```\\n\\nWhy?\\n\\nBecause the event **bubbles** from child to parent.\\n\\nYou can stop that:\\n\\n```javascript\\nchild.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", function(event) {\\n\\n    event.stopPropagation();\\n\\n    console.log(\\\"Child\\\");\\n\\n});\\n```\\n\\nNow:\\n\\n```text\\nChild\\n```\\n\\nThe parent listener doesn't receive the bubbling event.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 17. `preventDefault()` vs `stopPropagation()`\\n\\nMemorize this.\\n\\n| Method | Purpose |\\n|---|---|\\n| `preventDefault()` | Stop browser's default action |\\n| `stopPropagation()` | Stop event propagation |\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```javascript\\nevent.preventDefault();\\n```\\n\\nmeans:\\n\\n\u003e \\\"Don't perform the browser's normal action.\\\"\\n\\nWhereas:\\n\\n```javascript\\nevent.stopPropagation();\\n```\\n\\nmeans:\\n\\n\u003e \\\"Don't continue propagating this event.\\\"\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 18. Event Bubbling\\n\\nThis is one of the most important concepts.\\n\\nHTML:\\n\\n```html\\n\u003cdiv id=\\\"parent\\\"\u003e\\n    \u003cbutton id=\\\"child\\\"\u003eClick\u003c/button\u003e\\n\u003c/div\u003e\\n```\\n\\nListeners:\\n\\n```javascript\\nparent.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", () =\u003e {\\n    console.log(\\\"Parent\\\");\\n});\\n\\nchild.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", () =\u003e {\\n    console.log(\\\"Child\\\");\\n});\\n```\\n\\nClick button.\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nChild\\nParent\\n```\\n\\nWhy?\\n\\nThe event bubbles upward:\\n\\n```text\\nbutton\\n  ↑\\ndiv\\n  ↑\\nbody\\n  ↑\\nhtml\\n  ↑\\ndocument\\n  ↑\\nwindow\\n```\\n\\nThis is **event bubbling**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 19. Event Capturing\\n\\nEvents actually travel through phases.\\n\\nConceptually:\\n\\n```text\\n             WINDOW\\n                ↓\\n             DOCUMENT\\n                ↓\\n              HTML\\n                ↓\\n             PARENT\\n                ↓\\n              CHILD\\n                ↓\\n          TARGET EVENT\\n                ↑\\n             PARENT\\n                ↑\\n              HTML\\n                ↑\\n            DOCUMENT\\n```\\n\\nThere are three conceptual phases:\\n\\n### 1. Capturing phase\\n\\nEvent travels:\\n\\n```text\\ntop → target\\n```\\n\\n### 2. Target phase\\n\\nEvent reaches the target.\\n\\n### 3. Bubbling phase\\n\\nEvent travels:\\n\\n```text\\ntarget → top\\n```\\n\\nMost event handlers you write use **bubbling**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 20. Capturing Listener\\n\\nNormally:\\n\\n```javascript\\nparent.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", handler);\\n```\\n\\nuses bubbling.\\n\\nTo listen during capturing:\\n\\n```javascript\\nparent.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", handler, true);\\n```\\n\\nOr:\\n\\n```javascript\\nparent.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", handler, {\\n    capture: true\\n});\\n```\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```javascript\\nparent.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", function() {\\n    console.log(\\\"Parent\\\");\\n}, {\\n    capture: true\\n});\\n```\\n\\nNow when clicking the child, the parent handler can execute during the capturing phase.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 21. Complete Event Flow\\n\\nSuppose:\\n\\n```html\\n\u003cdiv id=\\\"parent\\\"\u003e\\n    \u003cbutton id=\\\"child\\\"\u003eClick\u003c/button\u003e\\n\u003c/div\u003e\\n```\\n\\nThe event flow is approximately:\\n\\n```text\\n1. window\\n2. document\\n3. html\\n4. parent\\n5. button ← target\\n6. parent\\n7. html\\n8. document\\n9. window\\n```\\n\\nThe first journey is:\\n\\n```text\\nCAPTURE\\n```\\n\\nThe second:\\n\\n```text\\nBUBBLE\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 22. Event Delegation\\n\\nThis is a **very important frontend interview concept**.\\n\\nSuppose you have:\\n\\n```html\\n\u003cul id=\\\"list\\\"\u003e\\n    \u003cli\u003eApple\u003c/li\u003e\\n    \u003cli\u003eBanana\u003c/li\u003e\\n    \u003cli\u003eMango\u003c/li\u003e\\n    \u003cli\u003eOrange\u003c/li\u003e\\n\u003c/ul\u003e\\n```\\n\\nYou could attach four listeners:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst items = document.querySelectorAll(\\\"li\\\");\\n\\nitems.forEach(item =\u003e {\\n    item.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", function() {\\n        console.log(this.textContent);\\n    });\\n});\\n```\\n\\nBut there is another technique.\\n\\nAttach **one listener to the parent**:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst list = document.getElementById(\\\"list\\\");\\n\\nlist.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", function(event) {\\n\\n    console.log(event.target.textContent);\\n\\n});\\n```\\n\\nNow clicking:\\n\\n```text\\nApple\\n```\\n\\ngives:\\n\\n```text\\nApple\\n```\\n\\nClicking:\\n\\n```text\\nMango\\n```\\n\\ngives:\\n\\n```text\\nMango\\n```\\n\\nThis works because of **event bubbling**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 23. Why Event Delegation?\\n\\nImagine:\\n\\n```text\\n10,000 buttons\\n```\\n\\nInstead of:\\n\\n```text\\n10,000 listeners\\n```\\n\\nyou can potentially use:\\n\\n```text\\n1 parent listener\\n```\\n\\nMental model:\\n\\n```text\\nChild click\\n    ↓\\nEvent bubbles\\n    ↓\\nParent listener\\n    ↓\\nevent.target tells us which child was clicked\\n```\\n\\nThis is called:\\n\\n\u003e **Event Delegation**\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 24. `closest()` + Event Delegation\\n\\nSuppose:\\n\\n```html\\n\u003cdiv id=\\\"products\\\"\u003e\\n\\n    \u003cbutton class=\\\"product\\\"\u003e\\n        \u003cspan\u003ePhone\u003c/span\u003e\\n    \u003c/button\u003e\\n\\n    \u003cbutton class=\\\"product\\\"\u003e\\n        \u003cspan\u003eLaptop\u003c/span\u003e\\n    \u003c/button\u003e\\n\\n\u003c/div\u003e\\n```\\n\\nIf the user clicks the `\u003cspan\u003e`, then:\\n\\n```javascript\\nevent.target\\n```\\n\\nis the `span`, not the button.\\n\\nWe can find the nearest button:\\n\\n```javascript\\nproducts.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", function(event) {\\n\\n    const button = event.target.closest(\\\".product\\\");\\n\\n    if (!button) {\\n        return;\\n    }\\n\\n    console.log(button.textContent);\\n\\n});\\n```\\n\\nThis pattern is very useful.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 25. `this` Inside Event Listeners\\n\\nThis connects directly to our earlier **`this` phase**.\\n\\nConsider:\\n\\n```javascript\\nbutton.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", function() {\\n    console.log(this);\\n});\\n```\\n\\nFor a normal function, `this` refers to the element on which the listener was registered.\\n\\nSo roughly:\\n\\n```javascript\\nthis === button\\n```\\n\\nBut with an arrow function:\\n\\n```javascript\\nbutton.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", () =\u003e {\\n    console.log(this);\\n});\\n```\\n\\nArrow functions **do not create their own `this`**.\\n\\nThey inherit `this` lexically.\\n\\nSo if you need the event element reliably, prefer:\\n\\n```javascript\\nevent.currentTarget\\n```\\n\\ninstead of relying on `this`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 26. `event.currentTarget` vs `this`\\n\\nWith a normal listener:\\n\\n```javascript\\nbutton.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", function(event) {\\n    console.log(this === event.currentTarget);\\n});\\n```\\n\\nUsually:\\n\\n```text\\ntrue\\n```\\n\\nBut:\\n\\n```javascript\\nbutton.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", (event) =\u003e {\\n    console.log(this);\\n});\\n```\\n\\ndoesn't give the same behavior.\\n\\nTherefore:\\n\\n```javascript\\nevent.currentTarget\\n```\\n\\nis often clearer.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 27. Removing Event Listeners\\n\\nYou can remove a listener using:\\n\\n```javascript\\nremoveEventListener()\\n```\\n\\nBut there's an important rule.\\n\\nThis:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction handleClick() {\\n    console.log(\\\"Clicked\\\");\\n}\\n\\nbutton.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", handleClick);\\n\\nbutton.removeEventListener(\\\"click\\\", handleClick);\\n```\\n\\nworks.\\n\\nBut this doesn't:\\n\\n```javascript\\nbutton.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", function() {\\n    console.log(\\\"Clicked\\\");\\n});\\n\\nbutton.removeEventListener(\\\"click\\\", function() {\\n    console.log(\\\"Clicked\\\");\\n});\\n```\\n\\nWhy?\\n\\nBecause these are **two different function objects**.\\n\\nJavaScript compares function references.\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction a() {}\\nfunction b() {}\\n\\nconsole.log(a === b);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nfalse\\n```\\n\\nEven if their code is identical.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 28. `once`\\n\\nSometimes you want an event listener to execute only once.\\n\\n```javascript\\nbutton.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", function() {\\n    console.log(\\\"Clicked once\\\");\\n}, {\\n    once: true\\n});\\n```\\n\\nFirst click:\\n\\n```text\\nClicked once\\n```\\n\\nSecond click:\\n\\n```text\\nnothing\\n```\\n\\nThe browser automatically removes the listener after its first invocation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 29. `passive`\\n\\nYou may encounter:\\n\\n```javascript\\nelement.addEventListener(\\\"touchmove\\\", handler, {\\n    passive: true\\n});\\n```\\n\\n`passive: true` tells the browser:\\n\\n\u003e \\\"This listener will not call `preventDefault()`.\\\"\\n\\nThis can help browsers optimize certain scrolling/touch interactions.\\n\\nYou don't need to use it everywhere, but you should understand it for interviews.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 30. Form Handling — Important\\n\\nConsider:\\n\\n```html\\n\u003cform id=\\\"loginForm\\\"\u003e\\n\\n    \u003cinput id=\\\"username\\\"\u003e\\n    \u003cinput id=\\\"password\\\"\u003e\\n\\n    \u003cbutton type=\\\"submit\\\"\u003e\\n        Login\\n    \u003c/button\u003e\\n\\n\u003c/form\u003e\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst form = document.getElementById(\\\"loginForm\\\");\\n\\nform.addEventListener(\\\"submit\\\", function(event) {\\n\\n    event.preventDefault();\\n\\n    const username =\\n        document.getElementById(\\\"username\\\").value;\\n\\n    const password =\\n        document.getElementById(\\\"password\\\").value;\\n\\n    console.log(username);\\n    console.log(password);\\n\\n});\\n```\\n\\nThis is the foundation for eventually doing:\\n\\n```text\\nHTML form\\n   ↓\\nJavaScript\\n   ↓\\nfetch()\\n   ↓\\nSpring Boot REST API\\n   ↓\\nDatabase\\n```\\n\\nThat's why this phase matters for your Java/Spring Boot stack.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 31. DOMContentLoaded\\n\\nSometimes JavaScript executes before the HTML elements exist.\\n\\nYou can wait until the DOM has been parsed:\\n\\n```javascript\\ndocument.addEventListener(\\\"DOMContentLoaded\\\", function() {\\n\\n    const button = document.getElementById(\\\"btn\\\");\\n\\n    console.log(button);\\n\\n});\\n```\\n\\nMeaning:\\n\\n\u003e \\\"Run this after the HTML document has been parsed and the DOM is ready.\\\"\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 32. `load` vs `DOMContentLoaded`\\n\\nImportant distinction.\\n\\n### `DOMContentLoaded`\\n\\nFires when:\\n\\n\u003e HTML has been parsed and DOM is ready.\\n\\n### `load`\\n\\nWaits for the whole page's resources, such as images, to finish loading.\\n\\nConceptually:\\n\\n```text\\nHTML parsed\\n     ↓\\nDOMContentLoaded\\n     ↓\\nImages/fonts/etc. finish\\n     ↓\\nload\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 33. Complete Mini Project\\n\\n### HTML\\n\\n```html\\n\u003c!DOCTYPE html\u003e\\n\u003chtml\u003e\\n\u003chead\u003e\\n    \u003ctitle\u003eEvent Demo\u003c/title\u003e\\n\u003c/head\u003e\\n\\n\u003cbody\u003e\\n\\n    \u003cinput id=\\\"name\\\" placeholder=\\\"Enter name\\\"\u003e\\n\\n    \u003cbutton id=\\\"btn\\\"\u003e\\n        Submit\\n    \u003c/button\u003e\\n\\n    \u003cp id=\\\"output\\\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\\n\\n    \u003cscript src=\\\"script.js\\\"\u003e\u003c/script\u003e\\n\\n\u003c/body\u003e\\n\u003c/html\u003e\\n```\\n\\n### JavaScript\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst input = document.getElementById(\\\"name\\\");\\nconst button = document.getElementById(\\\"btn\\\");\\nconst output = document.getElementById(\\\"output\\\");\\n\\nbutton.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", function(event) {\\n\\n    const name = input.value;\\n\\n    output.textContent = `Hello ${name}`;\\n\\n});\\n```\\n\\nFlow:\\n\\n```text\\nUser enters name\\n       ↓\\ninput.value\\n       ↓\\nUser clicks button\\n       ↓\\nclick event\\n       ↓\\ncallback executes\\n       ↓\\nDOM updated\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 34. Java vs JavaScript Event Handling\\n\\n| Java | JavaScript |\\n|---|---|\\n| `ActionListener` | `addEventListener()` |\\n| `actionPerformed()` | callback function |\\n| `ActionEvent` | `Event` object |\\n| Swing component | DOM element |\\n| GUI event loop | Browser event loop |\\n| `button.addActionListener(...)` | `button.addEventListener(...)` |\\n\\nThe **concept transfers**, but the browser's event system has much more functionality around propagation, bubbling, capturing, delegation, and DOM events.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 35. The Most Important Mental Model\\n\\nRemember this:\\n\\n```text\\n                BROWSER\\n                   │\\n             Event occurs\\n                   │\\n                   ↓\\n            Event propagation\\n                   │\\n          ┌────────┴────────┐\\n          ↓                 ↓\\n      Capturing          Bubbling\\n          │                 │\\n          └──────→ TARGET ←─┘\\n                     │\\n                     ↓\\n               Event Listener\\n                     │\\n                     ↓\\n               Callback runs\\n```\\n\\nAnd inside the callback:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction(event) {\\n\\n    event.target\\n    event.currentTarget\\n    event.preventDefault()\\n    event.stopPropagation()\\n\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 36. Interview Questions You Should Be Able to Answer\\n\\n### Q1. What is an event?\\n\\nAn event is a browser notification representing something that happened, such as a click, keyboard input, form submission, or page load.\\n\\n### Q2. What does `addEventListener()` do?\\n\\nIt registers a callback function that executes when a specified event occurs on an element.\\n\\n### Q3. What is event bubbling?\\n\\nEvent bubbling is the propagation of an event from the target element upward through its ancestors.\\n\\n### Q4. What is event capturing?\\n\\nCapturing is the propagation phase where the event travels from the outer ancestors toward the target.\\n\\n### Q5. `target` vs `currentTarget`?\\n\\n```text\\ntarget        → element where event originated\\ncurrentTarget → element whose listener is currently executing\\n```\\n\\n### Q6. `preventDefault()` vs `stopPropagation()`?\\n\\n```text\\npreventDefault()\\n→ prevents default browser behavior\\n\\nstopPropagation()\\n→ stops event propagation\\n```\\n\\n### Q7. What is event delegation?\\n\\nAttaching a listener to a parent and using event propagation to handle events from its children.\\n\\n### Q8. Why is event delegation useful?\\n\\nIt can reduce the number of event listeners and naturally handles dynamically added child elements.\\n\\n### Q9. Why does `removeEventListener()` sometimes fail?\\n\\nBecause it requires the same event type, listener function reference, and relevant listener options.\\n\\n### Q10. Why don't arrow functions have their own `this`?\\n\\nArrow functions inherit `this` from their surrounding lexical scope.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 37. Coding Checkpoint\\n\\nTry writing these **without looking at the solution**.\\n\\n### Problem 1\\n\\nCreate a button:\\n\\n```html\\n\u003cbutton id=\\\"btn\\\"\u003eClick\u003c/button\u003e\\n```\\n\\nWhen clicked, print:\\n\\n```text\\nButton clicked\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Problem 2\\n\\nCreate an input.\\n\\nWhenever the user types, display the current text inside a `\u003cp\u003e`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Problem 3\\n\\nCreate:\\n\\n```text\\nParent\\n └── Button\\n```\\n\\nAdd click listeners to both.\\n\\nPredict the output when the button is clicked.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Problem 4\\n\\nCreate a form and prevent the browser from refreshing the page when submitted.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Problem 5 — Important\\n\\nCreate:\\n\\n```html\\n\u003cul\u003e\\n    \u003cli\u003eJava\u003c/li\u003e\\n    \u003cli\u003eJavaScript\u003c/li\u003e\\n    \u003cli\u003ePython\u003c/li\u003e\\n    \u003cli\u003eC++\u003c/li\u003e\\n\u003c/ul\u003e\\n```\\n\\nUse **one event listener only** on `\u003cul\u003e`.\\n\\nWhen any `\u003cli\u003e` is clicked, print its text.\\n\\nThis tests:\\n\\n```text\\nevent bubbling\\n+\\nevent.target\\n+\\nevent delegation\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Phase 13 Complete ✅\\n\\nYour JavaScript knowledge now looks like:\\n\\n```text\\nPhase 0   Java → JS mental model\\nPhase 1   Variables \u0026 Data Types\\nPhase 1B  Operators \u0026 Coercion\\nPhase 2   Conditions \u0026 Loops\\nPhase 3   Arrays\\nPhase 4   Objects\\nPhase 5   Functions\\nPhase 6   OOP \u0026 Prototypes\\nPhase 7   Execution Model\\nPhase 8   Advanced Functions\\nPhase 9   Async JavaScript\\nPhase 10  Collections \u0026 Built-ins\\nPhase 11  Modules \u0026 Modern JS\\nPhase 12  DOM\\nPhase 13  Events \u0026 Event Handling ✅\\n```\\n\\n### Next: Phase 14 — HTTP, REST \u0026 API Consumption\\n\\nThis is particularly important for your **Java + Spring Boot backend**, because we'll connect:\\n\\n```text\\nJavaScript\\n   ↓\\nfetch()\\n   ↓\\nHTTP Request\\n   ↓\\nSpring Boot REST API\\n   ↓\\nJSON\\n   ↓\\nJavaScript\\n   ↓\\nDOM\\n```\\n\\nWe'll cover `fetch()`, HTTP methods, headers, status codes, JSON, GET/POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE, request bodies, CORS, authentication basics, and how JS communicates with your Spring Boot backend.\\n\\n---\\n\\nIf you want, I can:\\n\\n- Explain event delegation with an extended example\\n- Demonstrate event propagation phases with code\\n- Show how to remove an event listener correctly\",\"role\",\"assistant\",{},{\"_197\":201,\"_204\":662,\"_54\":663,\"_208\":664,\"_210\":211,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":665,\"_217\":218},\"900d8b1d-9cd9-4248-80e9-1d1a4151469c\",[81],{\"_656\":657,\"_215\":670},1790059451.2943056,{\"_651\":667,\"_668\":669},{\"_233\":234,\"_240\":241,\"_242\":241,\"_245\":660,\"_248\":10,\"_666\":34},\"is_redacted\",\"model_editable_context\",\"model_set_context\",\"\",{},{\"_197\":660,\"_204\":674,\"_54\":675,\"_208\":676,\"_210\":211,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":677,\"_217\":218},\"8fc4fffc-4451-4f6e-9496-ad92a4471891\",[201],{\"_656\":681,\"_215\":682},1790059450.8002672,{\"_651\":652,\"_653\":680},{\"_678\":34,\"_246\":679,\"_228\":67,\"_233\":234,\"_236\":237,\"_238\":239,\"_240\":241,\"_242\":241,\"_243\":67,\"_66\":67,\"_245\":672,\"_248\":10,\"_244\":14},\"is_visually_hidden_from_conversation\",\"316360b7-ae6f-4002-8bbc-057984b2e1dd\",[669],\"system\",{},{\"_197\":672,\"_204\":686,\"_54\":687,\"_208\":688,\"_210\":211,\"_212\":34,\"_213\":689,\"_215\":690,\"_217\":218},\"be71062d-40e0-4dda-b72a-feb7c1e62320\",[660],{\"_656\":681,\"_215\":693},1790059452.210627,{\"_651\":652,\"_653\":692},0,{\"_691\":34,\"_678\":34,\"_246\":679,\"_228\":67,\"_233\":234,\"_236\":237,\"_238\":239,\"_240\":241,\"_242\":241,\"_243\":67,\"_66\":67,\"_245\":684,\"_248\":10,\"_244\":14},\"rebase_system_message\",[669],{},{\"_197\":684,\"_204\":697,\"_54\":698,\"_56\":699,\"_208\":700,\"_210\":211,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":701,\"_217\":218},\"d1461491-2af2-4b4f-9a61-c6b8cf9f68d0\",[672],{\"_656\":709,\"_215\":710},1790059450.431,1790059497.692627,{\"_651\":652,\"_653\":707},{\"_702\":703,\"_236\":237,\"_704\":-5,\"_240\":241,\"_242\":241,\"_246\":679,\"_248\":10,\"_244\":14},\"serialization_metadata\",{\"_705\":706},\"message_source\",\"custom_symbol_offsets\",[],[708],\"next phase\",\"user\",{},{\"_197\":695,\"_204\":714,\"_54\":715,\"_56\":716,\"_208\":717,\"_210\":211,\"_212\":34,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":718,\"_217\":218,\"_219\":220},\"59d77c84-bf11-4a63-9a16-467866d0f4c6\",[684],{\"_656\":657,\"_215\":738},1790059067.810029,1790059302.905003,{\"_651\":652,\"_653\":736},{\"_221\":719,\"_223\":34,\"_224\":720,\"_226\":721,\"_228\":67,\"_229\":722,\"_231\":723,\"_233\":234,\"_235\":34,\"_236\":724,\"_238\":239,\"_240\":725,\"_242\":725,\"_243\":67,\"_66\":67,\"_244\":34,\"_245\":712,\"_246\":726,\"_248\":10},{\"_50\":647,\"_648\":735},[],[],{\"_286\":727,\"_288\":728,\"_290\":729,\"_292\":730},[],\"5498b663-1367-4005-9ef5-0ca4c162c4ed\",\"dc49892d-442e-4ff2-bd97-174dec7343a3\",\"730c5520-bc84-4327-a727-e2b2e35e631a\",{\"_197\":734,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":733,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":732,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":731,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},\"zcarks\",\"2941k0\",\"tv65o2\",\"lvckv9\",[650],[737],\"Absolutely. Since your **backend is Java/Spring Boot**, learning Node.js/Express.js would add unnecessary backend duplication. We'll remove those and make this a **Java-backend + JavaScript/React frontend + interview-focused** roadmap.\\n\\n## 🔄 Revised JavaScript Roadmap\\n\\n### ✅ Completed\\n\\n```text\\nPhase 0   Java → JavaScript Mental Model\\nPhase 1   Variables \u0026 Data Types\\nPhase 1B  Operators, Truthy/Falsy \u0026 Coercion\\nPhase 2   Conditions \u0026 Loops\\nPhase 3   Arrays\\nPhase 4   Objects\\nPhase 5   Functions\\nPhase 6   this + OOP + Prototypes\\nPhase 7   Execution Model\\n          ├── Execution Context\\n          ├── Call Stack\\n          ├── Scope Chain\\n          ├── Hoisting\\n          ├── TDZ\\n          ├── Closures\\n          ├── Event Loop\\n          ├── Microtasks\\n          └── Tasks\\n\\nPhase 8   Advanced Functions\\n          ├── Higher-order functions\\n          ├── Currying\\n          ├── Partial application\\n          ├── Composition\\n          ├── Pure functions\\n          ├── Immutability\\n          ├── Memoization\\n          ├── Iterators\\n          └── Generators\\n\\nPhase 9   Async JavaScript\\n          ├── Promises\\n          ├── Promise chaining\\n          ├── async/await\\n          ├── Promise.all\\n          ├── Promise.allSettled\\n          ├── Promise.race\\n          ├── Promise.any\\n          ├── fetch\\n          ├── AbortController\\n          └── concurrency\\n\\nPhase 10  Collections \u0026 Built-ins\\n          ├── Map\\n          ├── Set\\n          ├── WeakMap\\n          ├── WeakSet\\n          ├── String\\n          ├── Number\\n          ├── BigInt\\n          ├── Math\\n          ├── Date/Temporal\\n          └── RegExp\\n\\nPhase 11  Modules \u0026 Modern JavaScript\\n          ├── import/export\\n          ├── named/default exports\\n          ├── module scope\\n          ├── CommonJS\\n          ├── npm basics\\n          ├── package.json\\n          ├── dynamic imports\\n          ├── top-level await\\n          └── tree shaking\\n\\nPhase 12  Browser \u0026 DOM\\n          ├── window\\n          ├── document\\n          ├── DOM tree\\n          ├── selectors\\n          ├── textContent\\n          ├── innerHTML\\n          ├── classList\\n          ├── attributes\\n          ├── createElement\\n          ├── append/prepend\\n          └── DOM traversal\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🔥 Revised Upcoming Roadmap\\n\\nWe're going to focus much more heavily on **frontend JavaScript, React, APIs, and interviews**.\\n\\n```text\\n➡️ Phase 13  Events \u0026 Event Handling\\n             ├── addEventListener()\\n             ├── Event object\\n             ├── click\\n             ├── input\\n             ├── change\\n             ├── submit\\n             ├── keyboard events\\n             ├── mouse events\\n             ├── event.target\\n             ├── event.currentTarget\\n             ├── preventDefault()\\n             ├── stopPropagation()\\n             ├── bubbling\\n             ├── capturing\\n             └── event delegation\\n\\n➡️ Phase 14  HTTP, REST \u0026 API Consumption\\n             ├── HTTP fundamentals\\n             ├── request/response\\n             ├── HTTP methods\\n             ├── status codes\\n             ├── headers\\n             ├── JSON\\n             ├── fetch()\\n             ├── GET\\n             ├── POST\\n             ├── PUT\\n             ├── PATCH\\n             ├── DELETE\\n             ├── query parameters\\n             ├── path parameters\\n             ├── request body\\n             ├── authentication basics\\n             └── Java Spring Boot ↔ JavaScript communication\\n\\n➡️ Phase 15  Browser Storage \u0026 Web APIs\\n             ├── localStorage\\n             ├── sessionStorage\\n             ├── cookies\\n             ├── storage events\\n             ├── URL\\n             ├── URLSearchParams\\n             ├── FormData\\n             ├── Clipboard API\\n             └── useful browser APIs\\n\\n➡️ Phase 16  Advanced JavaScript Patterns\\n             ├── Factory pattern\\n             ├── Module pattern\\n             ├── Observer pattern\\n             ├── Pub/Sub\\n             ├── Debouncing\\n             ├── Throttling\\n             ├── Retry patterns\\n             ├── caching\\n             └── practical JS architecture\\n\\n➡️ Phase 17  JavaScript Performance \u0026 Memory\\n             ├── memory lifecycle\\n             ├── garbage collection\\n             ├── memory leaks\\n             ├── closures \u0026 memory\\n             ├── event listener leaks\\n             ├── DOM performance\\n             ├── reflow\\n             ├── repaint\\n             ├── debouncing/throttling\\n             ├── lazy loading\\n             └── performance optimization\\n\\n➡️ Phase 18  JavaScript Security\\n             ├── XSS\\n             ├── CSRF concepts\\n             ├── CORS\\n             ├── same-origin policy\\n             ├── DOM injection\\n             ├── unsafe innerHTML\\n             ├── token handling\\n             └── frontend security practices\\n\\n🔥 Phase 19  React JavaScript Foundations\\n             ├── why React\\n             ├── SPA\\n             ├── components\\n             ├── JSX\\n             ├── props\\n             ├── state\\n             ├── events\\n             ├── conditional rendering\\n             ├── lists\\n             └── component thinking\\n\\n🔥 Phase 20  React Core\\n             ├── useState\\n             ├── useEffect\\n             ├── useRef\\n             ├── useMemo\\n             ├── useCallback\\n             ├── useContext\\n             ├── custom hooks\\n             ├── component lifecycle\\n             └── state management\\n\\n🔥 Phase 21  React + Java Spring Boot\\n             ├── React ↔ Spring Boot\\n             ├── REST API calls\\n             ├── DTOs\\n             ├── JSON\\n             ├── CORS\\n             ├── authentication\\n             ├── JWT concepts\\n             ├── protected routes\\n             ├── login/signup flow\\n             ├── error handling\\n             └── full-stack architecture\\n\\n🔥 Phase 22  Advanced React\\n             ├── React Router\\n             ├── Redux Toolkit\\n             ├── React-Redux\\n             ├── async Redux\\n             ├── API state\\n             ├── performance\\n             ├── code splitting\\n             ├── lazy components\\n             └── project architecture\\n\\n🔥 Phase 23  JavaScript Interview Mastery\\n             ├── output questions\\n             ├── hoisting\\n             ├── closures\\n             ├── this\\n             ├── prototypes\\n             ├── promises\\n             ├── event loop\\n             ├── async/await\\n             ├── coercion\\n             ├── Map/Set\\n             ├── DOM\\n             ├── events\\n             ├── ES6+\\n             └── tricky interview questions\\n\\n🔥 Phase 24  JavaScript DSA\\n             ├── Arrays\\n             ├── Strings\\n             ├── HashMap\\n             ├── Set\\n             ├── Two Pointer\\n             ├── Sliding Window\\n             ├── Prefix Sum\\n             ├── Stack\\n             ├── Queue\\n             ├── Linked List\\n             ├── Binary Search\\n             ├── Trees\\n             ├── Graphs\\n             ├── Heap\\n             ├── Recursion\\n             ├── Backtracking\\n             └── DP\\n```\\n\\n## 🎯 Your final stack\\n\\nThis roadmap now targets the stack you're actually building:\\n\\n```text\\n                    FULL STACK\\n                       │\\n          ┌────────────┴────────────┐\\n          │                         │\\n       FRONTEND                  BACKEND\\n          │                         │\\n     JavaScript                  Java\\n          │                         │\\n       React                    Spring Boot\\n          │                         │\\n       Vite                    REST APIs\\n          │                         │\\n       Redux                  JPA/Hibernate\\n          │                         │\\n          └────────────┬────────────┘\\n                       │\\n                    Database\\n                       │\\n                  PostgreSQL\\n```\\n\\nSo **Node.js and Express.js are removed** from the curriculum.\\n\\nAnd importantly, I won't spend the upcoming phases teaching random browser APIs just for completeness. The focus will be:\\n\\n**JavaScript mastery → frontend/browser → API integration → React → React + Spring Boot → interview mastery → JS DSA.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🚀 Phase 13 — Events \u0026 Event Handling\\n\\nNext we'll learn:\\n\\n```text\\nEvent\\n ↓\\naddEventListener()\\n ↓\\nEvent Handler\\n ↓\\nEvent Object\\n ↓\\ntarget/currentTarget\\n ↓\\nBubbling\\n ↓\\nCapturing\\n ↓\\npreventDefault()\\n ↓\\nstopPropagation()\\n ↓\\nEvent Delegation\\n```\\n\\nThis is the natural continuation from the DOM phase, and it's **very important for React later**, because React's event system builds on these browser concepts.\\n\\n**We'll start Phase 13 from the absolute basics and keep using Java comparisons where they actually help.**\",{},{\"_197\":712,\"_204\":742,\"_54\":743,\"_208\":744,\"_210\":211,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":745,\"_217\":218},\"3e239cab-41f9-48df-a712-9d712274a3b8\",[695],{\"_656\":657,\"_215\":746},1790059067.8358712,{\"_651\":667,\"_668\":669},{\"_233\":234,\"_240\":725,\"_242\":725,\"_245\":740,\"_248\":10,\"_666\":34},{},{\"_197\":740,\"_204\":750,\"_54\":751,\"_208\":752,\"_210\":211,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":753,\"_217\":218},\"9563496b-0149-44e4-906e-749afca5d9