"152\",{\"_197\":1628,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},\"153\",{\"_197\":1627,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},\"bxfcmz\",\"32nmq8\",\"fz58qn\",\"83tv83\",\"jtp90s\",\"o2ffho\",\"xzhj3d\",\"j6tkl2\",\"l7iwps\",\"hva6qj\",\"nn3k5u\",\"ffuvs7\",\"5q0vza\",\"wdkhk1\",\"i6vtl5\",\"zxcu6i\",\"9xctjs\",\"7nri51\",\"bwplp8\",\"9scmio\",\"t52709\",\"ivebs5\",\"wuvlgo\",\"w9x1hg\",\"m4qkp1\",\"mi9g3t\",\"4z8iup\",\"cgkd6f\",\"cz4rdo\",\"dh7ojz\",\"42mply\",\"db0zjn\",\"ht15lx\",\"0wlmqe\",\"s7k097\",\"f86yrr\",\"4it8gn\",\"20uy10\",\"3sh4j1\",\"2r1zqt\",\"nu0d17\",\"py2zxb\",\"6rl1hi\",\"jzi3qd\",\"q6t9ad\",\"y17ppm\",\"9o5aav\",\"5p1e8y\",\"1yim02\",\"epxiiz\",\"nasucl\",\"q7wk69\",\"ndnv8k\",\"8gcxpx\",\"ny09b2\",\"jpizpw\",\"520wzh\",\"770ddq\",\"ie25ar\",\"c6jb31\",\"llkcbf\",\"b012e5\",\"6hvkdt\",\"7j028g\",\"lupd23\",\"6tjvur\",\"vi5ufd\",\"xew6ow\",\"75prum\",\"zxpvqk\",\"9fzvic\",\"0wpyy1\",\"samnt2\",\"arcx4k\",\"yqzfzo\",\"6levzh\",\"b7aq3s\",\"a4mu19\",\"gdgi0a\",\"m8koj4\",\"hs91zk\",\"pa9q4c\",\"2jfgpm\",\"3cakqq\",\"6s4kk8\",\"b3i0sb\",\"9lqski\",\"5sueka\",\"xyym17\",\"2f912o\",\"ex0w1l\",\"94e5a7\",\"a19gmx\",\"qzb71s\",\"w1qqwc\",\"zts2ss\",\"thcbb2\",\"orckwg\",\"xdb5f4\",\"wij0an\",\"8bdqs1\",\"7ric31\",\"xd2xdk\",\"8tufsh\",\"1b2tgm\",\"q4se13\",\"sxxuhv\",\"com5jd\",\"ax2bll\",\"xc6oui\",\"5yyg5e\",\"dqk102\",\"hbajmt\",\"43qctu\",\"uwa3yb\",\"7cj1ip\",\"f0w35k\",\"l2n3t6\",\"u0dauz\",\"sdt70k\",\"shbnhz\",\"4mh84j\",\"jbhcya\",\"uy189q\",\"st4u5a\",\"l5xh3v\",\"5pz7mi\",\"rzc309\",\"w78zj3\",\"qwl2ig\",\"m7p6ox\",\"42vme8\",\"onyc7v\",\"o3206k\",\"azo0nb\",\"ezzpgm\",\"9r8q81\",\"52ww7o\",\"a8l46z\",\"p932to\",\"h6r4hr\",\"no7mml\",\"hvmo2q\",\"onudlw\",\"36yqu6\",\"2dahul\",\"2kash1\",\"7ocw6c\",\"v07o5b\",\"ed827a\",\"tlhzzl\",\"tgdswx\",\"a09gjr\",\"owdy9y\",[650],[1783],\"# 🚀 Phase 10 — JavaScript Collections \u0026 Built-in Objects\\n\\nNow we're moving into **JavaScript's collection framework**.\\n\\nSince you already know Java, this phase will be much easier if we constantly map:\\n\\n```text\\nJava                         JavaScript\\n------------------------------------------------\\nArrayList                    Array\\nHashMap                      Map\\nHashSet                      Set\\nWeakHashMap                  WeakMap\\nWeak references              WeakMap / WeakSet\\nString                       String\\nMath                         Math\\nInteger / Double             Number\\nDate / time APIs             Date + modern Temporal\\nRegex                        RegExp\\n```\\n\\n⚠️ **Important:** These are conceptual comparisons, not exact equivalents.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.1 `Map` — Java's `HashMap` Equivalent\\n\\nIn Java:\\n\\n```java\\nMap\u003cString, Integer\u003e marks = new HashMap\u003c\u003e();\\n\\nmarks.put(\\\"Java\\\", 90);\\nmarks.put(\\\"SQL\\\", 85);\\n\\nSystem.out.println(marks.get(\\\"Java\\\"));\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst marks = new Map();\\n\\nmarks.set(\\\"Java\\\", 90);\\nmarks.set(\\\"SQL\\\", 85);\\n\\nconsole.log(marks.get(\\\"Java\\\"));\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n90\\n```\\n\\nThe basic mapping is:\\n\\n```text\\nJava HashMap.put()\\n        ↓\\nJS Map.set()\\n\\nJava HashMap.get()\\n        ↓\\nJS Map.get()\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.2 Why Use `Map` Instead of an Object?\\n\\nYou might ask:\\n\\n\u003e \\\"Why not just use an object?\\\"\\n\\nYou can.\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst marks = {\\n    Java: 90,\\n    SQL: 85\\n};\\n```\\n\\nBut `Map` is specifically designed as a **key-value collection**.\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst marks = new Map();\\n\\nmarks.set(\\\"Java\\\", 90);\\nmarks.set(\\\"SQL\\\", 85);\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.3 `Map` Can Have Almost Any Key Type\\n\\nThis is a major difference.\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst map = new Map();\\n\\nmap.set(\\\"name\\\", \\\"Dev\\\");\\nmap.set(10, \\\"Ten\\\");\\nmap.set(true, \\\"Boolean\\\");\\nmap.set({ id: 1 }, \\\"Object\\\");\\n```\\n\\nKeys aren't restricted to strings or symbols.\\n\\nYou can even use an object as a key.\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst user = { id: 1 };\\n\\nconst map = new Map();\\n\\nmap.set(user, \\\"Dev\\\");\\n```\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconsole.log(map.get(user));\\n```\\n\\ngives:\\n\\n```text\\nDev\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.4 Object Keys vs Map Keys\\n\\nWith an object:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst obj = {};\\n\\nobj[10] = \\\"Hello\\\";\\n```\\n\\nThe property key is effectively converted to a property-key type.\\n\\nWith `Map`:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst map = new Map();\\n\\nmap.set(10, \\\"Hello\\\");\\n```\\n\\n`10` remains a number key.\\n\\nThis makes `Map` much better when you genuinely need arbitrary key types.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.5 Basic `Map` Methods\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst map = new Map();\\n\\nmap.set(\\\"a\\\", 1);\\nmap.set(\\\"b\\\", 2);\\n```\\n\\n### `get()`\\n\\n```javascript\\nmap.get(\\\"a\\\");\\n```\\n\\n### `has()`\\n\\n```javascript\\nmap.has(\\\"a\\\");\\n```\\n\\n### `delete()`\\n\\n```javascript\\nmap.delete(\\\"a\\\");\\n```\\n\\n### `size`\\n\\n```javascript\\nconsole.log(map.size);\\n```\\n\\n### `clear()`\\n\\n```javascript\\nmap.clear();\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.6 Iterating a Map\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst map = new Map([\\n    [\\\"Java\\\", 90],\\n    [\\\"SQL\\\", 85],\\n    [\\\"JS\\\", 95]\\n]);\\n```\\n\\nYou can use:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfor (const [key, value] of map) {\\n    console.log(key, value);\\n}\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nJava 90\\nSQL 85\\nJS 95\\n```\\n\\nYou can also use:\\n\\n```javascript\\nmap.keys();\\nmap.values();\\nmap.entries();\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.7 Map Preserves Insertion Order\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst map = new Map();\\n\\nmap.set(\\\"C\\\", 3);\\nmap.set(\\\"A\\\", 1);\\nmap.set(\\\"B\\\", 2);\\n```\\n\\nIteration follows insertion order:\\n\\n```text\\nC\\nA\\nB\\n```\\n\\nThis is useful to know in interviews.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.8 Java `HashMap` vs JS `Map`\\n\\nDon't say:\\n\\n\u003e \\\"JavaScript Map is exactly HashMap.\\\"\\n\\nBetter:\\n\\n\u003e JavaScript `Map` is a key-value collection with semantics somewhat analogous to Java's `HashMap`, but the APIs, key semantics, ordering guarantees, and implementation details differ.\\n\\nFor interview purposes:\\n\\n```text\\nHashMap → Map\\n```\\n\\nis a useful mental mapping.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.9 `Set` — Java's `HashSet` Equivalent\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\nSet\u003cInteger\u003e set = new HashSet\u003c\u003e();\\n\\nset.add(10);\\nset.add(20);\\nset.add(10);\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst set = new Set();\\n\\nset.add(10);\\nset.add(20);\\nset.add(10);\\n```\\n\\nNow:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconsole.log(set);\\n```\\n\\ncontains:\\n\\n```text\\n10\\n20\\n```\\n\\nDuplicates are automatically ignored.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.10 Basic Set Methods\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst set = new Set([10, 20, 30]);\\n```\\n\\n### Add\\n\\n```javascript\\nset.add(40);\\n```\\n\\n### Check\\n\\n```javascript\\nset.has(20);\\n```\\n\\n### Delete\\n\\n```javascript\\nset.delete(20);\\n```\\n\\n### Size\\n\\n```javascript\\nset.size;\\n```\\n\\n### Clear\\n\\n```javascript\\nset.clear();\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.11 Set Is Excellent for Removing Duplicates\\n\\nVery common coding-interview problem.\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4];\\n\\nconst unique = [...new Set(numbers)];\\n\\nconsole.log(unique);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n[1, 2, 3, 4]\\n```\\n\\nBreak it down:\\n\\n```text\\nnumbers\\n   ↓\\nnew Set(numbers)\\n   ↓\\n{1,2,3,4}\\n   ↓\\nspread\\n   ↓\\n[1,2,3,4]\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.12 Set and Object Equality\\n\\nThis is important.\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst set = new Set();\\n\\nconst a = { x: 1 };\\nconst b = { x: 1 };\\n\\nset.add(a);\\nset.add(b);\\n\\nconsole.log(set.size);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n2\\n```\\n\\nWhy?\\n\\nBecause:\\n\\n```javascript\\na === b\\n```\\n\\nis:\\n\\n```text\\nfalse\\n```\\n\\nThey are different object references.\\n\\nBut:\\n\\n```javascript\\nset.add(a);\\nset.add(a);\\n```\\n\\nonly stores one reference.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.13 `Map` + `Set` Coding Pattern\\n\\nSuppose:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 2, 3, 1, 4];\\n```\\n\\nFind duplicates.\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst seen = new Set();\\nconst duplicates = new Set();\\n\\nfor (const n of numbers) {\\n    if (seen.has(n)) {\\n        duplicates.add(n);\\n    } else {\\n        seen.add(n);\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log([...duplicates]);\\n```\\n\\nResult:\\n\\n```text\\n[2, 1]\\n```\\n\\nThis pattern is extremely useful for DSA.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.14 Frequency Counter Using `Map`\\n\\nSuppose:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst nums = [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3];\\n```\\n\\nWe want:\\n\\n```text\\n1 → 1\\n2 → 2\\n3 → 3\\n```\\n\\nUse:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst freq = new Map();\\n\\nfor (const n of nums) {\\n    freq.set(n, (freq.get(n) || 0) + 1);\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNow:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconsole.log(freq);\\n```\\n\\nConceptually:\\n\\n```text\\n1 → 1\\n2 → 2\\n3 → 3\\n```\\n\\n### Java comparison\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\nMap\u003cInteger, Integer\u003e freq = new HashMap\u003c\u003e();\\n\\nfor (int n : nums) {\\n    freq.put(n, freq.getOrDefault(n, 0) + 1);\\n}\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfreq.set(n, (freq.get(n) || 0) + 1);\\n```\\n\\nThese are very similar patterns.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.15 A Better Modern Pattern\\n\\nYou can also write:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfreq.set(n, (freq.get(n) ?? 0) + 1);\\n```\\n\\n`??` is often safer when you specifically want to default only for `null`/`undefined`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.16 WeakMap\\n\\nNow we're entering advanced territory.\\n\\nA `WeakMap` stores key-value pairs where the keys must be **objects or non-registered symbols**, and the keys are held weakly.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst weakMap = new WeakMap();\\n\\nconst user = {};\\n\\nweakMap.set(user, \\\"Private data\\\");\\n\\nconsole.log(weakMap.get(user));\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nPrivate data\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.17 Why \\\"Weak\\\"?\\n\\nSuppose:\\n\\n```javascript\\nlet user = {\\n    name: \\\"Dev\\\"\\n};\\n\\nconst weakMap = new WeakMap();\\n\\nweakMap.set(user, \\\"Some data\\\");\\n```\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```javascript\\nuser = null;\\n```\\n\\nThe `WeakMap` does not itself keep the object alive merely because it has that key.\\n\\nIf the object becomes otherwise unreachable, it can be garbage-collected.\\n\\nThis is useful for associating metadata with objects without unnecessarily keeping those objects alive.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.18 `Map` vs `WeakMap`\\n\\n| Feature | Map | WeakMap |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Keys | Any value | Objects + non-registered symbols |\\n| Strong key reference | Yes | No |\\n| Iterable | Yes | No |\\n| `.size` | Yes | No |\\n| `.keys()` | Yes | No |\\n| Garbage-collection friendly | Normal | Weak keys |\\n\\nDon't try to memorize every detail blindly.\\n\\nThe key idea:\\n\\n```text\\nMap\\n→ normal collection\\n\\nWeakMap\\n→ object-associated data without strongly keeping keys alive\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.19 Common WeakMap Use Case\\n\\nImagine you want metadata associated with DOM objects.\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst metadata = new WeakMap();\\n\\nconst element = document.querySelector(\\\"#button\\\");\\n\\nmetadata.set(element, {\\n    clicked: true\\n});\\n```\\n\\nIf that DOM object becomes unreachable elsewhere, the WeakMap's weak key does not prevent garbage collection.\\n\\nThis is one reason libraries can use WeakMaps for object-associated metadata.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.20 WeakSet\\n\\n`WeakSet` is similar to `Set`, but stores objects weakly.\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst visited = new WeakSet();\\n\\nconst user = {};\\n\\nvisited.add(user);\\n\\nconsole.log(visited.has(user));\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\ntrue\\n```\\n\\nUseful when you want to mark objects without strongly retaining them.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.21 WeakMap / WeakSet Interview Trap\\n\\nCan you do:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfor (const x of weakMap) {\\n    ...\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNo.\\n\\nWeak collections are deliberately **not iterable**.\\n\\nWhy?\\n\\nTheir contents are tied to garbage-collection behavior, so exposing normal enumeration would create problematic semantics.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.22 `String`\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\nString name = \\\"Dev\\\";\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst name = \\\"Dev\\\";\\n```\\n\\nStrings are immutable in JavaScript.\\n\\n```javascript\\nlet name = \\\"Dev\\\";\\n\\nname[0] = \\\"X\\\";\\n\\nconsole.log(name);\\n```\\n\\nThe string itself isn't modified by that assignment.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.23 Important String Methods\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst text = \\\"Hello JavaScript\\\";\\n```\\n\\n### Length\\n\\n```javascript\\ntext.length;\\n```\\n\\n### Character\\n\\n```javascript\\ntext.charAt(0);\\n```\\n\\nor:\\n\\n```javascript\\ntext[0];\\n```\\n\\n### Includes\\n\\n```javascript\\ntext.includes(\\\"Java\\\");\\n```\\n\\n### Starts/ends\\n\\n```javascript\\ntext.startsWith(\\\"Hello\\\");\\ntext.endsWith(\\\"Script\\\");\\n```\\n\\n### Search\\n\\n```javascript\\ntext.indexOf(\\\"Java\\\");\\n```\\n\\n### Extract\\n\\n```javascript\\ntext.slice(0, 5);\\n```\\n\\n### Replace\\n\\n```javascript\\ntext.replace(\\\"JavaScript\\\", \\\"Java\\\");\\n```\\n\\n### Split\\n\\n```javascript\\ntext.split(\\\" \\\");\\n```\\n\\nResult:\\n\\n```javascript\\n[\\\"Hello\\\", \\\"JavaScript\\\"]\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.24 Template Literals\\n\\nYou already learned:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst name = \\\"Dev\\\";\\nconst age = 22;\\n\\nconsole.log(`My name is ${name} and I am ${age}`);\\n```\\n\\nThis is often cleaner than:\\n\\n```javascript\\n\\\"My name is \\\" + name + \\\" and I am \\\" + age\\n```\\n\\nJava has text blocks and string formatting mechanisms, but JavaScript template literals are especially convenient for interpolation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.25 `Number`\\n\\nJava has multiple numeric primitive types:\\n\\n```java\\nint\\nlong\\nfloat\\ndouble\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript's ordinary numeric type is:\\n\\n```javascript\\nnumber\\n```\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst a = 10;\\nconst b = 10.5;\\n```\\n\\nBoth:\\n\\n```text\\nnumber\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.26 `Number.isInteger()`\\n\\n```javascript\\nNumber.isInteger(10);\\n```\\n\\n```text\\ntrue\\n```\\n\\nBut:\\n\\n```javascript\\nNumber.isInteger(10.5);\\n```\\n\\n```text\\nfalse\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.27 `Number.isNaN()`\\n\\nRemember:\\n\\n```javascript\\nNaN\\n```\\n\\nmeans **Not-a-Number**, despite having:\\n\\n```javascript\\ntypeof NaN\\n```\\n\\nequal to:\\n\\n```text\\n\\\"number\\\"\\n```\\n\\nUse:\\n\\n```javascript\\nNumber.isNaN(value)\\n```\\n\\nwhen you specifically want to test for `NaN`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.28 `Number.isFinite()`\\n\\n```javascript\\nNumber.isFinite(10);\\n```\\n\\n```text\\ntrue\\n```\\n\\nBut:\\n\\n```javascript\\nNumber.isFinite(Infinity);\\n```\\n\\n```text\\nfalse\\n```\\n\\nThis is useful when validating numeric input.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.29 Safe Integers\\n\\nJavaScript's `number` uses IEEE 754 double-precision floating-point representation.\\n\\nThis means integer precision is limited.\\n\\nThe largest reliably represented integer boundary is:\\n\\n```javascript\\nNumber.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER\\n```\\n\\nwhich is:\\n\\n```text\\n9007199254740991\\n```\\n\\nFor integers beyond that range where exact integer arithmetic matters, use:\\n\\n```javascript\\nBigInt\\n```\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst big = 900719925474099100n;\\n```\\n\\nNotice the:\\n\\n```text\\nn\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.30 BigInt Trap\\n\\nYou cannot freely mix:\\n\\n```javascript\\n10n\\n```\\n\\nwith:\\n\\n```javascript\\n10\\n```\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n```javascript\\n10n + 10\\n```\\n\\nthrows a TypeError.\\n\\nYou need compatible numeric types.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.31 `Math`\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\nMath.max(10, 20);\\nMath.sqrt(25);\\nMath.pow(2, 3);\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```javascript\\nMath.max(10, 20);\\nMath.sqrt(25);\\nMath.pow(2, 3);\\n```\\n\\nVery familiar.\\n\\nAlso:\\n\\n```javascript\\nMath.floor(4.9);   // 4\\nMath.ceil(4.1);    // 5\\nMath.round(4.5);   // 5\\nMath.abs(-10);     // 10\\nMath.random();     // [0,1)\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.32 Random Number Example\\n\\nGenerate a random integer from `1` to `10`:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst random =\\n    Math.floor(Math.random() * 10) + 1;\\n```\\n\\nWhy?\\n\\n```text\\nMath.random()\\n→ 0 \u003c= x \u003c 1\\n\\n× 10\\n→ 0 \u003c= x \u003c 10\\n\\nMath.floor()\\n→ 0 ... 9\\n\\n+ 1\\n→ 1 ... 10\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.33 `Date`\\n\\nJavaScript has the classic:\\n\\n```javascript\\nDate\\n```\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst now = new Date();\\n\\nconsole.log(now);\\n```\\n\\nGet components:\\n\\n```javascript\\nnow.getFullYear();\\nnow.getMonth();\\nnow.getDate();\\nnow.getHours();\\n```\\n\\n⚠️ Important:\\n\\n`getMonth()` is **zero-based**.\\n\\n```text\\nJanuary → 0\\nFebruary → 1\\n...\\nDecember → 11\\n```\\n\\nThis is a classic interview trap.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.34 Java Date Comparison\\n\\nModern Java has:\\n\\n```java\\nLocalDate\\nLocalDateTime\\nZonedDateTime\\nInstant\\nDuration\\n```\\n\\nThese are much richer than JavaScript's classic `Date`.\\n\\nModern JavaScript also has the **Temporal API**, which provides a more comprehensive model for dates/times where available in the runtime.\\n\\nSo don't think:\\n\\n```text\\nJava Date = JS Date\\n```\\n\\nThey are not equivalent.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.35 Regular Expressions\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\nPattern\\nMatcher\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```javascript\\nRegExp\\n```\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst pattern = /java/i;\\n\\nconsole.log(pattern.test(\\\"I love Java\\\"));\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\ntrue\\n```\\n\\n`i` means case-insensitive.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.36 Regex Example\\n\\nCheck whether a string contains digits:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst pattern = /\\\\d/;\\n\\nconsole.log(pattern.test(\\\"abc123\\\"));\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\ntrue\\n```\\n\\nSome common patterns:\\n\\n```text\\n\\\\d    digit\\n\\\\w    word character\\n\\\\s    whitespace\\n.     any character except line terminators in the usual mode\\n^     beginning\\n$     end\\n+     one or more\\n*     zero or more\\n?     zero or one\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.37 Regex `match()`\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst text = \\\"Java 123 JavaScript 456\\\";\\n\\nconst numbers = text.match(/\\\\d+/g);\\n\\nconsole.log(numbers);\\n```\\n\\nResult:\\n\\n```javascript\\n[\\\"123\\\", \\\"456\\\"]\\n```\\n\\nThe `g` flag means global matching.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.38 Object Utility APIs\\n\\nSince objects are everywhere in JavaScript, these are extremely important.\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst user = {\\n    name: \\\"Dev\\\",\\n    age: 22\\n};\\n```\\n\\n### Keys\\n\\n```javascript\\nObject.keys(user);\\n```\\n\\n```javascript\\n[\\\"name\\\", \\\"age\\\"]\\n```\\n\\n### Values\\n\\n```javascript\\nObject.values(user);\\n```\\n\\n```javascript\\n[\\\"Dev\\\", 22]\\n```\\n\\n### Entries\\n\\n```javascript\\nObject.entries(user);\\n```\\n\\n```javascript\\n[\\n    [\\\"name\\\", \\\"Dev\\\"],\\n    [\\\"age\\\", 22]\\n]\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.39 Object → Map\\n\\nVery useful:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst user = {\\n    name: \\\"Dev\\\",\\n    age: 22\\n};\\n\\nconst map = new Map(Object.entries(user));\\n```\\n\\nNow:\\n\\n```javascript\\nmap.get(\\\"name\\\");\\n```\\n\\nreturns:\\n\\n```text\\nDev\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10.40 Map → Object\\n\\nYou can do:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst map = new Map([\\n    [\\\"name\\\", \\\"Dev\\\"],\\n    [\\\"age\\\", 22]\\n]);\\n\\nconst obj = Object.fromEntries(map);\\n```\\n\\nResult:\\n\\n```javascript\\n{\\n    name: \\\"Dev\\\",\\n    age: 22\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThis is very useful when working with APIs and data transformations.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🔥 10.41 Most Important Interview Comparison\\n\\nMemorize this:\\n\\n| Need | Use |\\n|---|---|\\n| Ordered sequence | Array |\\n| Unique values | Set |\\n| Key-value collection | Map |\\n| Object-keyed metadata with weak references | WeakMap |\\n| Object membership tracking with weak references | WeakSet |\\n| Structured record | Object |\\n| Text | String |\\n| Numeric calculation | Number / BigInt |\\n| Pattern matching | RegExp |\\n| Math operations | Math |\\n| Date/time | Date / Temporal |\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🔥 10.42 Map vs Object\\n\\nThis is a **very common interview question**.\\n\\n### Object\\n\\nGood for:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst user = {\\n    name: \\\"Dev\\\",\\n    age: 22\\n};\\n```\\n\\nRepresenting a structured entity.\\n\\n### Map\\n\\nGood for:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst cache = new Map();\\n\\ncache.set(userId, userData);\\n```\\n\\nRepresenting a dynamic key-value collection.\\n\\nInterview answer:\\n\\n\u003e Objects are generally used for structured records with properties, while `Map` is specifically designed for key-value collections and supports arbitrary key types.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🔥 10.43 Set vs Array\\n\\nArray:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst arr = [1, 2, 2, 3];\\n```\\n\\nDuplicates allowed.\\n\\nSet:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst set = new Set([1, 2, 2, 3]);\\n```\\n\\nDuplicates removed.\\n\\nSo if you need:\\n\\n```text\\nmembership + uniqueness\\n```\\n\\na `Set` is often appropriate.\\n\\nIf you need:\\n\\n```text\\nordered sequence + indexing\\n```\\n\\nuse an Array.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🧪 Phase 10 Coding Checkpoint\\n\\nTry these yourself.\\n\\n### Problem 1 — Frequency Counter\\n\\nGiven:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst nums = [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4];\\n```\\n\\nUse `Map` to produce:\\n\\n```text\\n1 → 1\\n2 → 2\\n3 → 3\\n4 → 1\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Problem 2 — Remove Duplicates\\n\\nGiven:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst nums = [5, 1, 5, 2, 1, 3];\\n```\\n\\nProduce:\\n\\n```text\\n[5, 1, 2, 3]\\n```\\n\\nusing `Set`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Problem 3 — First Duplicate\\n\\nGiven:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst nums = [4, 2, 7, 2, 5, 7];\\n```\\n\\nFind the first duplicate using a `Set`.\\n\\nExpected:\\n\\n```text\\n2\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Problem 4 — Character Frequency\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst text = \\\"javascript\\\";\\n```\\n\\nUse a `Map` to count each character.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Problem 5 — Map Conversion\\n\\nConvert:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst user = {\\n    name: \\\"Dev\\\",\\n    age: 22\\n};\\n```\\n\\nto a `Map`, then retrieve:\\n\\n```text\\nDev\\n```\\n\\nusing `map.get()`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🧠 Phase 10 — What You Should Now Know\\n\\n```text\\nCollections\\n│\\n├── Array\\n│\\n├── Set\\n│\\n├── Map\\n│\\n├── WeakSet\\n│\\n└── WeakMap\\n\\nBuilt-ins\\n│\\n├── Object\\n├── String\\n├── Number\\n├── BigInt\\n├── Math\\n├── Date\\n└── RegExp\\n```\\n\\nAnd your Java mental mapping should now look like:\\n\\n```text\\nArrayList       → Array\\nHashSet         → Set\\nHashMap         → Map\\nWeakHashMap     → WeakMap\\nIterator        → Iterator\\nPattern         → RegExp\\nMath            → Math\\nString          → String\\n```\\n\\nBut always remember:\\n\\n\u003e **Similar purpose does not mean identical behavior.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🗺️ Updated Roadmap\\n\\n```text\\n✅ Phase 0   Java → JS Mental Model\\n✅ Phase 1   Variables \u0026 Data Types\\n✅ Phase 1B  Operators \u0026 Coercion\\n✅ Phase 2   Conditions \u0026 Loops\\n✅ Phase 3   Arrays\\n✅ Phase 4   Objects\\n✅ Phase 5   Functions\\n✅ Phase 6   this + OOP + Prototypes\\n✅ Phase 7   Execution Model\\n✅ Phase 8   Advanced Functions\\n✅ Phase 9   Async JavaScript\\n✅ Phase 10  Collections \u0026 Built-ins\\n\\n➡️ Phase 11  Modules \u0026 Modern JavaScript\\n              ├── import / export\\n              ├── ES Modules\\n              ├── CommonJS\\n              ├── default vs named exports\\n              ├── dynamic imports\\n              ├── strict mode\\n              ├── top-level await\\n              ├── npm\\n              ├── package.json\\n              ├── dependencies\\n              └── module resolution\\n\\n➡️ Phase 12  Browser \u0026 DOM\\n➡️ Phase 13  Events \u0026 Event Handling\\n➡️ Phase 14  HTTP, REST \u0026 APIs\\n➡️ Phase 15  Advanced JavaScript Patterns\\n➡️ Phase 16  Node.js Fundamentals\\n➡️ Phase 17  Express.js\\n➡️ Phase 18  JavaScript Security\\n➡️ Phase 19  Performance \u0026 Memory\\n➡️ Phase 20  React JavaScript Foundations\\n➡️ Phase 21  JavaScript Interview Mastery\\n➡️ Phase 22  DSA in JavaScript\\n```\\n\\n**Phase 11 is where JavaScript starts connecting strongly to the code you'll actually write in React/Node/Vite projects:** modules, `import/export`, npm, `package.json`, dependencies, and how JavaScript files communicate with each other.\",{},{\"_197\":1432,\"_204\":1788,\"_54\":1789,\"_208\":1790,\"_210\":211,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":1791,\"_217\":218},\"cc30f2cc-02cb-4383-93f9-96e0b528df0f\",[1419],{\"_656\":657,\"_215\":1792},1789569687.9533904,{\"_651\":667,\"_668\":669},{\"_233\":234,\"_240\":1445,\"_242\":1445,\"_245\":1786,\"_248\":10,\"_666\":34},{},{\"_197\":1786,\"_204\":1796,\"_54\":1797,\"_208\":1798,\"_210\":211,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":1799,\"_217\":218},\"4031c4e0-844c-4050-858a-5bd36019df2d\",[1432],{\"_656\":681,\"_215\":1802},1789569687.6616607,{\"_651\":652,\"_653\":1801},{\"_678\":34,\"_246\":1800,\"_228\":67,\"_233\":234,\"_236\":1444,\"_238\":239,\"_240\":1445,\"_242\":1445,\"_243\":67,\"_66\":67,\"_245\":1794,\"_248\":10,\"_244\":14},\"0461e475-ae7a-4db3-92bc-a46d4601e232\",[669],{},{\"_197\":1794,\"_204\":1806,\"_54\":1807,\"_208\":1808,\"_210\":211,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":1809,\"_217\":218},\"4ce7b887-56e1-4451-b4d8-cf0602685f95\",[1786],{\"_656\":681,\"_215\":1811},1789569687.661312,{\"_651\":652,\"_653\":1810},{\"_678\":34,\"_246\":1800,\"_228\":67,\"_233\":234,\"_236\":1444,\"_238\":239,\"_240\":1445,\"_242\":1445,\"_243\":67,\"_66\":67,\"_245\":1804,\"_248\":10,\"_244\":14},[669],{},{\"_197\":1804,\"_204\":1815,\"_54\":1816,\"_208\":1817,\"_210\":211,\"_212\":34,\"_213\":689,\"_215\":1818,\"_217\":218},\"7f35c139-b755-489c-a929-7b8fc76801f0\",[1794],{\"_656\":681,\"_215\":1820},1789569688.702358,{\"_651\":652,\"_653\":1819},{\"_678\":34,\"_246\":1800,\"_228\":67,\"_233\":234,\"_236\":1444,\"_238\":239,\"_240\":1445,\"_242