999,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1998,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1997,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1996,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1995,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1994,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1993,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1992,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1991,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1990,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1989,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1988,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1987,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1986,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1985,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1984,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1983,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1982,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1981,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1980,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1979,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1978,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1977,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1976,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":1975,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},\"120g21\",\"63cu47\",\"nflqo8\",\"zjxybr\",\"d8mz03\",\"719nux\",\"lm3cqi\",\"2vxtwf\",\"7uozvc\",\"mxldjx\",\"z47o4l\",\"tjg9dx\",\"a8p3aq\",\"jq0irl\",\"9zs1ta\",\"og7w45\",\"mvpt7f\",\"z8mc8a\",\"ix77lv\",\"226oo6\",\"uz0qzj\",\"ub9352\",\"s2aq5n\",\"75gkn6\",\"kemxr1\",\"jorgfb\",\"j9pl77\",\"i4qnj2\",\"cqf5m1\",\"n9y92a\",\"b98hc7\",\"zd6iqd\",\"x86lfz\",\"ltrlhs\",\"yja6wp\",\"3xhhm7\",\"ndmw7a\",\"klbunw\",\"ta6kl5\",\"56vxi3\",\"3dv2sj\",\"6rkv37\",\"cpgxzc\",\"0ukqjx\",\"ogaagm\",\"aq32q3\",\"u437et\",\"ppoua3\",\"nbhj9b\",\"njsuvy\",\"7cb552\",\"zn8q62\",\"ap0eej\",\"a975f5\",\"rwollr\",\"mxxv1x\",\"j082ej\",\"pirflk\",\"ho7ylc\",\"5t4t1s\",\"9d35p6\",\"flwvft\",\"t20g0w\",\"7fowys\",\"ij3sdd\",\"20lzll\",\"dszzva\",\"usbcwm\",\"3tmqna\",\"4jkl5r\",\"k46i3i\",\"cavawf\",\"ybowic\",\"dpm5jj\",\"k3rtc8\",\"exj4sl\",\"u91z46\",\"fabgg0\",\"txbf1y\",\"ir7wwe\",\"g07y29\",\"4hgvhs\",\"gu1pih\",\"afv86k\",\"04yke8\",\"5sqo4q\",\"x3dnke\",\"mncik0\",\"ljtzw0\",\"uy5ihw\",\"m2atvl\",\"bg8vp1\",\"mwscf5\",\"7bc1jr\",\"ejaxvs\",\"hgrg44\",\"jnzsr8\",\"lc8a7n\",\"yfcrlw\",\"iaui42\",\"vkvvaw\",\"vwnj48\",\"396lls\",\"kc24pw\",\"piddwg\",\"h66cns\",\"binfnh\",\"bo3u8m\",\"bc9yfh\",\"bfi42n\",\"2bhhkn\",\"6bxuhw\",\"6iqpql\",\"7smht9\",\"prj3pl\",\"h8h0fx\",[650],[2093],\"# 🚀 Phase 9 — Asynchronous JavaScript: Deep Dive\\n\\nThis phase is **extremely important for JavaScript interviews**.\\n\\nYou already know the basic event loop, Promises, microtasks, and `async/await`. Now we're going to make you comfortable with:\\n\\n- Promise creation\\n- Promise chaining\\n- Promise error propagation\\n- `Promise.all()`\\n- `Promise.allSettled()`\\n- `Promise.race()`\\n- `Promise.any()`\\n- sequential vs parallel execution\\n- `async/await` behavior\\n- `fetch()`\\n- `AbortController`\\n- concurrency\\n- tricky output questions\\n\\nAnd throughout, I'll compare it to **Java `CompletableFuture`**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.1 First: What Exactly Is a Promise?\\n\\nA Promise represents the eventual result of an operation.\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst p = new Promise((resolve, reject) =\u003e {\\n    // asynchronous work\\n\\n    resolve(\\\"Success\\\");\\n});\\n```\\n\\nIt has three conceptual states:\\n\\n```text\\n             ┌───────────┐\\n             │  pending  │\\n             └─────┬─────┘\\n                   │\\n             ┌─────┴─────┐\\n             ↓           ↓\\n        fulfilled     rejected\\n```\\n\\nOnce settled, it cannot transition again.\\n\\n```javascript\\nresolve(\\\"A\\\");\\nresolve(\\\"B\\\");\\n```\\n\\nOnly the first settlement matters.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.2 `resolve()` Doesn't Mean \\\"Return\\\"\\n\\nThis is a common beginner mistake.\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst p = new Promise((resolve, reject) =\u003e {\\n\\n    resolve(\\\"Done\\\");\\n\\n    console.log(\\\"Hello\\\");\\n});\\n```\\n\\nYou might think `\\\"Hello\\\"` won't execute.\\n\\nIt **will**.\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nHello\\n```\\n\\n`resolve()` settles the Promise. It does not automatically stop JavaScript execution inside the executor.\\n\\nIf you want to stop executing:\\n\\n```javascript\\nreturn resolve(\\\"Done\\\");\\n```\\n\\nor use another control-flow mechanism.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.3 `.then()`\\n\\n```javascript\\nPromise.resolve(10)\\n    .then(value =\u003e {\\n        console.log(value);\\n    });\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n10\\n```\\n\\nBut `.then()` itself returns a **new Promise**.\\n\\nThis is what makes chaining possible.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.4 Promise Chaining\\n\\n```javascript\\nPromise.resolve(10)\\n    .then(x =\u003e x * 2)\\n    .then(x =\u003e x + 5)\\n    .then(x =\u003e console.log(x));\\n```\\n\\nFlow:\\n\\n```text\\n10\\n ↓\\n×2\\n ↓\\n20\\n ↓\\n+5\\n ↓\\n25\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n25\\n```\\n\\nThink of it as:\\n\\n```text\\nPromise\\n   ↓\\nthen()\\n   ↓\\nnew Promise\\n   ↓\\nthen()\\n   ↓\\nnew Promise\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.5 Why `return` Matters in `.then()`\\n\\nConsider:\\n\\n```javascript\\nPromise.resolve(10)\\n    .then(x =\u003e {\\n        return x * 2;\\n    })\\n    .then(x =\u003e {\\n        console.log(x);\\n    });\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n20\\n```\\n\\nNow:\\n\\n```javascript\\nPromise.resolve(10)\\n    .then(x =\u003e {\\n        x * 2;\\n    })\\n    .then(x =\u003e {\\n        console.log(x);\\n    });\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nundefined\\n```\\n\\nWhy?\\n\\nBecause the callback doesn't return anything.\\n\\nThe first `.then()` therefore fulfills its returned Promise with:\\n\\n```javascript\\nundefined\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.6 Returning a Promise from `.then()`\\n\\nThis is even more important.\\n\\n```javascript\\nPromise.resolve(10)\\n    .then(x =\u003e {\\n        return Promise.resolve(x * 2);\\n    })\\n    .then(x =\u003e {\\n        console.log(x);\\n    });\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n20\\n```\\n\\nThe Promise returned by the first `.then()` adopts the state/result of the Promise you returned.\\n\\nThis is called **Promise resolution/adoption**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.7 Promise Error Handling\\n\\nYou can use:\\n\\n```javascript\\n.catch()\\n```\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```javascript\\nPromise.reject(\\\"Something went wrong\\\")\\n    .catch(error =\u003e {\\n        console.log(error);\\n    });\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nSomething went wrong\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.8 Errors Propagate Through the Chain\\n\\n```javascript\\nPromise.resolve()\\n    .then(() =\u003e {\\n        throw new Error(\\\"Oops\\\");\\n    })\\n    .then(() =\u003e {\\n        console.log(\\\"This won't execute\\\");\\n    })\\n    .catch(error =\u003e {\\n        console.log(error.message);\\n    });\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nOops\\n```\\n\\nThe thrown error causes the Promise returned by that `.then()` to reject.\\n\\nThe rejection travels down the chain until a handler handles it.\\n\\nMental model:\\n\\n```text\\nthen()\\n  ↓\\nERROR\\n  ↓\\nrejected Promise\\n  ↓\\nskip normal then()\\n  ↓\\ncatch()\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.9 `finally()`\\n\\n`finally()` runs whether the Promise fulfills or rejects.\\n\\n```javascript\\nfetchData()\\n    .then(data =\u003e {\\n        console.log(data);\\n    })\\n    .catch(error =\u003e {\\n        console.log(error);\\n    })\\n    .finally(() =\u003e {\\n        console.log(\\\"Cleanup\\\");\\n    });\\n```\\n\\nTypical use:\\n\\n- hide loading indicator\\n- close resource\\n- cleanup\\n- reset state\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.10 Java Comparison\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\nfuture\\n    .thenApply(...)\\n    .exceptionally(...)\\n    .whenComplete(...);\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```javascript\\npromise\\n    .then(...)\\n    .catch(...)\\n    .finally(...);\\n```\\n\\nThey're conceptually similar, but don't assume their APIs and scheduling semantics are identical.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.11 `async` Functions Always Return a Promise\\n\\nThis is **very important**.\\n\\n```javascript\\nasync function test() {\\n    return 10;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nCalling:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst result = test();\\n\\nconsole.log(result);\\n```\\n\\ndoes **not** give:\\n\\n```text\\n10\\n```\\n\\nIt gives a fulfilled Promise containing `10`.\\n\\nConceptually:\\n\\n```javascript\\nasync function test() {\\n    return Promise.resolve(10);\\n}\\n```\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```javascript\\ntest().then(value =\u003e console.log(value));\\n```\\n\\nprints:\\n\\n```text\\n10\\n```\\n\\n### Interview question\\n\\n**Does an async function always return a Promise?**\\n\\nYes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.12 `await`\\n\\nConsider:\\n\\n```javascript\\nasync function test() {\\n    const result = await Promise.resolve(10);\\n\\n    console.log(result);\\n}\\n```\\n\\n`await` extracts the fulfilled value for the continuation of the async function.\\n\\nBut don't think:\\n\\n\u003e \\\"JavaScript blocks until the Promise finishes.\\\"\\n\\nThat's misleading.\\n\\nInstead, the async function's continuation is suspended until the awaited Promise settles.\\n\\nThe JavaScript thread can continue doing other work.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.13 Important Example\\n\\n```javascript\\nasync function test() {\\n    console.log(\\\"A\\\");\\n\\n    await Promise.resolve();\\n\\n    console.log(\\\"B\\\");\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(\\\"C\\\");\\n\\ntest();\\n\\nconsole.log(\\\"D\\\");\\n```\\n\\nLet's trace it.\\n\\nFirst:\\n\\n```text\\nC\\n```\\n\\nThen `test()` begins:\\n\\n```text\\nA\\n```\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```javascript\\nawait Promise.resolve();\\n```\\n\\nThe remainder:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconsole.log(\\\"B\\\");\\n```\\n\\nis scheduled to continue asynchronously.\\n\\nControl returns to synchronous code:\\n\\n```text\\nD\\n```\\n\\nThen the continuation runs:\\n\\n```text\\nB\\n```\\n\\nFinal:\\n\\n```text\\nC\\nA\\nD\\nB\\n```\\n\\n🔥 This is a very common interview pattern.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.14 `await` and Microtasks\\n\\nEven when the Promise is already fulfilled:\\n\\n```javascript\\nawait Promise.resolve();\\n```\\n\\ndoesn't mean the following statement executes immediately within the same synchronous stack.\\n\\nThe continuation happens asynchronously.\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```javascript\\nasync function test() {\\n    console.log(1);\\n    await Promise.resolve();\\n    console.log(2);\\n}\\n\\ntest();\\n\\nconsole.log(3);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n1\\n3\\n2\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.15 Sequential Async Operations\\n\\nSuppose you have:\\n\\n```javascript\\nasync function getUser() {\\n    return fetch(\\\"/user\\\");\\n}\\n\\nasync function getPosts() {\\n    return fetch(\\\"/posts\\\");\\n}\\n```\\n\\nIf you do:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst user = await getUser();\\nconst posts = await getPosts();\\n```\\n\\nthe second operation starts only after the first has completed.\\n\\nConceptually:\\n\\n```text\\ngetUser\\n   ↓\\nwait\\n   ↓\\ngetPosts\\n   ↓\\nwait\\n```\\n\\nThis is **sequential execution**.\\n\\nSometimes that's necessary.\\n\\nBut sometimes it's wasteful.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.16 Parallel / Concurrent Async Operations\\n\\nSuppose the operations don't depend on each other.\\n\\nInstead:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst userPromise = getUser();\\nconst postsPromise = getPosts();\\n\\nconst user = await userPromise;\\nconst posts = await postsPromise;\\n```\\n\\nBoth operations are started before waiting for either result.\\n\\nBetter yet:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst [user, posts] = await Promise.all([\\n    getUser(),\\n    getPosts()\\n]);\\n```\\n\\nConceptually:\\n\\n```text\\n             ┌── getUser ──┐\\nStart ───────┤             ├── results\\n             └── getPosts ─┘\\n```\\n\\nThis can be substantially faster when the operations are independent.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.17 The Important Distinction\\n\\nThese:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst a = await getA();\\nconst b = await getB();\\n```\\n\\nand:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst [a, b] = await Promise.all([\\n    getA(),\\n    getB()\\n]);\\n```\\n\\nare **not equivalent in timing**.\\n\\nFirst:\\n\\n```text\\nA starts\\n↓\\nA finishes\\n↓\\nB starts\\n↓\\nB finishes\\n```\\n\\nSecond:\\n\\n```text\\nA starts\\nB starts\\n↓\\nwait for both\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.18 `Promise.all()`\\n\\nThis is one of the most important Promise APIs.\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst results = await Promise.all([\\n    fetchUser(),\\n    fetchPosts(),\\n    fetchComments()\\n]);\\n```\\n\\nIf all fulfill:\\n\\n```javascript\\nresults\\n```\\n\\ncontains their results **in input order**.\\n\\nEven if completion order differs.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```text\\nInput:\\nA\\nB\\nC\\n\\nCompletion:\\nB\\nC\\nA\\n\\nPromise.all result:\\nA\\nB\\nC\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.19 `Promise.all()` Rejects Fast\\n\\nSuppose:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst result = await Promise.all([\\n    Promise.resolve(\\\"A\\\"),\\n    Promise.reject(\\\"B\\\"),\\n    Promise.resolve(\\\"C\\\")\\n]);\\n```\\n\\nThe combined Promise rejects.\\n\\n```text\\nrejected → \\\"B\\\"\\n```\\n\\nYou don't get:\\n\\n```javascript\\n[\\\"A\\\", \\\"B\\\", \\\"C\\\"]\\n```\\n\\nInstead, the aggregate Promise is rejected.\\n\\n### Important nuance\\n\\n\\\"Rejects fast\\\" does **not** mean the other underlying operations are automatically cancelled.\\n\\nFor example, if three network requests are already running and one rejects, `Promise.all()` rejecting does not inherently cancel the other requests.\\n\\nThis distinction is a great interview point.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.20 `Promise.allSettled()`\\n\\nWhat if you want the outcome of **every** operation?\\n\\nUse:\\n\\n```javascript\\nPromise.allSettled()\\n```\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst results = await Promise.allSettled([\\n    Promise.resolve(\\\"A\\\"),\\n    Promise.reject(\\\"B\\\"),\\n    Promise.resolve(\\\"C\\\")\\n]);\\n```\\n\\nYou get something conceptually like:\\n\\n```javascript\\n[\\n    { status: \\\"fulfilled\\\", value: \\\"A\\\" },\\n    { status: \\\"rejected\\\", reason: \\\"B\\\" },\\n    { status: \\\"fulfilled\\\", value: \\\"C\\\" }\\n]\\n```\\n\\nIt waits for all supplied Promises to settle.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.21 `Promise.all()` vs `allSettled()`\\n\\n| API | Waits for | If one rejects |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `Promise.all()` | all to fulfill | aggregate rejects |\\n| `Promise.allSettled()` | all to settle | gives every result |\\n\\nUse `all()` when:\\n\\n\u003e I need everything to succeed.\\n\\nUse `allSettled()` when:\\n\\n\u003e I want to know what happened to every operation regardless of failure.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.22 `Promise.race()`\\n\\n`Promise.race()` settles when the **first input Promise settles**.\\n\\nThat means the first result can be:\\n\\n- fulfillment\\n- rejection\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst result = await Promise.race([\\n    new Promise(resolve =\u003e\\n        setTimeout(() =\u003e resolve(\\\"A\\\"), 1000)\\n    ),\\n\\n    new Promise(resolve =\u003e\\n        setTimeout(() =\u003e resolve(\\\"B\\\"), 500)\\n    )\\n]);\\n\\nconsole.log(result);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nB\\n```\\n\\nbecause B settles first.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.23 `Promise.any()`\\n\\n`Promise.any()` waits for the **first fulfilled Promise**.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst result = await Promise.any([\\n    Promise.reject(\\\"A\\\"),\\n    Promise.resolve(\\\"B\\\"),\\n    Promise.resolve(\\\"C\\\")\\n]);\\n\\nconsole.log(result);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nB\\n```\\n\\nThe first rejection doesn't matter because another Promise fulfills.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.24 `race()` vs `any()`\\n\\nThis distinction is frequently asked.\\n\\n### `Promise.race()`\\n\\nFirst Promise to **settle** wins.\\n\\n```text\\nfulfilled OR rejected\\n```\\n\\n### `Promise.any()`\\n\\nFirst Promise to **fulfill** wins.\\n\\n```text\\nfulfilled only\\n```\\n\\nIf every Promise rejects, `Promise.any()` rejects with an **`AggregateError`**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.25 All Four Promise Combinators\\n\\nMemorize this table:\\n\\n| Method | Result condition |\\n|---|---|\\n| `Promise.all()` | all fulfill |\\n| `Promise.allSettled()` | all settle |\\n| `Promise.race()` | first settles |\\n| `Promise.any()` | first fulfills |\\n\\nMental model:\\n\\n```text\\nall\\n ↓\\nEVERYTHING must succeed\\n\\nallSettled\\n ↓\\nI want EVERYTHING's outcome\\n\\nrace\\n ↓\\nWHOEVER finishes first\\n\\nany\\n ↓\\nWHOEVER succeeds first\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.26 Practical Example — Multiple Servers\\n\\nImagine three servers can provide the same data:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst result = await Promise.any([\\n    fetch(\\\"server1\\\"),\\n    fetch(\\\"server2\\\"),\\n    fetch(\\\"server3\\\")\\n]);\\n```\\n\\nYou want:\\n\\n\u003e whichever server successfully responds first.\\n\\nThat's a good use case for `Promise.any()`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.27 `fetch()`\\n\\n`fetch()` is the modern browser API for HTTP requests.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst response = await fetch(\\\"/api/users\\\");\\n```\\n\\nImportant:\\n\\n\u003e `fetch()` returns a Promise.\\n\\nThe first result is a **Response object**, not automatically the parsed JSON.\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst response = await fetch(\\\"/api/users\\\");\\n\\nconst data = await response.json();\\n```\\n\\nThere are two asynchronous stages:\\n\\n```text\\nfetch()\\n  ↓\\nResponse\\n  ↓\\nresponse.json()\\n  ↓\\nJavaScript object/data\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.28 Important `fetch()` Error Trap\\n\\nMany beginners assume:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfetch(\\\"/does-not-exist\\\")\\n```\\n\\nautomatically rejects for an HTTP 404.\\n\\nGenerally, **HTTP error status codes such as 404 or 500 do not by themselves cause `fetch()` to reject**.\\n\\nThe network request can succeed while the HTTP response indicates failure.\\n\\nTherefore:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst response = await fetch(url);\\n\\nif (!response.ok) {\\n    throw new Error(`HTTP error: ${response.status}`);\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst data = await response.json();\\n```\\n\\nThis is a very good interview detail.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.29 `AbortController`\\n\\nSuppose you start:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst response = await fetch(\\\"/api/data\\\");\\n```\\n\\nbut you want to cancel the request.\\n\\nUse:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst controller = new AbortController();\\n\\nfetch(\\\"/api/data\\\", {\\n    signal: controller.signal\\n});\\n```\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```javascript\\ncontroller.abort();\\n```\\n\\nThis signals cancellation to the fetch operation.\\n\\nUseful for:\\n\\n- cancelling stale requests\\n- search boxes\\n- component cleanup\\n- timeouts\\n- avoiding unnecessary network work\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.30 Example: Search Box\\n\\nImagine:\\n\\n```text\\nUser types:\\nJ\\nJa\\nJav\\nJava\\n```\\n\\nYou don't necessarily want every previous request to continue.\\n\\nYou can cancel the previous request when a new query starts:\\n\\n```javascript\\nlet controller;\\n\\nasync function search(query) {\\n\\n    if (controller) {\\n        controller.abort();\\n    }\\n\\n    controller = new AbortController();\\n\\n    try {\\n        const response = await fetch(\\n            `/search?q=${query}`,\\n            { signal: controller.signal }\\n        );\\n\\n        const data = await response.json();\\n\\n        console.log(data);\\n\\n    } catch (error) {\\n        if (error.name === \\\"AbortError\\\") {\\n            return;\\n        }\\n\\n        console.error(error);\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThat's a practical real-world use of asynchronous JavaScript.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.31 Concurrency vs Parallelism\\n\\nThis distinction matters.\\n\\n### Concurrency\\n\\nMultiple tasks can make progress during overlapping periods.\\n\\nJavaScript's asynchronous model allows:\\n\\n```text\\nTask A ────────┐\\n               ├── progress overlaps\\nTask B ────────┘\\n```\\n\\n### Parallelism\\n\\nMultiple tasks literally execute simultaneously, typically on multiple execution resources/threads/cores.\\n\\nFor ordinary browser JavaScript execution, don't equate asynchronous concurrency with CPU parallelism.\\n\\nModern JavaScript environments can use mechanisms such as **Web Workers** for actual parallel computation.\\n\\nWe'll cover that later.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.32 Classic Interview Question #1\\n\\n```javascript\\nconsole.log(\\\"1\\\");\\n\\nsetTimeout(() =\u003e {\\n    console.log(\\\"2\\\");\\n}, 0);\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(() =\u003e {\\n    console.log(\\\"3\\\");\\n});\\n\\nconsole.log(\\\"4\\\");\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n1\\n4\\n3\\n2\\n```\\n\\nReason:\\n\\n```text\\nSynchronous:\\n1\\n4\\n\\nMicrotask:\\n3\\n\\nTask:\\n2\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.33 Classic Interview Question #2\\n\\n```javascript\\nasync function test() {\\n    console.log(\\\"A\\\");\\n\\n    await Promise.resolve();\\n\\n    console.log(\\\"B\\\");\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(\\\"C\\\");\\n\\ntest();\\n\\nconsole.log(\\\"D\\\");\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nC\\nA\\nD\\nB\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.34 Classic Interview Question #3\\n\\n```javascript\\nasync function test() {\\n    console.log(1);\\n\\n    await Promise.resolve();\\n\\n    console.log(2);\\n}\\n\\ntest();\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(() =\u003e {\\n    console.log(3);\\n});\\n\\nconsole.log(4);\\n```\\n\\nLet's carefully trace it.\\n\\n`test()` starts:\\n\\n```text\\n1\\n```\\n\\nIt reaches `await`.\\n\\nIts continuation is queued as a microtask.\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```javascript\\nPromise.resolve().then(...)\\n```\\n\\nqueues another microtask.\\n\\nThen synchronous:\\n\\n```text\\n4\\n```\\n\\nMicrotask queue:\\n\\n```text\\ntest continuation\\nthen callback\\n```\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```text\\n1\\n4\\n2\\n3\\n```\\n\\n🔥 Don't guess these. Trace the queue.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.35 Classic Interview Question #4\\n\\n```javascript\\nasync function test() {\\n    console.log(\\\"A\\\");\\n\\n    await Promise.resolve(\\\"B\\\");\\n\\n    console.log(\\\"C\\\");\\n}\\n\\ntest();\\n\\nconsole.log(\\\"D\\\");\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nA\\nD\\nC\\n```\\n\\nThe `\\\"B\\\"` value isn't printed because it is simply the result being awaited.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.36 Sequential vs Parallel — Interview Trap\\n\\nSuppose:\\n\\n```javascript\\nasync function task1() {\\n    return new Promise(resolve =\u003e\\n        setTimeout(() =\u003e resolve(\\\"A\\\"), 1000)\\n    );\\n}\\n\\nasync function task2() {\\n    return new Promise(resolve =\u003e\\n        setTimeout(() =\u003e resolve(\\\"B\\\"), 1000)\\n    );\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Version 1\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst a = await task1();\\nconst b = await task2();\\n```\\n\\nApproximate total:\\n\\n```text\\n2 seconds\\n```\\n\\n### Version 2\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst [a, b] = await Promise.all([\\n    task1(),\\n    task2()\\n]);\\n```\\n\\nApproximate total:\\n\\n```text\\n1 second\\n```\\n\\nbecause the operations overlap.\\n\\nThis is one of the most useful practical Promise concepts.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.37 But Don't Blindly Use `Promise.all()`\\n\\nSuppose:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst user = await getUser();\\n\\nconst posts = await getPosts(user.id);\\n```\\n\\nYou **cannot** necessarily parallelize those.\\n\\nWhy?\\n\\nBecause:\\n\\n```text\\nposts depends on user.id\\n```\\n\\nCorrect:\\n\\n```text\\ngetUser\\n  ↓\\nuser.id\\n  ↓\\ngetPosts(user.id)\\n```\\n\\nYou should only parallelize independent operations.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9.38 Java Comparison\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\nCompletableFuture\u003cUser\u003e user = getUser();\\nCompletableFuture\u003cPosts\u003e posts = getPosts();\\n\\nCompletableFuture.allOf(user, posts);\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst [user, posts] = await Promise.all([\\n    getUser(),\\n    getPosts()\\n]);\\n```\\n\\nConceptually similar:\\n\\n```text\\nstart independent work\\n        ↓\\nwait for all\\n        ↓\\ncontinue\\n```\\n\\nBut remember:\\n\\n**Promise ≠ CompletableFuture.**\\n\\nThe runtime and API semantics differ.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🔥 Phase 9 Interview Cheat Sheet\\n\\nYou should now know these cold:\\n\\n### Promise\\n\\n\u003e Object representing eventual fulfillment or rejection of an asynchronous operation.\\n\\n### `async`\\n\\n\u003e Declares a function whose return value is wrapped in a Promise and allows `await` inside it.\\n\\n### `await`\\n\\n\u003e Suspends the async function's continuation until the awaited value is settled; it does not block the JavaScript thread in the traditional sense.\\n\\n### `Promise.all()`\\n\\n\u003e Fulfills when all fulfill; rejects when one rejects.\\n\\n### `Promise.allSettled()`\\n\\n\u003e Waits for all inputs to settle and reports each outcome.\\n\\n### `Promise.race()`\\n\\n\u003e Settles when the first input settles.\\n\\n### `Promise.any()`\\n\\n\u003e Fulfills when the first input fulfills; rejects with `AggregateError` if all reject.\\n\\n### `fetch()`\\n\\n\u003e Returns a Promise for a `Response`; HTTP error statuses don't normally reject the Promise automatically.\\n\\n### `AbortController`\\n\\n\u003e Provides a mechanism for signalling cancellation to APIs such as `fetch()`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🧪 Phase 9 Coding Challenge\\n\\nDon't run these immediately. **Predict first.**\\n\\n### Challenge 1\\n\\n```javascript\\nconsole.log(\\\"A\\\");\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(() =\u003e {\\n    console.log(\\\"B\\\");\\n});\\n\\nsetTimeout(() =\u003e {\\n    console.log(\\\"C\\\");\\n}, 0);\\n\\nconsole.log(\\\"D\\\");\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Challenge 2\\n\\n```javascript\\nasync function test() {\\n    console.log(1);\\n\\n    await Promise.resolve();\\n\\n    console.log(2);\\n\\n    await Promise.resolve();\\n\\n    console.log(3);\\n}\\n\\ntest();\\n\\nconsole.log(4);\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Challenge 3\\n\\n```javascript\\nPromise.resolve(10)\\n    .then(x =\u003e x * 2)\\n    .then(x =\u003e {\\n        throw new Error(\\\"Oops\\\");\\n    })\\n    .then(() =\u003e {\\n        console.log(\\\"A\\\");\\n    })\\n    .catch(error =\u003e {\\n        console.log(error.message);\\n    })\\n    .finally(() =\u003e {\\n        console.log(\\\"B\\\");\\n    });\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Challenge 4\\n\\nWhat happens here?\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst result = await Promise.all([\\n    Promise.resolve(\\\"A\\\"),\\n    Promise.reject(\\\"B\\\"),\\n    Promise.resolve(\\\"C\\\")\\n]);\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Challenge 5\\n\\nWhich is faster when `getA()` and `getB()` are independent?\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst a = await getA();\\nconst b = await getB();\\n```\\n\\nor:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst [a, b] = await Promise.all([\\n    getA(),\\n    getB()\\n]);\\n```\\n\\nAnd **why**?\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🗺️ Updated Complete Roadmap\\n\\n```text\\n✅ Phase 0   Java → JS Mental Model\\n✅ Phase 1   Variables \u0026 Data Types\\n✅ Phase 1B  Operators \u0026 Coercion\\n✅ Phase 2   Conditions \u0026 Loops\\n✅ Phase 3   Arrays\\n✅ Phase 4   Objects\\n✅ Phase 5   Functions\\n✅ Phase 6   this + OOP + Prototypes\\n✅ Phase 7   Execution Model\\n              ├── Execution Context\\n              ├── Call Stack\\n              ├── Scope Chain\\n              ├── Hoisting\\n              ├── TDZ\\n              ├── Closures\\n              ├── Event Loop\\n              ├── Microtasks\\n              └── Tasks\\n\\n✅ Phase 8   Advanced Functions\\n              ├── Higher-order functions\\n              ├── Currying\\n              ├── Partial application\\n              ├── Composition\\n              ├── Pure functions\\n              ├── Immutability\\n              ├── Memoization\\n              ├── Iterators\\n              └── Generators\\n\\n🔥 Phase 9   Async JavaScript — Deep Dive\\n              ├── Promise chaining\\n              ├── Error propagation\\n              ├── async/await\\n              ├── Promise.all\\n              ├── Promise.allSettled\\n              ├── Promise.race\\n              ├── Promise.any\\n              ├── fetch\\n              ├── AbortController\\n              ├── Sequential vs parallel\\n              └── Concurrency\\n\\n➡️ Phase 10  JavaScript Collections \u0026 Built-ins\\n              ├── Map\\n              ├── Set\\n              ├── WeakMap\\n              ├── WeakSet\\n              ├── Date\\n              ├── RegExp\\n              ├── Math\\n              ├── Number\\n              ├── String\\n              └── Array/Object advanced APIs\\n```\\n\\n**Phase 10 will be especially useful for you as a Java programmer**, because we'll directly map Java's `HashMap`, `HashSet`, `WeakHashMap`, collections, strings, regex, dates, etc. to their JavaScript counterparts—and cover the differences that commonly appear in interviews.\",{},{\"_197\":1843,\"_204\":2098,\"_54\":2099,\"_208\":2100,\"_210\":211,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":2101,\"_217\":218},\"a9c990e9-93c1-4717-a42a-0ee8907b3cfd\",[1831],{\"_656\":681,\"_215\":2104},1789457260.9584267,{\"_651\":652,\"_653\":2103},{\"_678\":34,\"_246\":2102,\"_228\":67,\"_233\":234,\"_236\":1856,\"_238\":239,\"_240\":1857,\"_242\":1857,\"_243\":67,\"_66\":67,\"_245\":2096,\"_248\":10,\"_244\":14},\"81b76046-99eb-4a83-8bd4-183689b1eff9\",[669],{},{\"_197\":2096,\"_204\":2108,\"_54\":2109,\"_208\":2110,\"_210\":211,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":2111,\"_217\":218},\"faad1c86-c386-4906-bcdb-ffcf909af1ec\",[1843],{\"_656\":681,\"_215\":2113},1789457260.957998,{\"_651\":652,\"_653\":2112},{\"_678\":34,\"_246\":2102,\"_228\":67,\"_233\":234,\"_236\":1856,\"_238\":239,\"_240\":1857,\"_242\":1857,\"_243\":67,\"_66\":67,\"_245\":2106,\"_248\":10,\"_244\":14},[669],{},{\"_197\":2106,\"_204\":2117,\"_54\":2118,\"_56\":2119,\"_208\":2120,\"_210\":211,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":2121,\"_217\":218},\"43e423e2-227a-4fcc-9f14-1c9f26588137\",[2096],{\"_656\":709,\"_215\":2125},1789457261.126,1789457302.277074,{\"_651\":652,\"_653\":2124},{\"_702\":2122,\"_236\":1856,\"_704\":-5,\"_240\":1857,\"_242\":1857,\"_246\":2102,\"_248\":10,\"_244\":14},{\"_705\":2123},[],[708],{},{\"_197\":2115,\"_204\":2129,\"_54\":2130,\"_56\":2131,\"_208\":2132,\"_210\":211,\"_212\":34,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":2133,\"_217\":218,\"_21