:-5},{\"_197\":2308,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2307,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2306,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2305,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2304,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2303,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2302,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2301,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2300,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2299,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2298,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2297,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2296,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2295,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2294,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2293,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2292,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2291,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2290,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2289,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2288,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2287,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2286,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2285,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2284,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2283,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2282,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2281,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2280,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":2279,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},\"ef7s7q\",\"a8r5f7\",\"8df39z\",\"85xrcq\",\"z2fae3\",\"k1mc7j\",\"ms2e0i\",\"dcnkwe\",\"jbfke8\",\"v47af4\",\"txlu94\",\"55nx1s\",\"29w3kx\",\"atnom3\",\"5aztq6\",\"pxb37s\",\"hkr1mj\",\"42nyr9\",\"h6wu8u\",\"hpm9j5\",\"oub6yf\",\"h5xx1x\",\"zvuy8r\",\"r8a011\",\"aef449\",\"kl6p94\",\"9lc6du\",\"w21d7c\",\"wv9f66\",\"xgyutf\",\"8hclhh\",\"18ugqx\",\"bv5u12\",\"fvs5mf\",\"7z80wk\",\"iuctqv\",\"1qmj06\",\"51hrvp\",\"vy320n\",\"dl3bgu\",\"flxut8\",\"gg5uj9\",\"y2aqzm\",\"5sb2fz\",\"ustqg7\",\"3k58kh\",\"rkmy3d\",\"cq1bvv\",\"wy9prg\",\"cbh2sm\",\"6cixhi\",\"4kxa4l\",\"745a6o\",\"y2ukty\",\"8g8eyk\",\"0u2k8j\",\"a6ys1w\",\"5l74kj\",\"8w1zqx\",\"53uks7\",\"6zr7p2\",\"qky7l1\",\"y8g6gm\",\"obs5hw\",\"gqx9y9\",\"vslh7j\",\"khqu6b\",\"k489oc\",\"d7rl5m\",\"anepgo\",\"nxvxeo\",\"2b6jlp\",\"r2j3nx\",\"2q11hf\",\"eedx2h\",\"6dl1xd\",\"5o15ly\",\"91i043\",\"z4vzim\",\"7xb7ra\",\"mwj5lo\",\"68n1cx\",\"cm67j2\",\"o25qt5\",\"f0c7tw\",\"exqx4q\",\"p5kkpv\",\"mpxy8r\",\"2pigu7\",\"72esul\",\"ww5hhm\",\"bvppry\",\"1zysdr\",\"u06kph\",\"jlq4ni\",\"1gd1pd\",\"1wnp0t\",\"a70dyt\",\"nbk9hm\",\"qtwsnz\",\"cyw0bh\",\"3gey8x\",\"aq3eet\",\"vygknp\",\"kt3xkq\",\"fk47ow\",\"y92im5\",\"g1dqda\",\"bgthyh\",\"8xe22m\",\"sm6yj1\",\"6ntw8x\",\"bsd2to\",\"vmu3a1\",\"1ngv9a\",\"9ie8kc\",\"f09aqa\",\"e8351p\",\"gkogvx\",\"8iz32f\",\"g5gnt9\",\"e46cp4\",\"mtjqbr\",\"szz8da\",\"tg2y4h\",\"3omt08\",\"v517qa\",\"mzq9mx\",\"o0e2nj\",\"d225zq\",\"a81r6t\",\"muco8k\",\"zb5ix8\",\"0z4gl4\",\"u7y085\",\"cqiiyi\",[650],[2417],\"# 🚀 Phase 8 — Advanced Functions \u0026 Functional JavaScript\\n\\nNow we're going deeper into **how functions are used as building blocks** in JavaScript.\\n\\nYou already know:\\n- functions\\n- arrow functions\\n- callbacks\\n- closures\\n- `map/filter/reduce`\\n\\nThis phase connects those concepts and covers several **common interview topics**.\\n\\nAs a Java developer, think of this as the JavaScript side of **functional programming + Java lambdas + Streams**, but JavaScript is considerably more flexible.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.1 Functions Are First-Class Citizens\\n\\nYou've seen this already, but it is fundamental.\\n\\nIn JavaScript, a function is a value.\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction greet() {\\n    console.log(\\\"Hello\\\");\\n}\\n```\\n\\nYou can store it:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst fn = greet;\\n```\\n\\nCall it:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfn();\\n```\\n\\nPass it:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction execute(operation) {\\n    operation();\\n}\\n\\nexecute(greet);\\n```\\n\\nReturn it:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction createFunction() {\\n    return greet;\\n}\\n\\nconst f = createFunction();\\nf();\\n```\\n\\nSo functions can be:\\n\\n```text\\nstored\\npassed\\nreturned\\ncreated dynamically\\n```\\n\\n### Java comparison\\n\\nJava supports similar behavior through:\\n\\n```java\\nFunction\u003cT, R\u003e\\nConsumer\u003cT\u003e\\nPredicate\u003cT\u003e\\nSupplier\u003cT\u003e\\n```\\n\\nand lambdas:\\n\\n```java\\nx -\u003e x * 2\\n```\\n\\nBut JavaScript doesn't require you to wrap a function in a functional interface.\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\nFunction\u003cInteger, Integer\u003e square = x -\u003e x * x;\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst square = x =\u003e x * x;\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.2 Callback Functions\\n\\nA callback is simply:\\n\\n\u003e A function passed to another function so that it can be called later or at an appropriate point.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction processUser(name, callback) {\\n    console.log(\\\"Processing \\\" + name);\\n\\n    callback();\\n}\\n\\nprocessUser(\\\"Dev\\\", () =\u003e {\\n    console.log(\\\"Done\\\");\\n});\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nProcessing Dev\\nDone\\n```\\n\\n### Java equivalent\\n\\n```java\\nvoid processUser(String name, Runnable callback) {\\n    System.out.println(\\\"Processing \\\" + name);\\n    callback.run();\\n}\\n```\\n\\nSo this is a concept Java developers can understand easily.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.3 Higher-Order Functions\\n\\nA **higher-order function** is a function that:\\n\\n1. accepts another function as an argument, **or**\\n2. returns a function.\\n\\nExample 1:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction applyOperation(a, b, operation) {\\n    return operation(a, b);\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(\\n    applyOperation(10, 5, (x, y) =\u003e x + y)\\n);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n15\\n```\\n\\nExample 2:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction multiplier(n) {\\n    return function (x) {\\n        return x * n;\\n    };\\n}\\n\\nconst double = multiplier(2);\\n\\nconsole.log(double(10));\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n20\\n```\\n\\nThis is a higher-order function.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.4 Why `map()` Is a Higher-Order Function\\n\\nConsider:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3];\\n\\nconst result = numbers.map(x =\u003e x * 2);\\n```\\n\\n`map()` accepts:\\n\\n```javascript\\nx =\u003e x * 2\\n```\\n\\nwhich is a function.\\n\\nTherefore:\\n\\n\u003e `map()` is a higher-order function.\\n\\nSame idea:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfilter()\\nfind()\\nsome()\\nevery()\\nreduce()\\nforEach()\\n```\\n\\nall accept callback functions.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.5 Function Returning Function\\n\\nThis is extremely important for understanding **currying, closures, middleware, decorators, and functional patterns**.\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction greet(greeting) {\\n    return function (name) {\\n        return `${greeting}, ${name}`;\\n    };\\n}\\n\\nconst hello = greet(\\\"Hello\\\");\\n\\nconsole.log(hello(\\\"Dev\\\"));\\nconsole.log(hello(\\\"Alex\\\"));\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nHello, Dev\\nHello, Alex\\n```\\n\\nWhat's happening?\\n\\n```text\\ngreet(\\\"Hello\\\")\\n       ↓\\nreturns function\\n       ↓\\nhello\\n       ↓\\nhello(\\\"Dev\\\")\\n```\\n\\nAnd the returned function retains access to:\\n\\n```javascript\\ngreeting\\n```\\n\\nThat's a combination of:\\n\\n\u003e **Higher-order function + closure**\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.6 Currying\\n\\nNow we reach an important interview concept.\\n\\nSuppose you have:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction add(a, b, c) {\\n    return a + b + c;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNormal call:\\n\\n```javascript\\nadd(10, 20, 30);\\n```\\n\\nCurrying transforms it conceptually into:\\n\\n```javascript\\nadd(10)(20)(30);\\n```\\n\\nImplementation:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction add(a) {\\n    return function (b) {\\n        return function (c) {\\n            return a + b + c;\\n        };\\n    };\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(add(10)(20)(30));\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n60\\n```\\n\\n### Interview definition\\n\\n\u003e Currying transforms a function that takes multiple arguments into a sequence of functions, each taking one argument.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.7 Currying with Arrow Functions\\n\\nSame thing, shorter:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst add = a =\u003e b =\u003e c =\u003e a + b + c;\\n\\nconsole.log(add(10)(20)(30));\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n60\\n```\\n\\nDon't let the syntax confuse you.\\n\\nRead it from outside:\\n\\n```text\\nadd(10)\\n ↓\\nfunction waiting for b\\n ↓\\n(20)\\n ↓\\nfunction waiting for c\\n ↓\\n(30)\\n ↓\\n60\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.8 Currying vs Normal Function\\n\\nNormal:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction add(a, b, c) {\\n    return a + b + c;\\n}\\n\\nadd(1, 2, 3);\\n```\\n\\nCurried:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst add = a =\u003e b =\u003e c =\u003e a + b + c;\\n\\nadd(1)(2)(3);\\n```\\n\\n### Java comparison\\n\\nJava doesn't have built-in currying syntax, but you can achieve it with functions:\\n\\n```java\\nFunction\u003cInteger,\\n    Function\u003cInteger,\\n        Function\u003cInteger, Integer\u003e\u003e\u003e add;\\n```\\n\\nObviously much uglier.\\n\\nJavaScript's function-returning-function syntax makes this pattern much more natural.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.9 Partial Application\\n\\nCurrying and partial application are related but **not identical**.\\n\\nSuppose:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction multiply(a, b, c) {\\n    return a * b * c;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nWe want to fix `a = 2`.\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction multiplyByTwo(b, c) {\\n    return multiply(2, b, c);\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNow:\\n\\n```javascript\\nmultiplyByTwo(3, 4);\\n```\\n\\nreturns:\\n\\n```text\\n24\\n```\\n\\nWe've created a specialized function by **pre-filling some arguments**.\\n\\nThat's partial application.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.10 Currying vs Partial Application\\n\\nInterview question:\\n\\n### Currying\\n\\nTransforms:\\n\\n```text\\nf(a, b, c)\\n```\\n\\ninto:\\n\\n```text\\nf(a)(b)(c)\\n```\\n\\n### Partial application\\n\\nCreates a new function with some arguments already supplied:\\n\\n```text\\nf(a, b, c)\\n       ↓\\ng(b, c)\\n```\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n\u003e **Currying changes the function's argument structure.**\\n\\n\u003e **Partial application fixes some arguments and produces another function.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.11 `bind()` and Partial Application\\n\\nYou already learned:\\n\\n```javascript\\nbind()\\n```\\n\\nIt can also partially apply arguments.\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction multiply(a, b) {\\n    return a * b;\\n}\\n\\nconst double = multiply.bind(null, 2);\\n\\nconsole.log(double(5));\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n10\\n```\\n\\nHere:\\n\\n```javascript\\nmultiply.bind(null, 2)\\n```\\n\\npre-fills:\\n\\n```text\\na = 2\\n```\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```javascript\\ndouble(5)\\n```\\n\\nsupplies:\\n\\n```text\\nb = 5\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.12 Function Composition\\n\\nSuppose:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction double(x) {\\n    return x * 2;\\n}\\n\\nfunction square(x) {\\n    return x * x;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nYou can do:\\n\\n```javascript\\nsquare(double(5));\\n```\\n\\nResult:\\n\\n```text\\n100\\n```\\n\\nbecause:\\n\\n```text\\n5\\n ↓\\ndouble\\n ↓\\n10\\n ↓\\nsquare\\n ↓\\n100\\n```\\n\\nThis is **function composition**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.13 Creating a `compose()` Function\\n\\nWe can make:\\n\\n```javascript\\ncompose(square, double)(5);\\n```\\n\\nImplementation:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction compose(f, g) {\\n    return function (x) {\\n        return f(g(x));\\n    };\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst squareAfterDouble = compose(square, double);\\n\\nconsole.log(squareAfterDouble(5));\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n100\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.14 `pipe()` vs `compose()`\\n\\nThis is a common functional-programming distinction.\\n\\n### Compose\\n\\nUsually applies functions from **right to left**:\\n\\n```javascript\\ncompose(square, double)(5);\\n```\\n\\nmeans:\\n\\n```text\\n5\\n→ double\\n→ square\\n```\\n\\n### Pipe\\n\\nUsually applies from **left to right**:\\n\\n```text\\n5\\n→ double\\n→ square\\n```\\n\\nThe naming conventions can vary across libraries, so in interviews say:\\n\\n\u003e `compose` conventionally works right-to-left, while `pipe` conventionally works left-to-right.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.15 Rest Parameters\\n\\nYou learned rest earlier, but now let's use it functionally.\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction sum(...numbers) {\\n    return numbers.reduce((total, n) =\u003e total + n, 0);\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(sum(1, 2, 3, 4));\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n10\\n```\\n\\n`...numbers` collects arguments into an array.\\n\\nConceptually:\\n\\n```text\\nsum(1,2,3,4)\\n       ↓\\nnumbers = [1,2,3,4]\\n```\\n\\n### Java comparison\\n\\nJava uses:\\n\\n```java\\nvoid sum(int... numbers)\\n```\\n\\nThis is quite similar.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.16 Spread vs Rest — Don't Mix Them Up\\n\\nSame syntax:\\n\\n```javascript\\n...\\n```\\n\\nBut different purpose.\\n\\n### Rest\\n\\nCollects:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction test(...args) {}\\n```\\n\\n```text\\nmany → one array\\n```\\n\\n### Spread\\n\\nExpands:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst arr = [1, 2, 3];\\n\\nconsole.log(...arr);\\n```\\n\\n```text\\none array → many values\\n```\\n\\nInterview answer:\\n\\n\u003e Rest collects multiple values into a single array-like parameter, while spread expands an iterable or object into individual elements/properties depending on context.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.17 Memoization\\n\\nMemoization is an important optimization technique.\\n\\nSuppose:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction square(n) {\\n    console.log(\\\"Calculating...\\\");\\n    return n * n;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nCalling:\\n\\n```javascript\\nsquare(5);\\nsquare(5);\\nsquare(5);\\n```\\n\\nrecalculates every time.\\n\\nMemoization stores previous results.\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction memoize(fn) {\\n    const cache = new Map();\\n\\n    return function (value) {\\n        if (cache.has(value)) {\\n            return cache.get(value);\\n        }\\n\\n        const result = fn(value);\\n\\n        cache.set(value, result);\\n\\n        return result;\\n    };\\n}\\n```\\n\\nUse:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst square = memoize(n =\u003e n * n);\\n\\nconsole.log(square(5));\\nconsole.log(square(5));\\n```\\n\\nThe second call retrieves the cached result.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.18 Memoization Uses Closures\\n\\nNotice:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction memoize(fn) {\\n    const cache = new Map();\\n\\n    return function (value) {\\n        ...\\n    };\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe returned function can still access:\\n\\n```javascript\\ncache\\n```\\n\\nafter `memoize()` has finished.\\n\\nWhy?\\n\\n**Closure.**\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```text\\nHigher-order function\\n        +\\nClosure\\n        +\\nMap\\n        ↓\\nMemoization\\n```\\n\\nThis is why understanding Phase 7 was important.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.19 Pure Functions\\n\\nA pure function has two important properties:\\n\\n### 1. Same input → same output\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### 2. No side effects\\n\\nIt doesn't modify external state.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nis pure.\\n\\nBut:\\n\\n```javascript\\nlet total = 0;\\n\\nfunction addToTotal(x) {\\n    total += x;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nis not pure because it changes external state.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.20 Side Effects\\n\\nA side effect is when a function does something beyond simply calculating and returning a value.\\n\\nExamples:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconsole.log()\\n```\\n\\n```javascript\\ndocument.body.innerHTML = ...\\n```\\n\\n```javascript\\nfetch(...)\\n```\\n\\n```javascript\\ndatabase.save(...)\\n```\\n\\n```javascript\\narray.push(...)\\n```\\n\\ndepending on what state is being modified.\\n\\nPure functions are easier to:\\n\\n- test\\n- reason about\\n- reuse\\n- debug\\n- compose\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.21 Immutability\\n\\nFunctional JavaScript often prefers not modifying existing data.\\n\\nInstead of:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3];\\n\\nnumbers.push(4);\\n```\\n\\nyou can create:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst newNumbers = [...numbers, 4];\\n```\\n\\nOriginal:\\n\\n```text\\n[1,2,3]\\n```\\n\\nNew:\\n\\n```text\\n[1,2,3,4]\\n```\\n\\nThis is particularly important in frameworks such as React.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.22 But Be Careful: Spread Is Shallow\\n\\nYou already learned this.\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst user = {\\n    name: \\\"Dev\\\",\\n    address: {\\n        city: \\\"Chennai\\\"\\n    }\\n};\\n\\nconst copy = { ...user };\\n```\\n\\nThe outer object is copied, but:\\n\\n```javascript\\ncopy.address\\n```\\n\\nand:\\n\\n```javascript\\nuser.address\\n```\\n\\nrefer to the same nested object.\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```javascript\\ncopy.address.city = \\\"Delhi\\\";\\n```\\n\\ncan affect:\\n\\n```javascript\\nuser.address.city\\n```\\n\\nThis is a **very common interview trap**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.23 Iterators\\n\\nNow we're moving into a more advanced JavaScript feature.\\n\\nAn **iterator** is an object that provides a sequence of values.\\n\\nIt follows the iterator protocol.\\n\\nAn iterator has a:\\n\\n```javascript\\nnext()\\n```\\n\\nmethod.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst iterator = {\\n    next() {\\n        return {\\n            value: 10,\\n            done: false\\n        };\\n    }\\n};\\n```\\n\\nA proper iterator eventually returns:\\n\\n```javascript\\n{\\n    value: ...,\\n    done: true\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.24 Iterables\\n\\nAn **iterable** is something that can be iterated using:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfor...of\\n```\\n\\nExamples:\\n\\n```javascript\\nArray\\nString\\nMap\\nSet\\n```\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfor (const x of [10, 20, 30]) {\\n    console.log(x);\\n}\\n```\\n\\nArrays are iterable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.25 Iterable vs Iterator\\n\\nVery common interview question.\\n\\n### Iterable\\n\\nHas:\\n\\n```javascript\\n[Symbol.iterator]()\\n```\\n\\nwhich returns an iterator.\\n\\n### Iterator\\n\\nHas:\\n\\n```javascript\\nnext()\\n```\\n\\nwhich returns:\\n\\n```javascript\\n{\\n    value,\\n    done\\n}\\n```\\n\\nMental model:\\n\\n```text\\nIterable\\n   ↓\\nSymbol.iterator()\\n   ↓\\nIterator\\n   ↓\\nnext()\\n   ↓\\n{ value, done }\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.26 Generators\\n\\nGenerators make creating iterators much easier.\\n\\nSyntax:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction* numbers() {\\n    yield 1;\\n    yield 2;\\n    yield 3;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst gen = numbers();\\n```\\n\\nCalling:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconsole.log(gen.next());\\n```\\n\\ngives approximately:\\n\\n```javascript\\n{\\n    value: 1,\\n    done: false\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNext:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconsole.log(gen.next());\\n```\\n\\ngives:\\n\\n```javascript\\n{\\n    value: 2,\\n    done: false\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNext:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconsole.log(gen.next());\\n```\\n\\ngives:\\n\\n```javascript\\n{\\n    value: 3,\\n    done: false\\n}\\n```\\n\\nFinally:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconsole.log(gen.next());\\n```\\n\\ngives:\\n\\n```javascript\\n{\\n    value: undefined,\\n    done: true\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.27 `yield`\\n\\nThe key concept:\\n\\n```javascript\\nyield\\n```\\n\\n**pauses the generator.**\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction* test() {\\n    console.log(\\\"A\\\");\\n\\n    yield 1;\\n\\n    console.log(\\\"B\\\");\\n\\n    yield 2;\\n\\n    console.log(\\\"C\\\");\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst g = test();\\n```\\n\\nNothing from the body runs yet.\\n\\nFirst:\\n\\n```javascript\\ng.next();\\n```\\n\\nOutput includes:\\n\\n```text\\nA\\n```\\n\\nand returns:\\n\\n```javascript\\n{ value: 1, done: false }\\n```\\n\\nSecond:\\n\\n```javascript\\ng.next();\\n```\\n\\ncontinues from where it paused:\\n\\n```text\\nB\\n```\\n\\nand returns:\\n\\n```javascript\\n{ value: 2, done: false }\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.28 Java Comparison\\n\\nJava has no exact equivalent syntax to JavaScript generators.\\n\\nJava has mechanisms such as:\\n\\n- `Iterator`\\n- `Iterable`\\n- Streams\\n- custom stateful iterators\\n\\nBut JavaScript generators provide a convenient language-level mechanism for producing values lazily.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.29 Why Generators Matter\\n\\nGenerators can produce values **lazily**.\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction* infiniteNumbers() {\\n    let n = 1;\\n\\n    while (true) {\\n        yield n++;\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThis doesn't immediately create an infinite array.\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst numbers = infiniteNumbers();\\n```\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```javascript\\nnumbers.next().value; // 1\\nnumbers.next().value; // 2\\nnumbers.next().value; // 3\\n```\\n\\nValues are generated when requested.\\n\\nThis is the idea of **lazy evaluation**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8.30 Phase 8 Mental Map\\n\\nYou should now connect these concepts:\\n\\n```text\\nFunctions\\n   │\\n   ├── First-class values\\n   │\\n   ├── Callbacks\\n   │\\n   ├── Higher-order functions\\n   │       │\\n   │       ├── map\\n   │       ├── filter\\n   │       ├── reduce\\n   │       └── functions returning functions\\n   │\\n   ├── Closures\\n   │\\n   ├── Currying\\n   │\\n   ├── Partial application\\n   │\\n   ├── Composition\\n   │\\n   ├── Pure functions\\n   │\\n   ├── Immutability\\n   │\\n   ├── Memoization\\n   │\\n   └── Iterators\\n           │\\n           └── Generators\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🔥 Phase 8 Interview Questions\\n\\nYou should eventually be able to answer:\\n\\n1. What is a first-class function?\\n2. What is a callback?\\n3. What is a higher-order function?\\n4. Why is `map()` a higher-order function?\\n5. What is currying?\\n6. Currying vs partial application?\\n7. What is function composition?\\n8. `compose()` vs `pipe()`?\\n9. What is memoization?\\n10. How can closures be used for memoization?\\n11. What is a pure function?\\n12. What is a side effect?\\n13. What does immutability mean?\\n14. What is an iterable?\\n15. What is an iterator?\\n16. What is `[Symbol.iterator]`?\\n17. What does `next()` return?\\n18. What is a generator?\\n19. What does `yield` do?\\n20. Why are generators useful?\\n21. How are JavaScript generators different from Java `Iterator`?\\n22. What is the difference between rest and spread?\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🧪 Phase 8 Coding Checkpoint\\n\\n### 1. Currying\\n\\nImplement:\\n\\n```javascript\\nadd(2)(3)(4)\\n```\\n\\nto return:\\n\\n```text\\n9\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. Closure + Higher-Order Function\\n\\nImplement:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst double = multiplier(2);\\n\\ndouble(5);  // 10\\ndouble(8);  // 16\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. Memoization\\n\\nImplement:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst square = memoize(n =\u003e n * n);\\n```\\n\\nsuch that repeated calls with the same input don't recalculate.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 4. Composition\\n\\nImplement:\\n\\n```javascript\\ncompose(\\n    x =\u003e x + 10,\\n    x =\u003e x * 2\\n)(5);\\n```\\n\\nExpected:\\n\\n```text\\n20\\n```\\n\\nBecause:\\n\\n```text\\n5\\n↓\\n×2\\n↓\\n10\\n↓\\n+10\\n↓\\n20\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 5. Generator\\n\\nCreate:\\n\\n```javascript\\nfunction* counter() {\\n    // ...\\n}\\n```\\n\\nso:\\n\\n```javascript\\nconst c = counter();\\n\\nc.next().value; // 1\\nc.next().value; // 2\\nc.next().value; // 3\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🗺️ Updated Roadmap\\n\\n```text\\n✅ Phase 0  Java → JS Mental Model\\n✅ Phase 1  Variables \u0026 Data Types\\n✅ Phase 1B Operators \u0026 Coercion\\n✅ Phase 2  Conditions \u0026 Loops\\n✅ Phase 3  Arrays\\n✅ Phase 4  Objects\\n✅ Phase 5  Functions\\n✅ Phase 6  this + OOP + Prototypes\\n✅ Phase 7  Execution Model\\n             ├── Call Stack\\n             ├── Execution Context\\n             ├── Scope Chain\\n             ├── Hoisting\\n             ├── TDZ\\n             ├── Closures\\n             ├── Event Loop\\n             ├── Microtasks\\n             ├── Promises\\n             └── async/await\\n\\n🔥 Phase 8  Advanced Functions\\n             ├── Higher-order functions\\n             ├── Currying\\n             ├── Partial application\\n             ├── Composition\\n             ├── Pure functions\\n             ├── Immutability\\n             ├── Memoization\\n             ├── Iterators\\n             └── Generators\\n\\n➡️ Phase 9  Asynchronous JavaScript — Deep Dive\\n             ├── Promises in depth\\n             ├── Promise chaining\\n             ├── Promise error handling\\n             ├── Promise combinators\\n             ├── async/await internals\\n             ├── fetch()\\n             ├── AbortController\\n             ├── concurrency\\n             ├── sequential vs parallel async\\n             └── advanced event-loop problems\\n```\\n\\n**Phase 9 is particularly important for interviews**, because we'll go beyond the basic `Promise`/`async-await` explanation and learn things like `Promise.all()`, `allSettled()`, `race()`, `any()`, parallel vs sequential execution, and the classic tricky output questions.\",{},{\"_197\":2127,\"_204\":2422,\"_54\":2423,\"_208\":2424,\"_210\":211,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":2425,\"_217\":218},\"74c6d3ef-e84c-4d13-9885-aef1f0101b88\",[2115],{\"_656\":657,\"_215\":2426},1789450588.259623,{\"_651\":667,\"_668\":669},{\"_233\":234,\"_240\":2141,\"_242\":2141,\"_245\":2420,\"_248\":10,\"_666\":34},{},{\"_197\":2420,\"_204\":2430,\"_54\":2431,\"_208\":2432,\"_210\":211,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":2433,\"_217\":218},\"6e8aad49-4621-4f1f-8b1b-7b633141c9fe\",[2127],{\"_656\":681,\"_215\":2436},1789450587.8412375,{\"_651\":652,\"_653\":2435},{\"_678\":34,\"_246\":2434,\"_228\":67,\"_233\":234,\"_236\":2140,\"_238\":239,\"_240\":2141,\"_242\":2141,\"_243\":67,\"_66\":67,\"_245\":2428,\"_248\":10,\"_244\":14},\"154506f8-b2a5-4bd1-9614-608873ea5fe2\",[669],{},{\"_197\":2428,\"_204\":2440,\"_54\":2441,\"_208\":2442,\"_210\":211,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":2443,\"_217\":218},\"bfb1d5ed-66c7-4866-a75d-72819d489d0d\",[2420],{\"_656\":681,\"_215\":2445},1789450587.840805,{\"_651\":652,\"_653\":2444},{\"_678\":34,\"_246\":2434,\"_228\":67,\"_233\":234,\"_236\":2140,\"_238\":239,\"_240\":2141,\"_242\":2141,\"_243\":67,\"_66\":67,\"_245\":2438,\"_248\":