0\",\"71gqr5\",\"mmfbmt\",\"5q3kof\",\"n5deuo\",\"u6aqze\",\"2k6hyf\",\"5helr7\",\"nxsh8m\",\"rr9l6s\",\"4mwhz2\",\"nprm7g\",\"arpwhm\",\"4yahj7\",\"ukzf40\",\"m54hza\",\"0jytzg\",\"7dt3a6\",\"1p2ycg\",\"2v4wj0\",\"gkbfdc\",\"i50u51\",\"akz2s7\",\"ymdvsa\",\"du6vo8\",\"54my55\",\"r9l3dn\",\"ytviiy\",\"n3r96u\",\"62ulj3\",\"dq9o7i\",\"6qh6qr\",\"vl985d\",\"jy176p\",\"j7qrsr\",\"riqvup\",\"e5d7sn\",\"zejy1r\",\"fn28f3\",\"tew0gj\",\"3yku4i\",\"yp1ksq\",\"74tf4s\",\"hejwi5\",\"v0ia7q\",\"wnlkjo\",\"aebzmu\",\"zyv51c\",\"tefmgg\",\"yw2d4y\",\"okywy7\",\"8l6453\",\"4gocfv\",\"pxvsbg\",\"ykdpmo\",\"6wfqrb\",\"h2h6af\",\"cmgxwa\",\"0gqk1r\",\"re7tei\",\"xo3ip4\",\"951xbn\",\"rkcovt\",\"u6ylud\",\"2ddd0w\",\"dsz0i0\",\"lwm99h\",\"mmb15n\",\"5znhiv\",\"tqeoly\",\"rmfwv7\",\"rcmjlp\",\"ainn27\",\"c04aee\",\"qf9g8o\",\"x0nshy\",\"76e5oc\",\"s1bv9t\",\"x2ybmx\",\"1g34op\",\"ka214a\",\"g1jjzg\",\"oumq1g\",\"69cwbw\",\"gqmsfv\",\"1olarb\",\"k0qnar\",\"sgliuo\",\"yswuxo\",\"uqw87e\",\"1fxdr4\",\"ml0eq6\",\"t959i7\",\"ysyp0y\",\"bk82py\",\"h438so\",\"f4raya\",\"t8yh5b\",\"h8wzpe\",\"q0oovi\",\"mit4n2\",\"kcfidc\",\"f29kw7\",\"7np7lc\",\"qbz2yi\",\"frn00g\",\"pfsshd\",\"vyb8kh\",\"c2vmrn\",\"195zcr\",\"ti2jlx\",\"7m9w0x\",\"hqeaj8\",\"xbznlz\",\"7kwjm8\",\"242p0b\",\"20bz7k\",\"hmek3b\",\"ihlyhs\",\"iqh682\",\"s3bmkq\",\"1qr2b9\",\"q7tht8\",\"dljvsr\",\"lehk09\",\"2yn42w\",\"h65738\",\"bwxooj\",\"yu2a40\",\"o0q2ao\",\"w4xpts\",\"8ynb5i\",\"umlik2\",\"f99k23\",\"rpqe1k\",\"52jcj9\",\"6mf27u\",\"rzb21w\",\"s20mt5\",[650],[3159],\"# Phase 5 — Functions ⭐⭐⭐\\n\\nThis is one of the **most important phases in JavaScript**.\\n\\nYou already know Java methods, so we'll use that as the starting point. But JavaScript takes the concept much further because **functions are first-class values**.\\n\\nBy the end of this phase, you should be able to understand things like:\\n\\n```js\\nusers.filter(user =\u003e user.age \u003e 18)\\n```\\n\\nwithout thinking \\\"what is this weird syntax?\\\"\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.1 Java method → JavaScript function\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\nint add(int a, int b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nCall:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(add(10, 20));\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n30\\n```\\n\\nSo the basic mapping is:\\n\\n```text\\nJava method\\n      ↓\\nJavaScript function\\n```\\n\\nBut there is a huge difference:\\n\\n\u003e **A JavaScript function can itself be stored in a variable, passed as an argument, returned from another function, and stored inside objects/arrays.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.2 Function declaration\\n\\nThis is what we just used:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```js\\nadd(10, 20);\\n```\\n\\nThis is called a **function declaration**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.3 Parameters vs arguments\\n\\nSame terminology as Java.\\n\\n```js\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\n```\\n\\n`a` and `b` are **parameters**.\\n\\nWhen you call:\\n\\n```js\\nadd(10, 20);\\n```\\n\\n`10` and `20` are **arguments**.\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```text\\nfunction add(a, b)\\n            ↑  ↑\\n        parameters\\n\\nadd(10, 20)\\n    ↑   ↑\\n arguments\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.4 Return values\\n\\nExactly like Java:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction square(x) {\\n    return x * x;\\n}\\n\\nconst result = square(5);\\n\\nconsole.log(result);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n25\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.5 JavaScript doesn't require parameter types\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\nint add(int a, int b)\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction add(a, b)\\n```\\n\\nTherefore this is valid:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(add(10, 20));\\nconsole.log(add(\\\"Hello\\\", \\\"World\\\"));\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n30\\nHelloWorld\\n```\\n\\nBecause JavaScript is dynamically typed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.6 JavaScript functions can receive different numbers of arguments\\n\\nConsider:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(add(10));\\n```\\n\\nWhat happens?\\n\\n`a = 10`\\n\\n`b = undefined`\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```text\\n10 + undefined\\n```\\n\\nresults in:\\n\\n```text\\nNaN\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\nWhat about extra arguments?\\n\\n```js\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(add(10, 20, 30));\\n```\\n\\nThe first two arguments are assigned:\\n\\n```text\\na = 10\\nb = 20\\n```\\n\\nThe extra `30` doesn't cause a compile-time error.\\n\\nWe'll see how to access extra arguments using **rest parameters**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.7 Default parameters ⭐\\n\\nJava has method overloading and defaults aren't directly built into method parameters.\\n\\nJavaScript provides default parameters:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction greet(name = \\\"Guest\\\") {\\n    console.log(`Hello ${name}`);\\n}\\n\\ngreet(\\\"Dev\\\");\\ngreet();\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nHello Dev\\nHello Guest\\n```\\n\\nIf no argument is supplied:\\n\\n```text\\nname = \\\"Guest\\\"\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.8 Default parameter with `undefined`\\n\\nInteresting:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction greet(name = \\\"Guest\\\") {\\n    console.log(name);\\n}\\n\\ngreet(undefined);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nGuest\\n```\\n\\nBut:\\n\\n```js\\ngreet(null);\\n```\\n\\noutputs:\\n\\n```text\\nnull\\n```\\n\\nThe default applies when the value is `undefined`, not generally when it's falsy or null.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.9 Function expression\\n\\nJava programmers usually learn:\\n\\n```java\\nint add(int a, int b) {\\n}\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript also allows:\\n\\n```js\\nconst add = function(a, b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n};\\n```\\n\\nHere:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction(a, b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nis a function value assigned to:\\n\\n```js\\nconst add\\n```\\n\\nThis is a **function expression**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.10 Why is this important?\\n\\nBecause now:\\n\\n```js\\nconst add = function(a, b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n};\\n```\\n\\nmeans `add` is holding a function.\\n\\nYou can do:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(add(2, 3));\\n```\\n\\nJust like before.\\n\\nBut now we can do something much more interesting.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.11 Functions can be stored in arrays\\n\\n```js\\nconst operations = [\\n    function(a, b) {\\n        return a + b;\\n    },\\n\\n    function(a, b) {\\n        return a - b;\\n    }\\n];\\n```\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(operations[0](10, 5));\\nconsole.log(operations[1](10, 5));\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n15\\n5\\n```\\n\\nThat's a major mindset change.\\n\\nA function is a **value**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.12 Functions can be passed to functions ⭐⭐⭐\\n\\nConsider:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\n\\nfunction execute(operation) {\\n    return operation(10, 20);\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(execute(add));\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n30\\n```\\n\\nWhat's happening?\\n\\nWe pass:\\n\\n```js\\nadd\\n```\\n\\ninto:\\n\\n```js\\nexecute()\\n```\\n\\nInside:\\n\\n```js\\noperation(10, 20)\\n```\\n\\ncalls the function.\\n\\nThis is possible because functions are **first-class citizens**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.13 Java comparison — functional interfaces\\n\\nThis should feel somewhat familiar because of Java lambdas.\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\ninterface Operation {\\n    int execute(int a, int b);\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```java\\nOperation add = (a, b) -\u003e a + b;\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript doesn't require you to define a functional interface first.\\n\\nYou can simply do:\\n\\n```js\\nconst add = (a, b) =\u003e a + b;\\n```\\n\\nand pass it around directly.\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```text\\nJava\\nFunctional interface\\n      ↓\\nLambda\\n\\nJavaScript\\nFunction value\\n      ↓\\nArrow function / function\\n```\\n\\nConceptually related, but JavaScript is much more flexible.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.14 Arrow functions ⭐⭐⭐\\n\\nNow the syntax you'll see everywhere.\\n\\nNormal function:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nArrow:\\n\\n```js\\nconst add = (a, b) =\u003e {\\n    return a + b;\\n};\\n```\\n\\nAnd because this function only returns one expression:\\n\\n```js\\nconst add = (a, b) =\u003e a + b;\\n```\\n\\nThat's extremely common.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.15 Arrow function syntax\\n\\nBasic:\\n\\n```js\\nconst square = (x) =\u003e {\\n    return x * x;\\n};\\n```\\n\\nCan shorten:\\n\\n```js\\nconst square = x =\u003e x * x;\\n```\\n\\nFor one parameter, parentheses are optional.\\n\\nMultiple parameters:\\n\\n```js\\nconst add = (a, b) =\u003e a + b;\\n```\\n\\nNo parameters:\\n\\n```js\\nconst greet = () =\u003e console.log(\\\"Hello\\\");\\n```\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```text\\n0 parameters → ()\\n1 parameter  → x or (x)\\n2+           → (a, b)\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.16 Implicit return\\n\\nCompare:\\n\\n```js\\nconst square = (x) =\u003e {\\n    return x * x;\\n};\\n```\\n\\nwith:\\n\\n```js\\nconst square = x =\u003e x * x;\\n```\\n\\nThe second has an **implicit return**.\\n\\nMeaning:\\n\\n```js\\nx =\u003e x * x\\n```\\n\\nis approximately:\\n\\n```js\\nx =\u003e {\\n    return x * x;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nfor this simple expression form.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.17 ⚠️ Arrow function braces trap\\n\\nThis:\\n\\n```js\\nconst square = x =\u003e x * x;\\n```\\n\\nreturns the result.\\n\\nBut:\\n\\n```js\\nconst square = x =\u003e {\\n    x * x;\\n};\\n```\\n\\nreturns:\\n\\n```text\\nundefined\\n```\\n\\nbecause once you use `{}`, you need explicit:\\n\\n```js\\nreturn\\n```\\n\\nif you want to return something.\\n\\nCorrect:\\n\\n```js\\nconst square = x =\u003e {\\n    return x * x;\\n};\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.18 Returning an object from an arrow function ⚠️\\n\\nThis looks like it should return an object:\\n\\n```js\\nconst createUser = name =\u003e {\\n    name: name\\n};\\n```\\n\\nBut it doesn't.\\n\\nBecause `{}` is interpreted as the function body.\\n\\nCorrect:\\n\\n```js\\nconst createUser = name =\u003e ({\\n    name: name\\n});\\n```\\n\\nOr with property shorthand:\\n\\n```js\\nconst createUser = name =\u003e ({\\n    name\\n});\\n```\\n\\nThis becomes very common in React.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.19 Callback functions ⭐⭐⭐\\n\\nNow we're going to connect this with `forEach`, `map`, and `filter`.\\n\\nA **callback** is simply:\\n\\n\u003e A function passed to another function to be called later/by that function.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction greet(name) {\\n    console.log(`Hello ${name}`);\\n}\\n\\nfunction processUser(callback) {\\n    callback(\\\"Dev\\\");\\n}\\n\\nprocessUser(greet);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nHello Dev\\n```\\n\\nHere:\\n\\n```text\\ngreet\\n ↓\\npassed into processUser\\n ↓\\ncallback\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.20 Anonymous callback\\n\\nYou don't need to create a named function.\\n\\n```js\\nfunction processUser(callback) {\\n    callback(\\\"Dev\\\");\\n}\\n\\nprocessUser(function(name) {\\n    console.log(`Hello ${name}`);\\n});\\n```\\n\\nOr arrow:\\n\\n```js\\nprocessUser(name =\u003e {\\n    console.log(`Hello ${name}`);\\n});\\n```\\n\\nThis is exactly the style you've already seen:\\n\\n```js\\nnumbers.forEach(number =\u003e console.log(number));\\n```\\n\\nThe arrow function is the callback.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.21 `map()` suddenly makes sense\\n\\nEarlier:\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3];\\n\\nconst result = numbers.map(x =\u003e x * 2);\\n```\\n\\nNow understand the architecture:\\n\\n```text\\nnumbers\\n   ↓\\nmap()\\n   ↓\\ncallback function\\n   ↓\\nx =\u003e x * 2\\n   ↓\\nrun callback for each element\\n   ↓\\nnew array\\n```\\n\\nConceptually:\\n\\n```js\\nmap(callback)\\n```\\n\\nis doing something like:\\n\\n```js\\nfor each element:\\n    call callback(element)\\n    put returned value into new array\\n```\\n\\nYou don't need to implement `map()` yourself yet, but understanding this is crucial.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.22 Higher-order functions\\n\\nA function is called a **higher-order function** if it:\\n\\n1. takes a function as an argument, **or**\\n2. returns a function.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction execute(operation) {\\n    return operation(10, 20);\\n}\\n```\\n\\n`execute` is a higher-order function because it receives a function.\\n\\nBuilt-in examples:\\n\\n```js\\nmap()\\nfilter()\\nforEach()\\nreduce()\\nsetTimeout()\\n```\\n\\nall work with callback functions.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.23 Function returning a function ⭐\\n\\nThis is where JavaScript becomes really powerful.\\n\\n```js\\nfunction multiplier(x) {\\n    return function(y) {\\n        return x * y;\\n    };\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNow:\\n\\n```js\\nconst double = multiplier(2);\\n\\nconsole.log(double(5));\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n10\\n```\\n\\nLet's understand it.\\n\\nFirst:\\n\\n```js\\nmultiplier(2)\\n```\\n\\nreturns:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction(y) {\\n    return 2 * y;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```js\\ndouble\\n```\\n\\nis now a function.\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```js\\ndouble(5)\\n```\\n\\n→ `10`.\\n\\nThis leads directly to **closures**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.24 Closures ⭐⭐⭐\\n\\nThis is an extremely important JavaScript interview topic.\\n\\nConsider:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction multiplier(x) {\\n    return function(y) {\\n        return x * y;\\n    };\\n}\\n```\\n\\nWhen:\\n\\n```js\\nconst double = multiplier(2);\\n```\\n\\nthe returned function still has access to:\\n\\n```text\\nx = 2\\n```\\n\\neven though `multiplier()` has already finished executing.\\n\\nThat's a **closure**.\\n\\n### Interview-quality definition\\n\\n\u003e A closure is a function together with access to the lexical environment in which it was created, allowing it to retain access to variables from its outer scope even after that outer function has finished executing.\\n\\nThat's the definition you should eventually be comfortable giving.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.25 Closure example\\n\\n```js\\nfunction counter() {\\n    let count = 0;\\n\\n    return function() {\\n        count++;\\n        return count;\\n    };\\n}\\n\\nconst c = counter();\\n\\nconsole.log(c());\\nconsole.log(c());\\nconsole.log(c());\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n1\\n2\\n3\\n```\\n\\nWhere is `count`?\\n\\nIt's inside:\\n\\n```js\\ncounter()\\n```\\n\\nYet the returned function can still access it.\\n\\nThat's closure behavior.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.26 Why closures are useful\\n\\nClosures can provide:\\n\\n### Private-like state\\n\\n```js\\nfunction counter() {\\n    let count = 0;\\n\\n    return {\\n        increment() {\\n            count++;\\n        },\\n\\n        getCount() {\\n            return count;\\n        }\\n    };\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNow:\\n\\n```js\\nconst c = counter();\\n\\nc.increment();\\nc.increment();\\n\\nconsole.log(c.getCount());\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\n2\\n```\\n\\nBut there's no direct:\\n\\n```js\\nc.count\\n```\\n\\nthat exposes the internal variable.\\n\\nThis pattern is historically important in JavaScript.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.27 Scope\\n\\nNow let's understand why closures work.\\n\\nJava has:\\n\\n```java\\n{\\n    int x = 10;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nand `x` is local to the block.\\n\\nJavaScript has several important scopes:\\n\\n```text\\nGlobal scope\\nFunction scope\\nBlock scope\\nModule scope\\n```\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```js\\nlet global = 10;\\n\\nfunction test() {\\n    let local = 20;\\n\\n    if (true) {\\n        let block = 30;\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nEach variable has a scope.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.28 Lexical scope\\n\\nConsider:\\n\\n```js\\nlet x = 10;\\n\\nfunction test() {\\n    console.log(x);\\n}\\n\\ntest();\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n10\\n```\\n\\nWhy can `test()` see `x`?\\n\\nBecause the function was **defined in a scope where `x` exists**.\\n\\nJavaScript uses **lexical scoping**.\\n\\nThink:\\n\\n\u003e Where the function is written determines what outer variables it can access.\\n\\nNot where the function is called.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.29 Scope chain\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```js\\nconst a = 10;\\n\\nfunction outer() {\\n    const b = 20;\\n\\n    function inner() {\\n        const c = 30;\\n\\n        console.log(a);\\n        console.log(b);\\n        console.log(c);\\n    }\\n\\n    inner();\\n}\\n```\\n\\n`inner()` can access:\\n\\n```text\\nc → its own scope\\nb → outer scope\\na → global scope\\n```\\n\\nThis search is essentially the **scope chain**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.30 Hoisting ⭐\\n\\nNow something very important.\\n\\nConsider:\\n\\n```js\\nsayHello();\\n\\nfunction sayHello() {\\n    console.log(\\\"Hello\\\");\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThis works.\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nHello\\n```\\n\\nWhy?\\n\\nFunction declarations are hoisted.\\n\\nYou can conceptually think of JavaScript as making the function declaration available before execution reaches that line.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.31 `let` and `const` hoisting\\n\\nThis is more subtle.\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(x);\\n\\nlet x = 10;\\n```\\n\\nThis produces:\\n\\n```text\\nReferenceError\\n```\\n\\nSimilarly:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(x);\\n\\nconst x = 10;\\n```\\n\\n→ `ReferenceError`.\\n\\nThey are technically hoisted in the sense that their bindings are established during environment setup, but they cannot be accessed before initialization.\\n\\nThis period is called the:\\n\\n\u003e **Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ)**\\n\\nWe'll revisit this in the advanced JavaScript phase.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.32 `var` behaves differently\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(x);\\n\\nvar x = 10;\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nundefined\\n```\\n\\nThis is one of the reasons `var` can cause confusing behavior.\\n\\nConceptually:\\n\\n```js\\nvar x;\\nconsole.log(x);\\nx = 10;\\n```\\n\\nThe declaration is hoisted and initialized to `undefined`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.33 Function declaration vs function expression\\n\\nCompare:\\n\\n```js\\nsayHello();\\n\\nfunction sayHello() {\\n    console.log(\\\"Hello\\\");\\n}\\n```\\n\\nWorks.\\n\\nBut:\\n\\n```js\\nsayHello();\\n\\nconst sayHello = function() {\\n    console.log(\\\"Hello\\\");\\n};\\n```\\n\\n→ `ReferenceError`.\\n\\nWhy?\\n\\nBecause `sayHello` is a `const` binding and is in the TDZ until initialization.\\n\\nThis is a classic interview question.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.34 IIFE\\n\\nYou may encounter:\\n\\n\u003e **Immediately Invoked Function Expression**\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```js\\n(function() {\\n    console.log(\\\"Hello\\\");\\n})();\\n```\\n\\nThe function is:\\n\\n1. created\\n2. immediately called\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nHello\\n```\\n\\nWhy was this historically used?\\n\\nBefore modern modules and block scoping, IIFEs were commonly used to create private scopes and avoid polluting global variables.\\n\\nYou don't need to use IIFEs much in modern code, but you should understand them for interviews/legacy code.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.35 Rest parameters ⭐⭐\\n\\nRemember this?\\n\\n```js\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n}\\n```\\n\\nWhat if you want any number of arguments?\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction add(...numbers) {\\n    console.log(numbers);\\n}\\n\\nadd(10, 20, 30, 40);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n[10, 20, 30, 40]\\n```\\n\\n`...numbers` collects the remaining arguments into an array.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction sum(...numbers) {\\n    return numbers.reduce((total, number) =\u003e total + number, 0);\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(sum(10, 20, 30));\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\n60\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.36 Rest vs spread\\n\\nSame `...` syntax, different jobs.\\n\\n### Spread\\n\\nExpands an existing collection:\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3];\\n\\nconsole.log(...numbers);\\n```\\n\\nConceptually:\\n\\n```text\\n1 2 3\\n```\\n\\n### Rest\\n\\nCollects multiple values into one array:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction test(...numbers) {\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThink:\\n\\n```text\\nspread → unpack\\nrest   → collect\\n```\\n\\nThis distinction is an interview favorite.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.37 Callback + array methods\\n\\nNow your previous phase should make much more sense.\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];\\n\\nconst result = numbers\\n    .filter(x =\u003e x % 2 === 0)\\n    .map(x =\u003e x * 10);\\n\\nconsole.log(result);\\n```\\n\\nFirst:\\n\\n```text\\nfilter\\n[1,2,3,4,5]\\n ↓\\n[2,4]\\n```\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```text\\nmap\\n[2,4]\\n ↓\\n[20,40]\\n```\\n\\nThe callbacks are:\\n\\n```js\\nx =\u003e x % 2 === 0\\n```\\n\\nand:\\n\\n```js\\nx =\u003e x * 10\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.38 A Java comparison\\n\\nYou know Java streams:\\n\\n```java\\nList\u003cInteger\u003e result = numbers.stream()\\n    .filter(x -\u003e x % 2 == 0)\\n    .map(x -\u003e x * 10)\\n    .toList();\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```js\\nconst result = numbers\\n    .filter(x =\u003e x % 2 === 0)\\n    .map(x =\u003e x * 10);\\n```\\n\\nThis should now look very familiar.\\n\\nConceptually:\\n\\n```text\\nJava Streams\\n     ↕\\nJS Array functional methods\\n```\\n\\nBut the underlying APIs and language semantics aren't identical.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5.39 ⚠️ One major arrow-function difference: `this`\\n\\nArrow functions do **not** have their own `this`.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```js\\nconst obj = {\\n    name: \\\"Dev\\\",\\n\\n    regular() {\\n        console.log(this.name);\\n    },\\n\\n    arrow: () =\u003e {\\n        console.log(this.name);\\n    }\\n};\\n\\nobj.regular();\\nobj.arrow();\\n```\\n\\nThe first behaves as you'd expect:\\n\\n```text\\nDev\\n```\\n\\nThe arrow function's `this` is determined lexically rather than by the object method call.\\n\\n**Don't try to memorize the exact output here yet.**\\n\\nWe'll dedicate **Phase 6 entirely to `this`, call/apply/bind, prototypes and object behavior.**\\n\\nThis is one of the most important areas for JavaScript interviews.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🧠 Phase 5 mental model\\n\\nYou should now have:\\n\\n```text\\nFunction\\n   ↓\\nfirst-class value\\n   ↓\\ncan be stored\\n   ↓\\ncan be passed\\n   ↓\\ncan be returned\\n   ↓\\ncallbacks\\n   ↓\\nhigher-order functions\\n   ↓\\nclosures\\n```\\n\\nAnd:\\n\\n```text\\nJava method\\n     ↓\\nJS function\\n\\nJava lambda\\n     ↓\\nJS arrow function\\n\\nJava functional interface\\n     ↓\\nJS function value / callback concept\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🔥 Interview questions\\n\\nYou should eventually be able to answer these naturally.\\n\\n### 1. What is a first-class function?\\n\\n\u003e A function is treated as a value, meaning it can be assigned to variables, passed as arguments, returned from functions, and stored in data structures.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. What is a callback?\\n\\n\u003e A callback is a function passed to another function so that the receiving function can invoke it at an appropriate time.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. What is a higher-order function?\\n\\n\u003e A function that takes another function as an argument, returns a function, or both.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 4. What is a closure?\\n\\n\u003e A closure is a function together with its surrounding lexical environment, allowing it to retain access to variables from its outer scope even after the outer function has completed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 5. Difference between function declaration and function expression?\\n\\n```js\\nfunction add() {}\\n```\\n\\nis a function declaration.\\n\\n```js\\nconst add = function() {};\\n```\\n\\nis a function expression.\\n\\nFunction declarations are hoisted in a way that allows them to be called before their declaration; a function expression assigned to `let`/`const` cannot be accessed before initialization.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 6. What is an arrow function?\\n\\n\u003e A concise function syntax introduced in ES6. Arrow functions also differ from regular functions in how they handle `this`, `arguments`, and constructor behavior.\\n\\nWe'll explore those differences deeply later.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 7. Rest vs spread?\\n\\n```text\\nrest   → collects\\nspread → expands\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🧪 Coding checkpoint\\n\\nDo these yourself.\\n\\n### Problem 1\\n\\nCreate a function:\\n\\n```text\\nmultiply(5, 4)\\n```\\n\\nthat returns:\\n\\n```text\\n20\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Problem 2\\n\\nRewrite it as an arrow function.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Problem 3\\n\\nCreate:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction calculate(a, b, operation)\\n```\\n\\nso that:\\n\\n```js\\ncalculate(10, 5, add)\\ncalculate(10, 5, subtract)\\ncalculate(10, 5, multiply)\\n```\\n\\nwork.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Problem 4\\n\\nWrite:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction sum(...numbers)\\n```\\n\\nthat can do:\\n\\n```js\\nsum(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)\\n```\\n\\n→ `15`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Problem 5\\n\\nCreate a function:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction counter()\\n```\\n\\nthat returns a function such that:\\n\\n```js\\nconst c = counter();\\n\\nconsole.log(c()); // 1\\nconsole.log(c()); // 2\\nconsole.log(c()); // 3\\n```\\n\\nThis tests whether you've understood **closures**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Problem 6 — Interview\\n\\nExplain the difference between:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nand:\\n\\n```js\\nconst add = (a, b) =\u003e a + b;\\n```\\n\\nDon't just say \\\"one is arrow syntax.\\\" Explain the **semantic differences you currently know**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Next Phase — `this`, Objects, Prototypes \u0026 OOP 🔥\\n\\nThis is where we'll connect your Java OOP knowledge to JavaScript:\\n\\n```text\\nthis\\nconstructor functions\\nclass\\nconstructor\\nstatic\\nextends\\nsuper\\nprototype\\nprototype chain\\n__proto__\\ninstanceof\\ngetters/setters\\nprivate fields\\ncall()\\napply()\\nbind()\\n```\\n\\nAnd most importantly, we'll answer:\\n\\n\u003e **Why does `this` in JavaScript behave differently from `this` in Java?**\\n\\nThis phase is **very important for interviews**, so we'll take it carefully rather than rushing through it.\",{},{\"_197\":2841,\"_204\":3164,\"_54\":3165,\"_56\":3166,\"_208\":3167,\"_210\":211,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":3168,\"_217\":218},\"d6b06f40-aed1-4cb3-863c-09c2234191a3\",[2829],{\"_656\":709,\"_215\":3171},1789053066.529,1789053107.670032,{\"_651\":652,\"_653\":3170},{\"_236\":2854,\"_704\":-5,\"_240\":2855,\"_242\":2855,\"_246\":3169,\"_248\":10,\"_244\":14},\"99dc83b8-637a-4377-a969-eb1d9ac15bba\",[708],{},{\"_197\":3162,\"_204\":3175,\"_54\":3176,\"_56\":3177,\"_208\":3178,\"_210\":211,\"_212\":34,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":3179,\"_217\":218,\"_219\":220},\"904eacd3-1d61-498c-aad8-dba2b84c1295\",[2841],{\"_656\":657,\"_215\":3474},1789051240.507087,17890