,{\"_197\":3691,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3690,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3689,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3688,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3687,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3686,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3685,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3684,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3683,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3682,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3681,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3680,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3679,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3678,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3677,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3676,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3675,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3674,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3673,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3672,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3671,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3670,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3669,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3668,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3667,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3666,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3665,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3664,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3663,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3662,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3661,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3660,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3659,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3658,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3657,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3656,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3655,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3654,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3653,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3652,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3651,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3650,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3649,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3648,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":3647,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},\"vagv0e\",\"gj6r7l\",\"gqa505\",\"7sf8g8\",\"4r2j29\",\"mtvdxq\",\"xzpd5o\",\"ci4bfg\",\"jllw6c\",\"y7fqmu\",\"sdnklj\",\"hh047v\",\"5o62ma\",\"rdvhx4\",\"pzwksu\",\"s3kt0o\",\"8vzw19\",\"ro17b0\",\"5r9jxv\",\"4xk0n3\",\"7cpbkz\",\"z5sgti\",\"d1dq8q\",\"0prue9\",\"rqss9s\",\"m666cx\",\"qbdcow\",\"p12usa\",\"amqr9x\",\"792izs\",\"vhc9vh\",\"k516g9\",\"gxciaz\",\"ik7rm2\",\"hggkx5\",\"hnzydn\",\"520qh2\",\"18g9ni\",\"e3vwpw\",\"4o1xy6\",\"e6a4t2\",\"pza7ms\",\"ruyt99\",\"f3glc0\",\"912u0z\",\"mnbg2x\",\"f8m1n6\",\"mgneso\",\"363gfl\",\"f128ar\",\"j9u9o3\",\"e5lfq2\",\"u92aet\",\"5dj4so\",\"ardwgf\",\"80iz2l\",\"vxl0du\",\"b3yxbb\",\"yfqobi\",\"lo3crg\",\"wyw5zs\",\"u12h72\",\"vrp7ka\",\"p1rw47\",\"y06ba2\",\"kdtwsu\",\"iut0ho\",\"toz9nb\",\"dgef2b\",\"z6p34p\",\"qs084z\",\"qbxf41\",\"nm01oi\",\"6p91l6\",\"h6b01d\",\"uf7uws\",\"hjc4hc\",\"ckd1if\",\"7mzgfe\",\"9t2wvr\",\"biffwz\",\"it5c6j\",\"p7s8z5\",\"0k5ia0\",\"852a1x\",\"m0xckp\",\"pl34wj\",\"jqyf95\",\"0oozzs\",\"4iosp0\",\"it97a9\",\"4k23qc\",\"1bcof6\",\"hu8i2z\",\"p8x6gx\",\"oy5fd0\",\"6b40qr\",\"24ia4u\",\"zqo0yd\",\"dubi4g\",\"vsfzdk\",\"f4mkg4\",\"bv6gor\",\"4yyxsx\",\"islzah\",\"n092oc\",\"lpcs9v\",\"y6a28b\",\"08loxl\",\"3c99cd\",\"bzoxr7\",\"feyhpk\",\"emtd32\",\"q6pt4l\",\"tksc91\",\"ewd3kn\",\"y19toy\",\"k2qssh\",\"tqhkai\",\"akca1o\",\"6cxean\",\"cek332\",\"fmjtwg\",\"7wncwd\",\"6hijfg\",\"wyf2py\",\"o09vlx\",\"kseipi\",\"myj883\",\"2r7820\",\"z0whrq\",\"zyzaf7\",\"dwpovq\",\"yfai8h\",\"sz0lja\",\"i95ue7\",\"9wata9\",\"kd2gkn\",\"7jlia0\",\"0pkb33\",\"kfoism\",\"k5kue4\",[650],[3791],\"# Phase 3 — Arrays in JavaScript\\n\\nNow we're getting into **very important JavaScript territory**.\\n\\nIf you know Java's:\\n\\n```java\\nint[] arr\\nArrayList\u003cInteger\u003e list\\n```\\n\\nyou have a good starting point—but **JavaScript's `Array` combines some ideas from both and has many additional capabilities.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.1 Creating an array\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\nint[] arr = {10, 20, 30};\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```js\\nconst arr = [10, 20, 30];\\n```\\n\\nAccessing:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(arr[0]); // 10\\nconsole.log(arr[1]); // 20\\nconsole.log(arr[2]); // 30\\n```\\n\\nSame zero-based indexing as Java.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.2 JavaScript arrays can contain different types\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\nint[] arr = {10, 20, 30};\\n```\\n\\nAn `int[]` can't contain a String.\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```js\\nconst arr = [10, \\\"hello\\\", true, null, { name: \\\"Dev\\\" }];\\n```\\n\\nThis is perfectly valid.\\n\\nAn array can contain values of different types.\\n\\nYou *can* do this, but in good application code, you'll usually keep arrays logically consistent.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n```js\\nconst names = [\\\"Dev\\\", \\\"Alex\\\", \\\"John\\\"];\\n```\\n\\nis much more sensible than:\\n\\n```js\\nconst data = [10, \\\"Dev\\\", true, null];\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.3 `length`\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\narr.length\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```js\\nconst arr = [10, 20, 30];\\n\\nconsole.log(arr.length);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n3\\n```\\n\\nVery similar.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.4 Adding elements — `push()`\\n\\nJava `ArrayList`:\\n\\n```java\\nlist.add(40);\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```js\\narr.push(40);\\n```\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 20, 30];\\n\\nnumbers.push(40);\\n\\nconsole.log(numbers);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n[10, 20, 30, 40]\\n```\\n\\n`push()` adds to the **end**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.5 Removing from the end — `pop()`\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 20, 30];\\n\\nconst removed = numbers.pop();\\n\\nconsole.log(removed);\\nconsole.log(numbers);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n30\\n[10, 20]\\n```\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```text\\npush() → add to end\\npop()  → remove from end\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.6 Adding to the beginning — `unshift()`\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [20, 30];\\n\\nnumbers.unshift(10);\\n\\nconsole.log(numbers);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n[10, 20, 30]\\n```\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```text\\npush()     → end\\nunshift()  → beginning\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.7 Removing from the beginning — `shift()`\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 20, 30];\\n\\nconst removed = numbers.shift();\\n\\nconsole.log(removed);\\nconsole.log(numbers);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n10\\n[20, 30]\\n```\\n\\nTherefore:\\n\\n```text\\npush()     → add end\\npop()      → remove end\\nunshift()  → add beginning\\nshift()    → remove beginning\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.8 `indexOf()`\\n\\nSimilar to searching for an element.\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 20, 30, 20];\\n\\nconsole.log(numbers.indexOf(20));\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n1\\n```\\n\\nIt returns the **first matching index**.\\n\\nIf not found:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(numbers.indexOf(99));\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n-1\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.9 `includes()`\\n\\nIf you only want to know whether something exists:\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 20, 30];\\n\\nconsole.log(numbers.includes(20));\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\ntrue\\n```\\n\\nAnd:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(numbers.includes(50));\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\nfalse\\n```\\n\\nThis is cleaner than:\\n\\n```js\\nnumbers.indexOf(20) !== -1\\n```\\n\\nwhen you don't need the index.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.10 `slice()` ⭐\\n\\nThis is important.\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50];\\n\\nconst result = numbers.slice(1, 4);\\n\\nconsole.log(result);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n[20, 30, 40]\\n```\\n\\nThe rule:\\n\\n```text\\nslice(start, end)\\n```\\n\\nincludes `start` but excludes `end`.\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```text\\nindex:   0   1   2   3   4\\nvalue:  10  20  30  40  50\\n             ↑       ↑\\n            start   end\\n```\\n\\n`slice(1,4)` → indexes `1,2,3`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.11 `slice()` does NOT modify the original array\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40];\\n\\nconst result = numbers.slice(1, 3);\\n\\nconsole.log(numbers);\\nconsole.log(result);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n[10, 20, 30, 40]\\n[20, 30]\\n```\\n\\nOriginal remains unchanged.\\n\\nThis concept of **mutating vs non-mutating methods** becomes extremely important in React.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.12 `splice()` — ⚠️ Don't confuse with `slice()`\\n\\n`splice()` **modifies the original array**.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40];\\n\\nnumbers.splice(1, 2);\\n\\nconsole.log(numbers);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n[10, 40]\\n```\\n\\nWhat happened?\\n\\n```text\\nsplice(start, deleteCount)\\n```\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```js\\nnumbers.splice(1, 2);\\n```\\n\\nmeans:\\n\\n\u003e Starting at index 1, remove 2 elements.\\n\\nIt removed:\\n\\n```text\\n20\\n30\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.13 `splice()` can also insert\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 40];\\n\\nnumbers.splice(1, 0, 20, 30);\\n\\nconsole.log(numbers);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n[10, 20, 30, 40]\\n```\\n\\nMeaning:\\n\\n```text\\nstart = 1\\ndelete = 0\\ninsert = 20, 30\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.14 `slice` vs `splice`\\n\\nThis is an interview question.\\n\\n| `slice()` | `splice()` |\\n|---|---|\\n| Doesn't modify original | Modifies original |\\n| Extracts portion | Removes/inserts/replaces |\\n| Returns new array | Returns removed elements |\\n| `slice(start,end)` | `splice(start,deleteCount,...items)` |\\n\\n### Memory trick\\n\\n\u003e **slice = take a slice**\\n\\n\u003e **splice = modify**\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.15 `concat()`\\n\\n```js\\nconst a = [1, 2];\\nconst b = [3, 4];\\n\\nconst result = a.concat(b);\\n\\nconsole.log(result);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n[1, 2, 3, 4]\\n```\\n\\nOriginal arrays remain unchanged.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.16 Spread operator `...` ⭐⭐⭐\\n\\nThis is extremely important for modern JavaScript.\\n\\n```js\\nconst a = [1, 2, 3];\\n\\nconst b = [...a];\\n\\nconsole.log(b);\\n```\\n\\nThink:\\n\\n```text\\n...a\\n↓\\nspread the elements of a\\n```\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```js\\n[...a]\\n```\\n\\ncreates a new array containing the elements.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.17 Combining arrays with spread\\n\\nInstead of:\\n\\n```js\\nconst result = a.concat(b);\\n```\\n\\nyou can write:\\n\\n```js\\nconst result = [...a, ...b];\\n```\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```js\\nconst a = [1, 2];\\nconst b = [3, 4];\\n\\nconst result = [...a, ...b];\\n\\nconsole.log(result);\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\n[1, 2, 3, 4]\\n```\\n\\nYou'll see this constantly in React.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.18 Why spread matters\\n\\nConsider:\\n\\n```js\\nconst original = [10, 20, 30];\\n\\nconst copy = [...original];\\n\\ncopy.push(40);\\n\\nconsole.log(original);\\nconsole.log(copy);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n[10, 20, 30]\\n[10, 20, 30, 40]\\n```\\n\\nThey're separate arrays.\\n\\nBut there's a **very important catch**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.19 Spread creates a shallow copy\\n\\nConsider:\\n\\n```js\\nconst original = [\\n    {\\n        name: \\\"Dev\\\"\\n    }\\n];\\n\\nconst copy = [...original];\\n\\ncopy[0].name = \\\"Alex\\\";\\n\\nconsole.log(original[0].name);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nAlex\\n```\\n\\n😈\\n\\nWhy?\\n\\nBecause:\\n\\n```js\\n[...original]\\n```\\n\\ncreates a new **array**, but the object inside is still the same object.\\n\\nConceptually:\\n\\n```text\\noriginal ───────┐\\n                ↓\\n             [ object ]\\n                ↑\\ncopy ───────────┘\\n```\\n\\nBoth arrays point to the same inner object.\\n\\nThis is called a **shallow copy**.\\n\\nWe'll study references and deep copying much more deeply in the Objects phase.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.20 `map()` ⭐⭐⭐\\n\\nNow we reach one of the most important JavaScript concepts.\\n\\nSuppose:\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4];\\n```\\n\\nYou want:\\n\\n```text\\n2\\n4\\n6\\n8\\n```\\n\\nJava-style approach:\\n\\n```java\\nfor (int i = 0; i \u003c numbers.length; i++) {\\n    numbers[i] = numbers[i] * 2;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```js\\nconst result = numbers.map(number =\u003e number * 2);\\n\\nconsole.log(result);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n[2, 4, 6, 8]\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.21 What does `map()` actually mean?\\n\\nThink:\\n\\n```text\\nmap\\n ↓\\ntake every element\\n ↓\\ntransform it\\n ↓\\nreturn a new array\\n```\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 20, 30];\\n\\nconst result = numbers.map(number =\u003e number + 5);\\n```\\n\\nTransformation:\\n\\n```text\\n10 → 15\\n20 → 25\\n30 → 35\\n```\\n\\nResult:\\n\\n```text\\n[15, 25, 35]\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.22 `map()` does NOT modify the original\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3];\\n\\nconst result = numbers.map(x =\u003e x * 10);\\n\\nconsole.log(numbers);\\nconsole.log(result);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n[1, 2, 3]\\n[10, 20, 30]\\n```\\n\\nThis is why `map()` is so useful.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.23 `filter()` ⭐⭐⭐\\n\\nSuppose:\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 15, 20, 25, 30];\\n```\\n\\nYou want only numbers greater than 20.\\n\\n```js\\nconst result = numbers.filter(number =\u003e number \u003e 20);\\n\\nconsole.log(result);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n[25, 30]\\n```\\n\\nThink:\\n\\n```text\\nfilter\\n ↓\\ncheck each element\\n ↓\\nkeep elements where condition = true\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.24 `map()` vs `filter()`\\n\\nVery important:\\n\\n### `map`\\n\\n**Transforms every element.**\\n\\n```js\\n[1, 2, 3].map(x =\u003e x * 2);\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\n[2, 4, 6]\\n```\\n\\n### `filter`\\n\\n**Selects some elements.**\\n\\n```js\\n[1, 2, 3].filter(x =\u003e x \u003e 1);\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\n[2, 3]\\n```\\n\\nMemory:\\n\\n```text\\nmap    → change\\nfilter → select\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.25 `find()`\\n\\nSuppose:\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40];\\n```\\n\\nFind the first number greater than 25:\\n\\n```js\\nconst result = numbers.find(number =\u003e number \u003e 25);\\n\\nconsole.log(result);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n30\\n```\\n\\nImportant:\\n\\n`find()` returns the **element**.\\n\\nIf nothing matches:\\n\\n```js\\nconst result = numbers.find(number =\u003e number \u003e 100);\\n\\nconsole.log(result);\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\nundefined\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.26 `findIndex()`\\n\\nIf you want the index:\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40];\\n\\nconst index = numbers.findIndex(number =\u003e number \u003e 25);\\n\\nconsole.log(index);\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\n2\\n```\\n\\nIf not found:\\n\\n```text\\n-1\\n```\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```text\\nfind()       → element\\nfindIndex()  → index\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.27 `some()`\\n\\nChecks whether **at least one** element satisfies a condition.\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 20, 35, 40];\\n\\nconst result = numbers.some(number =\u003e number \u003e 30);\\n\\nconsole.log(result);\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\ntrue\\n```\\n\\nBecause `35` and `40` satisfy the condition.\\n\\nThink:\\n\\n\u003e **Does at least one satisfy this?**\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.28 `every()`\\n\\nChecks whether **all** elements satisfy a condition.\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 20, 30];\\n\\nconst result = numbers.every(number =\u003e number \u003e 5);\\n\\nconsole.log(result);\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\ntrue\\n```\\n\\nBut:\\n\\n```js\\nconst result = numbers.every(number =\u003e number \u003e 20);\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\nfalse\\n```\\n\\nThink:\\n\\n```text\\nsome  → at least one?\\nevery → all?\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.29 `reduce()` ⭐⭐⭐\\n\\nThis is one of the most important methods for JavaScript interviews.\\n\\nSuppose:\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40];\\n```\\n\\nYou want the sum.\\n\\nNormal loop:\\n\\n```js\\nlet sum = 0;\\n\\nfor (const number of numbers) {\\n    sum += number;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nWith `reduce()`:\\n\\n```js\\nconst sum = numbers.reduce((total, number) =\u003e {\\n    return total + number;\\n}, 0);\\n\\nconsole.log(sum);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n100\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.30 Understand `reduce()` instead of memorizing it\\n\\nThis:\\n\\n```js\\nnumbers.reduce((total, number) =\u003e {\\n    return total + number;\\n}, 0);\\n```\\n\\nmeans:\\n\\n```text\\ninitial total = 0\\n\\n10 → total = 10\\n20 → total = 30\\n30 → total = 60\\n40 → total = 100\\n```\\n\\nFinal result:\\n\\n```text\\n100\\n```\\n\\nThe general idea:\\n\\n```text\\narray\\n ↓\\nprocess each element\\n ↓\\nmaintain accumulated result\\n ↓\\nreturn one final value\\n```\\n\\nThat's why it's called **reduce**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.31 `reduce()` isn't only for sums\\n\\nExample: product.\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4];\\n\\nconst result = numbers.reduce((product, number) =\u003e {\\n    return product * number;\\n}, 1);\\n\\nconsole.log(result);\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\n24\\n```\\n\\nIt can also build:\\n\\n- objects\\n- arrays\\n- frequency maps\\n- grouped data\\n- totals\\n- complex structures\\n\\nWe'll practice those later.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.32 The Big Three\\n\\nYou should remember this:\\n\\n```text\\nmap\\n ↓\\ntransform\\n\\nfilter\\n ↓\\nselect\\n\\nreduce\\n ↓\\naccumulate into one result\\n```\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];\\n\\nconst result = numbers\\n    .filter(x =\u003e x % 2 === 0)\\n    .map(x =\u003e x * 10);\\n\\nconsole.log(result);\\n```\\n\\nStep 1:\\n\\n```text\\n[1,2,3,4,5]\\n      ↓ filter even\\n[2,4]\\n```\\n\\nStep 2:\\n\\n```text\\n[2,4]\\n ↓ map ×10\\n[20,40]\\n```\\n\\nResult:\\n\\n```text\\n[20, 40]\\n```\\n\\nThis chaining style is **extremely common in JavaScript**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.33 `sort()` ⚠️ JavaScript trap\\n\\nHere's a famous one.\\n\\nYou have:\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 2, 30, 4];\\n\\nnumbers.sort();\\n\\nconsole.log(numbers);\\n```\\n\\nYou might expect:\\n\\n```text\\n[2, 4, 10, 30]\\n```\\n\\nBut you get:\\n\\n```text\\n[10, 2, 30, 4]\\n```\\n\\nWhy?\\n\\n**By default, `sort()` converts elements to strings and sorts lexicographically.**\\n\\nSo conceptually:\\n\\n```text\\n\\\"10\\\"\\n\\\"2\\\"\\n\\\"30\\\"\\n\\\"4\\\"\\n```\\n\\nAlphabetically:\\n\\n```text\\n\\\"10\\\"\\n\\\"2\\\"\\n\\\"30\\\"\\n\\\"4\\\"\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.34 Correct numeric sorting\\n\\nUse a comparator:\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 2, 30, 4];\\n\\nnumbers.sort((a, b) =\u003e a - b);\\n\\nconsole.log(numbers);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n[2, 4, 10, 30]\\n```\\n\\nDescending:\\n\\n```js\\nnumbers.sort((a, b) =\u003e b - a);\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.35 Why `a - b` works\\n\\nJavaScript's sort comparator follows the idea:\\n\\n```text\\nnegative → a before b\\nzero     → equal order\\npositive → b before a\\n```\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```js\\n(a, b) =\u003e a - b\\n```\\n\\nmeans:\\n\\n```text\\na \u003c b → negative → a first\\na = b → 0\\na \u003e b → positive → b first\\n```\\n\\nFor descending:\\n\\n```js\\n(b, a) =\u003e b - a\\n```\\n\\nor simply:\\n\\n```js\\n(a, b) =\u003e b - a\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3.36 Array method cheat sheet\\n\\n| Method | Purpose | Original modified? |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `push()` | Add end | ✅ |\\n| `pop()` | Remove end | ✅ |\\n| `shift()` | Remove beginning | ✅ |\\n| `unshift()` | Add beginning | ✅ |\\n| `slice()` | Extract portion | ❌ |\\n| `splice()` | Insert/remove | ✅ |\\n| `concat()` | Combine | ❌ |\\n| `includes()` | Check existence | ❌ |\\n| `indexOf()` | Find index | ❌ |\\n| `find()` | Find first element | ❌ |\\n| `findIndex()` | Find first index | ❌ |\\n| `some()` | At least one? | ❌ |\\n| `every()` | All? | ❌ |\\n| `map()` | Transform | ❌ |\\n| `filter()` | Select | ❌ |\\n| `reduce()` | Accumulate | ❌ |\\n| `sort()` | Sort | ✅* |\\n| `reverse()` | Reverse | ✅ |\\n\\n`sort()` modifies the array in place, so keep that in mind.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🚨 Important: Java → JavaScript mental mapping\\n\\nIf you know Java:\\n\\n```java\\nArrayList\u003cInteger\u003e list\\n```\\n\\nthink:\\n\\n```js\\nconst list = [];\\n```\\n\\nThen roughly:\\n\\n```text\\nJava ArrayList                 JavaScript Array\\n\\nadd(x)                         push(x)\\nremove(last)                   pop()\\nremove(0)                      shift()\\nadd(0, x)                      unshift(x)\\ncontains(x)                    includes(x)\\nindexOf(x)                     indexOf(x)\\nsize()                         length\\nsubList(...)                   slice(...)\\nsort(...)                      sort(...)\\n```\\n\\nBut don't assume they're identical internally or behaviorally.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Interview-level questions\\n\\n### 1. Difference between `slice()` and `splice()`?\\n\\n\u003e `slice()` creates a portion of an array without modifying the original, while `splice()` modifies the original array by removing, inserting, or replacing elements.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. Difference between `map()` and `forEach()`?\\n\\n`map()` returns a **new array** containing the results of transforming each element.\\n\\n`forEach()` simply executes a function for each element and does not produce a transformed array.\\n\\nWe'll explore this much more when we formally learn functions/callbacks.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. What does `filter()` return?\\n\\nA **new array containing elements that satisfy the callback condition**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 4. What does `find()` return?\\n\\nThe **first element** satisfying the condition, or `undefined` if none matches.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 5. `some()` vs `every()`?\\n\\n```text\\nsome  → at least one\\nevery → all\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 6. Why does this produce unexpected sorting?\\n\\n```js\\n[10, 2, 30, 4].sort()\\n```\\n\\nBecause default sorting is lexicographic/string-based rather than numeric.\\n\\nUse:\\n\\n```js\\n.sort((a, b) =\u003e a - b)\\n```\\n\\nfor ascending numeric sorting.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🧪 Your coding checkpoint\\n\\nDon't skip this. These exercises are what will make you able to **code without me**.\\n\\n### Problem 1\\n\\nGiven:\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50];\\n```\\n\\nCreate a new array:\\n\\n```text\\n[20, 40, 60, 80, 100]\\n```\\n\\nusing `map()`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Problem 2\\n\\nGiven:\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35];\\n```\\n\\nGet:\\n\\n```text\\n[20, 30]\\n```\\n\\nusing `filter()`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Problem 3\\n\\nFind the first number greater than `50`:\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 25, 40, 60, 70];\\n```\\n\\nusing `find()`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Problem 4\\n\\nCheck whether **any** number is negative:\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 20, -5, 30];\\n```\\n\\nusing `some()`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Problem 5\\n\\nCheck whether **every** number is positive:\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40];\\n```\\n\\nusing `every()`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Problem 6\\n\\nCalculate the sum using `reduce()`:\\n\\n```js\\nconst numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50];\\n```\\n\\nExpected:\\n\\n```text\\n150\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Problem 7 — Interview\\n\\nExplain in your own words:\\n\\n```text\\nmap vs filter vs reduce\\n```\\n\\nIf you can comfortably solve these, we'll move to **Phase 4 — Objects**, which is arguably even more important than arrays because JavaScript objects are at the center of **JS, JSON, APIs, Node.js and React**.\",{},{\"_197\":3489,\"_204\":3796,\"_54\":3797,\"_208\":3798,\"_210\":211,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":3799,\"_217\":218},\"2358cb15-d6ab-4e17-8b5c-ca999075b71c\",[3476],{\"_656\":657,\"_215\":3800},1789050152.4964898,{\"_651\":667,\"_668\":669},{\"_233\":234,\"_240\":3503,\"_242\":3503,\"_245\":3794,\"_248\":10,\"_666\":34},{},{\"_197\":3794,\"_204\":3804,\"_54\":3805,\"_208\":3806,\"_210\":211,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":3807,\"_217\":218},\"582fad75-aabb-4860-b795-00722f9c0f37\",[3489],{\"_656\":681,\"_215\":3810},1789050152.012321,{\"_651\":652,\"_653\":3809},{\"_678\":34,\"_246\":3808,\"_228\":67,\"_233\":234,\"_236\":3502,\"_238\":239,\"_240\":3503,\"_242\":3503,\"_243\":67,\"_66\":67,\"_245\":3802,\"_248\":10,\"_244\":14},\"d9c1e9d4-f264-4fdb-a3cb-0c6fd228253d\",[669],{},{\"_197\":3802,\"_204\":3814,\"_54\":3815,\"_56\":3816,\"_208\":3817,\"_210\":211,\"_213\