"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4495,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4494,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},\"50v7g1\",\"n667ev\",\"fvgcow\",\"bfanja\",\"2eoi8a\",\"ueo1tf\",\"g9jonm\",\"r63zdl\",\"huk32w\",\"zb63cc\",\"nqlxx9\",\"wp0rk8\",\"i7sxy4\",\"nr7c98\",\"yc6w44\",\"6liwb5\",\"s0jd9c\",\"aome0t\",\"3ry9q8\",\"9v9ch2\",\"ipppy9\",\"21e94d\",\"sv9gxc\",\"144jig\",\"gjfqt1\",\"x1f9xg\",\"4nmpln\",\"ssls6m\",\"3q8h0h\",\"kr5l9y\",\"gd7e9r\",\"01reta\",\"l8duea\",\"5ut6si\",\"1z3ntt\",\"3x7vaa\",\"ouqxn3\",\"yo8p86\",\"6ue559\",\"t1a6ta\",\"4au4pg\",\"u6i0ax\",\"ac6b6s\",\"z07dy9\",\"68mw0u\",\"d4auzv\",\"gd2siy\",\"d9x4fg\",\"d4y59r\",\"y3le6j\",\"3fwpuz\",\"a9j5xe\",\"begnld\",\"n962pc\",\"px7r3g\",\"sb2n36\",\"31lq1t\",\"f6l8zz\",\"ikk9mn\",\"1c09up\",\"mzjw9j\",\"hk794l\",\"i8qtea\",\"sav1i3\",\"p39cri\",\"9pgfun\",\"0ymf6i\",\"71vczj\",\"8rwz6w\",\"ifna1t\",\"nn4o9h\",\"lwb8mn\",\"ti3fxq\",\"hpggwq\",\"klsg91\",\"k5ik5q\",\"0d4db9\",\"mtdmta\",\"dqmnxc\",\"z514bk\",\"7d6qci\",\"q1zdcx\",\"w5fnmk\",\"qze1dt\",\"knwezt\",\"jn9t71\",\"i98mu6\",\"qvxnds\",\"vx7lhv\",\"mq8k9j\",\"gegeu8\",\"kyctn7\",\"1rv2c7\",\"j41x69\",\"qhhy4k\",\"l7ow4h\",\"3koaqf\",\"ia9jh7\",\"wi8rk7\",\"uka5n3\",\"zm851q\",\"u3zfmi\",\"alhv4f\",\"cdatvr\",\"i95yvs\",\"ay6j43\",\"etrnlb\",\"8k3xo9\",\"37ni4c\",\"dfwc54\",\"64a7wk\",\"py3woa\",\"uxcjo9\",\"xaadl0\",\"ug5cpl\",\"28czff\",\"y7r9di\",\"iz2p31\",\"xgvo7f\",\"zrn20n\",\"7tdd3f\",\"802xk8\",\"1z75a0\",\"yopzr3\",[650],[4620],\"Good. Since you're comfortable with the mental model, let's move on.\\n\\n# Phase 1 — Variables, Data Types \u0026 Type Conversion\\n\\nWe're going to go **Java → JavaScript**, not generic JavaScript teaching.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1.1 Declaring variables\\n\\nIn Java you know:\\n\\n```java\\nint age = 21;\\nString name = \\\"Dev\\\";\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript has three keywords:\\n\\n```js\\nlet age = 21;\\nconst name = \\\"Dev\\\";\\nvar oldWay = 10;\\n```\\n\\nFor modern JavaScript:\\n\\n\u003e **Use `const` by default. Use `let` when the value needs to change. Avoid `var`.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n## `let`\\n\\nUse when reassignment is needed.\\n\\n```js\\nlet age = 21;\\n\\nage = 22;\\n\\nconsole.log(age);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n22\\n```\\n\\nThink:\\n\\n```java\\nint age = 21;\\nage = 22;\\n```\\n\\nThe important difference is that JavaScript doesn't require you to declare the type.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## `const`\\n\\n```js\\nconst age = 21;\\n\\nage = 22; // ❌ TypeError\\n```\\n\\nYou cannot reassign the variable.\\n\\nBut remember the object example:\\n\\n```js\\nconst user = {\\n    name: \\\"Dev\\\"\\n};\\n\\nuser.name = \\\"Alex\\\"; // ✅\\n```\\n\\nWhy?\\n\\nBecause:\\n\\n```text\\nconst\\n ↓\\nprevents reassignment of the variable binding\\n```\\n\\nIt does **not** automatically freeze the object.\\n\\nWe'll return to this when we study objects.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1.2 `var` — understand it, don't use it\\n\\nYou will see this in older JavaScript code:\\n\\n```js\\nvar x = 10;\\n```\\n\\nThe biggest reason you need to understand it is **interviews**.\\n\\n`var` is function-scoped, whereas `let` and `const` are block-scoped.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```js\\nif (true) {\\n    var x = 10;\\n    let y = 20;\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(x); // 10\\nconsole.log(y); // ReferenceError\\n```\\n\\nWhy?\\n\\nThe `if` block creates a scope for `let`/`const`, but `var` doesn't respect that block scope.\\n\\nCompare with Java:\\n\\n```java\\nif (true) {\\n    int x = 10;\\n}\\n\\nSystem.out.println(x); // ❌\\n```\\n\\nJava's local variable is block-scoped.\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```js\\nvar x = 10;\\n```\\n\\nisn't block-scoped.\\n\\n### Interview answer\\n\\n\u003e `var` is function-scoped, while `let` and `const` are block-scoped.\\n\\nWe'll study **hoisting** later because it makes `var` even more interesting.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1.3 JavaScript data types\\n\\nJava has primitive types:\\n\\n```text\\nbyte\\nshort\\nint\\nlong\\nfloat\\ndouble\\nchar\\nboolean\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript has **7 primitive types**:\\n\\n```text\\nstring\\nnumber\\nbigint\\nboolean\\nundefined\\nnull\\nsymbol\\n```\\n\\nAnd one non-primitive category:\\n\\n```text\\nobject\\n```\\n\\nLet's go one by one.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1.4 `number`\\n\\nThis is a major difference.\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\nint a = 10;\\ndouble b = 10.5;\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```js\\nlet a = 10;\\nlet b = 10.5;\\n```\\n\\nBoth are:\\n\\n```js\\nnumber\\n```\\n\\nCheck:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(typeof a);\\nconsole.log(typeof b);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nnumber\\nnumber\\n```\\n\\nSo JavaScript doesn't have separate normal types like:\\n\\n```text\\nint\\nfloat\\ndouble\\n```\\n\\nInstead, ordinary numeric values use `number`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Special numeric values\\n\\nJavaScript also has:\\n\\n```js\\nInfinity\\n-Infinity\\nNaN\\n```\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(10 / 0);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nInfinity\\n```\\n\\nUnlike Java's integer division:\\n\\n```java\\n10 / 0\\n```\\n\\nwhich throws an `ArithmeticException`.\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```js\\n10 / 0\\n```\\n\\n→ `Infinity` because it's numeric floating-point semantics.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1.5 `NaN`\\n\\n`NaN` means:\\n\\n\u003e **Not a Number**\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(\\\"hello\\\" * 5);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nNaN\\n```\\n\\nInterestingly:\\n\\n```js\\ntypeof NaN\\n```\\n\\nreturns:\\n\\n```text\\n\\\"number\\\"\\n```\\n\\nYes, another JavaScript oddity.\\n\\nWhy?\\n\\n`NaN` is a special value within JavaScript's numeric type.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1.6 `string`\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\nString name = \\\"Dev\\\";\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```js\\nlet name = \\\"Dev\\\";\\n```\\n\\nYou can use:\\n\\n```js\\n\\\"Dev\\\"\\n'Dev'\\n`Dev`\\n```\\n\\nThe third one is called a **template literal**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Template literals\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\nString name = \\\"Dev\\\";\\nSystem.out.println(\\\"Hello \\\" + name);\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```js\\nlet name = \\\"Dev\\\";\\n\\nconsole.log(`Hello ${name}`);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nHello Dev\\n```\\n\\nThis:\\n\\n```js\\n${name}\\n```\\n\\nallows expressions inside template literals.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n```js\\nlet a = 10;\\nlet b = 20;\\n\\nconsole.log(`Sum = ${a + b}`);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nSum = 30\\n```\\n\\nYou'll use this constantly.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1.7 `boolean`\\n\\nSame basic concept:\\n\\n```js\\nlet isLoggedIn = true;\\nlet isAdmin = false;\\n```\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\nboolean isLoggedIn = true;\\n```\\n\\nVery similar.\\n\\nBut JavaScript additionally allows truthy/falsy values in conditions.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n```js\\nif (10) {\\n    console.log(\\\"runs\\\");\\n}\\n```\\n\\nWe'll explore this properly when we cover conditions.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1.8 `undefined`\\n\\nThis is important.\\n\\n```js\\nlet x;\\n\\nconsole.log(x);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nundefined\\n```\\n\\nThe variable exists, but no value was assigned.\\n\\nAlso:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction test() {\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(test());\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nundefined\\n```\\n\\nBecause the function doesn't explicitly return anything.\\n\\nJava programmers often initially think:\\n\\n\u003e \\\"Where is JavaScript's `void`?\\\"\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\nvoid test() {\\n}\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction test() {\\n}\\n```\\n\\nCalling it produces:\\n\\n```js\\nundefined\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1.9 `null`\\n\\n```js\\nlet user = null;\\n```\\n\\nMeans you deliberately have no value.\\n\\nSimilar conceptually to:\\n\\n```java\\nUser user = null;\\n```\\n\\nBut don't confuse:\\n\\n```js\\nlet user;\\n```\\n\\nwith:\\n\\n```js\\nlet user = null;\\n```\\n\\nFirst:\\n\\n```text\\nundefined\\n```\\n\\nSecond:\\n\\n```text\\nnull\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1.10 `bigint`\\n\\nFor very large integers:\\n\\n```js\\nconst x = 123456789012345678901234567890n;\\n```\\n\\nNotice the:\\n\\n```text\\nn\\n```\\n\\nat the end.\\n\\nCheck:\\n\\n```js\\ntypeof x\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\nbigint\\n```\\n\\nYou generally won't need this in normal web development, but it can appear in interviews.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1.11 `symbol`\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```js\\nconst id = Symbol(\\\"id\\\");\\n```\\n\\nSymbols create unique primitive values.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n```js\\nconst a = Symbol(\\\"id\\\");\\nconst b = Symbol(\\\"id\\\");\\n\\nconsole.log(a === b);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nfalse\\n```\\n\\nEven though both have `\\\"id\\\"` as their description.\\n\\nThis is more advanced and isn't something you'll use frequently at first.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1.12 Checking a type\\n\\nJavaScript gives you:\\n\\n```js\\ntypeof\\n```\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(typeof 10);\\nconsole.log(typeof \\\"hello\\\");\\nconsole.log(typeof true);\\nconsole.log(typeof undefined);\\nconsole.log(typeof 10n);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nnumber\\nstring\\nboolean\\nundefined\\nbigint\\n```\\n\\nBut remember:\\n\\n```js\\ntypeof null\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\nobject\\n```\\n\\nHistorical JavaScript quirk.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1.13 Type conversion\\n\\nNow we reach something **very important for Java programmers**.\\n\\nJava is generally strict about conversions.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n```java\\nString s = \\\"10\\\";\\n\\nint x = Integer.parseInt(s);\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript has explicit conversions **and** automatic coercion.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## String → Number\\n\\n```js\\nlet x = \\\"100\\\";\\n\\nlet n = Number(x);\\n\\nconsole.log(n);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n100\\n```\\n\\nCheck:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(typeof n);\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\nnumber\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Number → String\\n\\n```js\\nlet x = 100;\\n\\nlet s = String(x);\\n\\nconsole.log(typeof s);\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\nstring\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Boolean conversion\\n\\n```js\\nBoolean(1);      // true\\nBoolean(0);      // false\\nBoolean(\\\"hello\\\"); // true\\nBoolean(\\\"\\\");      // false\\nBoolean(null);    // false\\n```\\n\\nWe'll learn the exact truthy/falsy rules shortly.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1.14 Automatic type coercion 🔥\\n\\nThis is where JavaScript differs dramatically from Java.\\n\\nConsider:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(5 + \\\"10\\\");\\n```\\n\\nResult:\\n\\n```text\\n510\\n```\\n\\nWhy?\\n\\nJavaScript sees:\\n\\n```text\\nnumber + string\\n```\\n\\nand converts the number to a string:\\n\\n```text\\n\\\"5\\\" + \\\"10\\\"\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\n\\\"510\\\"\\n```\\n\\nBut:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(5 - \\\"2\\\");\\n```\\n\\nResult:\\n\\n```text\\n3\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript converts `\\\"2\\\"` to a number.\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```js\\n5 + \\\"2\\\"  // \\\"52\\\"\\n5 - \\\"2\\\"  // 3\\n5 * \\\"2\\\"  // 10\\n5 / \\\"2\\\"  // 2.5\\n```\\n\\nThis is called **type coercion**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1.15 The famous `+` trap\\n\\nRemember:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(10 + 20);\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\n30\\n```\\n\\nBut:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(\\\"10\\\" + 20);\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\n1020\\n```\\n\\nAnd:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(10 + 20 + \\\"30\\\");\\n```\\n\\nEvaluation happens left-to-right:\\n\\n```text\\n10 + 20\\n ↓\\n30\\n\\n30 + \\\"30\\\"\\n ↓\\n\\\"3030\\\"\\n```\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(10 + 20 + \\\"30\\\");\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\n3030\\n```\\n\\nBut:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(\\\"10\\\" + 20 + 30);\\n```\\n\\nFirst:\\n\\n```text\\n\\\"10\\\" + 20\\n↓\\n\\\"1020\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```text\\n\\\"1020\\\" + 30\\n↓\\n\\\"102030\\\"\\n```\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(\\\"10\\\" + 20 + 30);\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\n102030\\n```\\n\\nThis is a classic interview question.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1.16 Explicit vs implicit conversion\\n\\n### Explicit\\n\\nYou tell JavaScript to convert:\\n\\n```js\\nNumber(\\\"10\\\");\\nString(10);\\nBoolean(1);\\n```\\n\\n### Implicit\\n\\nJavaScript converts automatically:\\n\\n```js\\n\\\"10\\\" - 5\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\n5\\n```\\n\\nYou didn't explicitly ask for conversion.\\n\\nJava programmers should be particularly careful here.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1.17 `==` vs `===` revisited\\n\\nNow type coercion makes the difference clearer.\\n\\n```js\\n5 == \\\"5\\\"\\n```\\n\\n→ `true`\\n\\nbecause coercion occurs.\\n\\n```js\\n5 === \\\"5\\\"\\n```\\n\\n→ `false`\\n\\nbecause:\\n\\n```text\\nnumber\\n≠\\nstring\\n```\\n\\nTherefore:\\n\\n### Prefer\\n\\n```js\\n===\\n!==\\n```\\n\\n### Avoid casually using\\n\\n```js\\n==\\n!=\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1.18 One important JavaScript operator: `??`\\n\\nYou'll see this constantly in modern JS/React.\\n\\n```js\\nlet name = null;\\n\\nlet result = name ?? \\\"Guest\\\";\\n\\nconsole.log(result);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nGuest\\n```\\n\\n`??` means:\\n\\n\u003e Use the right side if the left side is `null` or `undefined`.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```js\\nlet name = undefined;\\n\\nconsole.log(name ?? \\\"Guest\\\");\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\nGuest\\n```\\n\\nBut:\\n\\n```js\\nlet name = \\\"\\\";\\n\\nconsole.log(name ?? \\\"Guest\\\");\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\n\\\"\\\"\\n```\\n\\nBecause empty string is **not** null/undefined.\\n\\nThis differs from `||`.\\n\\n```js\\nlet name = \\\"\\\";\\n\\nconsole.log(name || \\\"Guest\\\");\\n```\\n\\n→\\n\\n```text\\nGuest\\n```\\n\\nbecause `\\\"\\\"` is falsy.\\n\\nWe'll revisit this when we learn logical operators.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🧠 Java Programmer Cheat Sheet\\n\\nAt this point, your mental model should be:\\n\\n```text\\nJava                         JavaScript\\n\\nint/double                  number\\nString                      string\\nboolean                     boolean\\nnull                        null\\n(no direct equivalent)      undefined\\nlong huge integer           bigint\\nchar                        string (usually)\\nfinal                       const (not identical)\\nvariable                    let / const\\nInteger.parseInt(\\\"10\\\")      Number(\\\"10\\\")\\nString.valueOf(10)          String(10)\\nboolean conversion          Boolean(...)\\n==                           === is usually preferred\\ntype casting                explicit conversion/coercion\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🎯 Mini coding test\\n\\nDon't just read. Try predicting these **before running them**.\\n\\n### 1\\n\\n```js\\nlet x = 10;\\nx = \\\"JavaScript\\\";\\n\\nconsole.log(x);\\nconsole.log(typeof x);\\n```\\n\\n### 2\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(10 + \\\"20\\\");\\nconsole.log(10 - \\\"20\\\");\\nconsole.log(10 * \\\"2\\\");\\n```\\n\\n### 3\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(10 + 20 + \\\"30\\\");\\nconsole.log(\\\"10\\\" + 20 + 30);\\n```\\n\\n### 4\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(5 == \\\"5\\\");\\nconsole.log(5 === \\\"5\\\");\\n```\\n\\n### 5\\n\\n```js\\nlet x;\\n\\nconsole.log(x);\\nconsole.log(typeof x);\\n```\\n\\n### 6\\n\\n```js\\nlet x = null;\\n\\nconsole.log(typeof x);\\n```\\n\\n### 7\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(Boolean(0));\\nconsole.log(Boolean(1));\\nconsole.log(Boolean(\\\"\\\"));\\nconsole.log(Boolean(\\\"hello\\\"));\\n```\\n\\n### 8 — interview level\\n\\nWhat is the difference between:\\n\\n```js\\nlet x;\\n```\\n\\n```js\\nlet x = null;\\n```\\n\\nand:\\n\\n```js\\nlet x = 0;\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\nOnce these are clear, **Phase 1B will be Operators + Truthy/Falsy + Type Coercion in depth**, including the nasty interview expressions like:\\n\\n```js\\n[] == false\\n\\\"\\\" == false\\nnull == undefined\\n\\\"5\\\" + 2\\n\\\"5\\\" - 2\\n```\\n\\nAfter that we'll move into **conditions and loops**, then arrays/objects.\",{},{\"_197\":4354,\"_204\":4625,\"_54\":4626,\"_56\":4627,\"_208\":4628,\"_210\":211,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":4629,\"_217\":218},\"6283b2b5-5c8d-4c93-9c11-b8276b3d21f3\",[4341],{\"_656\":709,\"_215\":4634},1789040266.41,1789040291.797671,{\"_651\":652,\"_653\":4633},{\"_702\":4630,\"_236\":4367,\"_704\":-5,\"_240\":4368,\"_242\":4368,\"_246\":4631,\"_248\":10,\"_244\":14},{\"_705\":4632},\"cddcfc32-a71e-4248-9e88-74c4fc971398\",[],[239],{},{\"_197\":4623,\"_204\":4638,\"_54\":4639,\"_56\":4640,\"_208\":4641,\"_210\":211,\"_212\":34,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":4642,\"_217\":218,\"_219\":220},\"1e9e48e0-71d1-4128-9340-cedfd1e0239b\",[4354],{\"_6