\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4754,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4753,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4752,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4751,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4750,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4749,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4748,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4747,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4746,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4745,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4744,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4743,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4742,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4741,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4740,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4739,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4738,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4737,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4736,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4735,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4734,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4733,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4732,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4731,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4730,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4729,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4728,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4727,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},\"7exv2j\",\"ge85m3\",\"9o994i\",\"qqg417\",\"pfklxw\",\"vg5ipk\",\"are5r9\",\"18qcqm\",\"15vv18\",\"u65cam\",\"hs4g1r\",\"qaeceb\",\"5uygoo\",\"tbxh6j\",\"82eqmz\",\"x1m5il\",\"urcck6\",\"u8gk3d\",\"hhvkda\",\"0n8h08\",\"38ckyr\",\"skfr9f\",\"sgigly\",\"0u1lqt\",\"yxawij\",\"k8tyab\",\"vzggkw\",\"g2huu4\",\"uef6nv\",\"h79amw\",\"oocoo7\",\"xvo72r\",\"28t91u\",\"enoq73\",\"wufppr\",\"xua8mb\",\"nq1ifm\",\"s9qbk3\",\"q1l4eg\",\"ou3kb8\",\"1750bn\",\"2id7qj\",\"fm848k\",\"1zb3q9\",\"u0ojv2\",\"2edisj\",\"bfyk25\",\"e3gv8e\",\"89kdoo\",\"98aaqt\",\"8wk02h\",\"qvz8wx\",\"sg42jp\",\"co9lfu\",\"wtce68\",\"uejiwx\",\"3rb1zh\",\"mr7yd7\",\"xrzae4\",\"gxsuel\",\"1xg4s6\",\"b143y4\",\"lx7bba\",\"cpbul2\",\"4y0m3f\",\"xwlgsg\",\"arpp8a\",\"t3m20v\",\"ix1cka\",\"p747io\",\"dgcko8\",\"yjnnfu\",\"w9ayo4\",\"9v7aue\",\"7vrkw9\",[650],[4804],\"# Phase 0 — Java → JavaScript Mental Model\\n\\nSince you already know Core Java, I'll teach JavaScript by saying **\\\"In Java, you think X. In JavaScript, think Y.\\\"**\\n\\nDon't memorize everything in this lesson. The goal is to build the correct mental model.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0.1 Java and JavaScript are NOT the same kind of language\\n\\nThe names are misleading.\\n\\n| Java | JavaScript |\\n|---|---|\\n| General-purpose language | General-purpose scripting/programming language |\\n| Statically typed | Dynamically typed |\\n| Class-based OOP | Prototype-based object model |\\n| Compiled to bytecode | Typically JIT/interpreted by JS engines |\\n| JVM | JS engine such as V8 |\\n| Strong compile-time type checking | Types determined at runtime |\\n| Multithreading model | Single main JS execution thread + async runtime mechanisms |\\n\\nFor you, the biggest three differences initially are:\\n\\n**typing + objects + asynchronous execution.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 0.2 Variables — Java vs JavaScript\\n\\nYou already know Java:\\n\\n```java\\nint age = 20;\\nString name = \\\"Dev\\\";\\ndouble salary = 50000.5;\\n```\\n\\nIn JavaScript:\\n\\n```js\\nlet age = 20;\\nlet name = \\\"Dev\\\";\\nlet salary = 50000.5;\\n```\\n\\nNotice something important:\\n\\nJava tells you the type:\\n\\n```java\\nint age\\nString name\\ndouble salary\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript doesn't.\\n\\n```js\\nlet age = 20;\\nlet name = \\\"Dev\\\";\\nlet salary = 50000.5;\\n```\\n\\nThe JavaScript engine determines the type from the value.\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```js\\nlet x = 10;\\n\\nx = \\\"hello\\\";\\n\\nx = true;\\n```\\n\\nAll are valid.\\n\\nIn Java:\\n\\n```java\\nint x = 10;\\n\\nx = \\\"hello\\\"; // ❌ compile-time error\\n```\\n\\n### Mental model\\n\\n**Java**\\n\\n\u003e Variable has a declared type.\\n\\n**JavaScript**\\n\\n\u003e Variable can hold values of different types.\\n\\nWe'll later learn why this doesn't mean JavaScript is \\\"typeless.\\\"\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 0.3 `let`, `const`, and `var`\\n\\nThis is your first major JavaScript-specific topic.\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\nint x = 10;\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```js\\nlet x = 10;\\n```\\n\\nYou can reassign:\\n\\n```js\\nlet x = 10;\\nx = 20;\\n```\\n\\nBut:\\n\\n```js\\nconst x = 10;\\nx = 20; // ❌\\n```\\n\\n`const` means the variable binding cannot be reassigned.\\n\\n### But here's an important trap\\n\\n```js\\nconst user = {\\n    name: \\\"Dev\\\"\\n};\\n\\nuser.name = \\\"Alex\\\"; // ✅\\n```\\n\\nWhy?\\n\\nBecause `const` prevents:\\n\\n```js\\nuser = anotherObject;\\n```\\n\\nIt does **not** make the object immutable.\\n\\nWe'll go deep into this later.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### What about `var`?\\n\\n```js\\nvar x = 10;\\n```\\n\\nIt's the older variable declaration mechanism.\\n\\nModern JavaScript generally prefers:\\n\\n```js\\nlet\\nconst\\n```\\n\\nWe'll learn exactly why `var` behaves differently when we study **scope and hoisting**.\\n\\nFor now:\\n\\n\u003e **Use `const` by default. Use `let` when reassignment is required. Avoid `var` in modern code.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 0.4 Primitive types\\n\\nJava has:\\n\\n```text\\nbyte\\nshort\\nint\\nlong\\nfloat\\ndouble\\nchar\\nboolean\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript has:\\n\\n```text\\nnumber\\nbigint\\nstring\\nboolean\\nundefined\\nnull\\nsymbol\\n```\\n\\nand:\\n\\n```text\\nobject\\n```\\n\\nis the major non-primitive category.\\n\\n### Huge difference\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\nint x = 10;\\ndouble y = 10.5;\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```js\\nlet x = 10;\\nlet y = 10.5;\\n```\\n\\nBoth are:\\n\\n```js\\nnumber\\n```\\n\\nCheck:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(typeof x);\\nconsole.log(typeof y);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nnumber\\nnumber\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript's `number` normally represents both integers and floating-point numbers.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 0.5 `undefined` — Java programmers need to understand this\\n\\nJava doesn't have a direct equivalent of JavaScript's `undefined`.\\n\\nConsider:\\n\\n```js\\nlet x;\\n\\nconsole.log(x);\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nundefined\\n```\\n\\nThe variable exists, but it currently has no assigned value.\\n\\nCompare:\\n\\n```js\\nlet x;\\n```\\n\\nwith:\\n\\n```js\\nlet x = null;\\n```\\n\\nThese are different.\\n\\n### `undefined`\\n\\nUsually means:\\n\\n\u003e A value hasn't been provided/assigned.\\n\\n### `null`\\n\\nUsually means:\\n\\n\u003e Intentionally no value.\\n\\nThink approximately:\\n\\n```java\\nString name = null;\\n```\\n\\nis conceptually closer to:\\n\\n```js\\nlet name = null;\\n```\\n\\nBut JavaScript also has:\\n\\n```js\\nlet name;\\n```\\n\\n→ `undefined`\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 0.6 The weirdest JavaScript fact you'll see today\\n\\nRun:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(typeof null);\\n```\\n\\nWhat do you think?\\n\\nYou might expect:\\n\\n```text\\nnull\\n```\\n\\nActual result:\\n\\n```text\\nobject\\n```\\n\\n😐\\n\\nYes.\\n\\n```js\\ntypeof null === \\\"object\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThis is a historical behavior of JavaScript.\\n\\n**Interview question:**\\n\\n\u003e What is the type of `null` according to `typeof`?\\n\\nAnswer:\\n\\n\u003e `\\\"object\\\"`, due to a historical quirk in JavaScript.\\n\\nDon't try to rationalize it as good design.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 0.7 `==` vs `===`\\n\\nThis is **extremely important**.\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\n10 == 10\\n```\\n\\ncompares values for primitives.\\n\\nJavaScript has:\\n\\n```js\\n==\\n```\\n\\nand:\\n\\n```js\\n===\\n```\\n\\n### `==`\\n\\nLoose equality.\\n\\nIt may perform type conversion.\\n\\n```js\\n5 == \\\"5\\\"\\n```\\n\\nResult:\\n\\n```text\\ntrue\\n```\\n\\nBecause JavaScript converts `\\\"5\\\"` to a number for the comparison.\\n\\n### `===`\\n\\nStrict equality.\\n\\nNo implicit type conversion.\\n\\n```js\\n5 === \\\"5\\\"\\n```\\n\\nResult:\\n\\n```text\\nfalse\\n```\\n\\nbecause:\\n\\n```text\\nnumber !== string\\n```\\n\\n### Rule\\n\\nIn modern JavaScript:\\n\\n\u003e **Prefer `===` and `!==`.**\\n\\nUnless you specifically understand why you want loose equality.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 0.8 Truthy and falsy\\n\\nJava has:\\n\\n```java\\nif (condition)\\n```\\n\\nwhere condition must be a boolean.\\n\\nJavaScript is different.\\n\\nThis is valid:\\n\\n```js\\nif (5) {\\n    console.log(\\\"hello\\\");\\n}\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript treats `5` as **truthy**.\\n\\nThis:\\n\\n```js\\nif (0) {\\n    console.log(\\\"hello\\\");\\n}\\n```\\n\\ndoesn't execute because `0` is **falsy**.\\n\\nImportant falsy values include:\\n\\n```text\\nfalse\\n0\\n-0\\n0n\\n\\\"\\\"\\nnull\\nundefined\\nNaN\\n```\\n\\nAlmost everything else is truthy.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```js\\nlet name = \\\"\\\";\\n\\nif (name) {\\n    console.log(\\\"Name exists\\\");\\n}\\n```\\n\\nDoesn't execute.\\n\\nThis is very common in real JavaScript and React.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 0.9 Objects are fundamentally different from Java classes\\n\\nThis is probably the **most important conceptual difference** for you.\\n\\nIn Java:\\n\\n```java\\nclass Student {\\n    String name;\\n    int age;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```java\\nStudent s = new Student();\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript allows:\\n\\n```js\\nconst student = {\\n    name: \\\"Dev\\\",\\n    age: 21\\n};\\n```\\n\\nNo class required.\\n\\nYou can even add properties:\\n\\n```js\\nstudent.college = \\\"SRM\\\";\\n```\\n\\nNow:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(student);\\n```\\n\\ncontains:\\n\\n```text\\nname\\nage\\ncollege\\n```\\n\\nJava doesn't normally let you dynamically add a new field to an existing object.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 0.10 Functions are values\\n\\nThis is one of the biggest mindset changes.\\n\\nIn Java, you have methods:\\n\\n```java\\nint add(int a, int b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nSo far, familiar.\\n\\nBut JavaScript allows:\\n\\n```js\\nconst x = add;\\n```\\n\\nNow `x` contains the function.\\n\\nYou can do:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(x(10, 20));\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\n30\\n```\\n\\nYou can also:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction execute(operation) {\\n    return operation(10, 20);\\n}\\n\\nexecute(add);\\n```\\n\\nThis is possible because:\\n\\n\u003e **Functions are first-class values in JavaScript.**\\n\\nThis concept becomes extremely important when we learn:\\n\\n- callbacks\\n- `map`\\n- `filter`\\n- `reduce`\\n- promises\\n- event handlers\\n- React\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 0.11 JavaScript has a prototype-based object model\\n\\nYou know Java:\\n\\n```java\\nclass Animal {\\n    void eat() {}\\n}\\n\\nclass Dog extends Animal {\\n}\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript can look similar:\\n\\n```js\\nclass Animal {\\n    eat() {\\n        console.log(\\\"eating\\\");\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nclass Dog extends Animal {\\n}\\n```\\n\\nBut here's the important part:\\n\\n**JavaScript's underlying inheritance mechanism is prototypes.**\\n\\nThe `class` syntax is essentially a cleaner abstraction over the prototype system.\\n\\nWe'll eventually get to:\\n\\n```js\\nDog.prototype\\n```\\n\\nand:\\n\\n```js\\nObject.getPrototypeOf(dog)\\n```\\n\\nand the **prototype chain**.\\n\\nDon't worry about this yet.\\n\\nJust remember:\\n\\n\u003e Java → class-based object model  \\n\u003e JavaScript → prototype-based object model, with `class` syntax built on top of it\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 0.12 JavaScript execution is different\\n\\nThis becomes critical later.\\n\\nSuppose:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(\\\"A\\\");\\n\\nsetTimeout(() =\u003e {\\n    console.log(\\\"B\\\");\\n}, 0);\\n\\nconsole.log(\\\"C\\\");\\n```\\n\\nYou might think:\\n\\n```text\\nA\\nB\\nC\\n```\\n\\nBut you'll get:\\n\\n```text\\nA\\nC\\nB\\n```\\n\\nWhy?\\n\\nBecause JavaScript has an **event loop and asynchronous runtime model**.\\n\\nWe'll dedicate an entire phase to:\\n\\n```text\\nCall Stack\\n    ↓\\nWeb/Node APIs\\n    ↓\\nTask Queue\\n    ↓\\nMicrotask Queue\\n    ↓\\nEvent Loop\\n```\\n\\nDon't learn this yet.\\n\\nJust recognize that **JavaScript's execution model is not simply \\\"top-to-bottom like Java.\\\"**\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Your Java → JS mental map\\n\\nKeep this table in your head:\\n\\n| If you know Java... | JavaScript equivalent |\\n|---|---|\\n| `int x = 10` | `let x = 10` |\\n| `final` variable | `const` (roughly, but not identical) |\\n| `String` | `string` |\\n| `boolean` | `boolean` |\\n| `double/int` | `number` |\\n| `null` | `null` |\\n| no initialized value | `undefined` |\\n| `==` | Don't directly equate with JS `==` |\\n| `==` for strict primitive equality | `===` is usually the desired JS comparison |\\n| Class | `class` exists, but underlying model is prototypes |\\n| Method | Function / method |\\n| Interface | No exact equivalent |\\n| Method overloading | No traditional method overloading |\\n| Array | Array |\\n| `HashMap` | `Map` / object depending on use case |\\n| `HashSet` | `Set` |\\n| Lambda | Arrow function |\\n| Functional interface | Often callback/function |\\n| `try/catch` | `try/catch` |\\n| Thread-based thinking | Event-loop/async thinking |\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 🧠 First checkpoint\\n\\nBefore we move to Phase 1, make sure you can answer these **without looking above**.\\n\\n### Q1\\n\\nWhat happens?\\n\\n```js\\nlet x = 10;\\nx = \\\"hello\\\";\\nconsole.log(x);\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Q2\\n\\nWhat is the difference between:\\n\\n```js\\nlet x;\\n```\\n\\nand:\\n\\n```js\\nlet x = null;\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Q3\\n\\nWhat is the output?\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(5 == \\\"5\\\");\\nconsole.log(5 === \\\"5\\\");\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Q4\\n\\nWhat is the output?\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(typeof null);\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Q5\\n\\nIs this valid?\\n\\n```js\\nconst student = {\\n    name: \\\"Dev\\\"\\n};\\n\\nstudent.name = \\\"Alex\\\";\\n```\\n\\nWhy?\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Q6\\n\\nWhy can this work?\\n\\n```js\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\n\\nconst operation = add;\\n\\nconsole.log(operation(2, 3));\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Q7 — Java programmer trap\\n\\nWould this work in Java?\\n\\n```java\\nint x = 10;\\nx = \\\"hello\\\";\\n```\\n\\nWould the equivalent JavaScript work?\\n\\n```js\\nlet x = 10;\\nx = \\\"hello\\\";\\n```\\n\\nExplain **why they differ**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Answer these 7 yourself.** Don't worry about perfect wording. I'll correct them, then we'll start **Phase 1: Variables, Data Types \u0026 Type Conversion**, where we actually begin writing JavaScript programs.\",{},{\"_197\":4636,\"_204\":4809,\"_54\":4810,\"_56\":4811,\"_208\":4812,\"_210\":211,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":4813,\"_217\":218},\"3bb1f55d-2c44-423b-9776-9114b013dfb8\",[4623],{\"_656\":709,\"_215\":4819},1789039734.115,1789039759.043218,{\"_651\":652,\"_653\":4817},{\"_702\":4814,\"_236\":4649,\"_704\":-5,\"_240\":4650,\"_242\":4650,\"_246\":4815,\"_248\":10,\"_244\":14},{\"_705\":4816},\"02d8f123-0f87-4a57-9292-55557ae29ae5\",[],[4818],\"start\",{},{\"_197\":4807,\"_204\":4823,\"_54\":4824,\"_56\":4825,\"_208\":4826,\"_210\":211,\"_212\":34,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":4827,\"_217\":218,\"_219\":220},\"21663ad9-5f7e-43c5-a57b-06b7773ae935\",[4636],{\"_656\":657,\"_215\":4884},1789039706.096019,1789039723.871307,{\"_651\":652,\"_653\":4882},{\"_221\":4828,\"_223\":34,\"_224\":4829,\"_249\":4830,\"_226\":4831,\"_228\":67,\"_229\":4832,\"_231\":4833,\"_233\":234,\"_235\":34,\"_236\":4834,\"_238\":239,\"_240\":4835,\"_242\":4835,\"_243\":67,\"_66\":67,\"_244\":34,\"_245\":4821,\"_246\":4836,\"_248\":10},{\"_50\":647,\"_648\":4881},[],[],[],{\"_286\":4837,\"_288\":4838,\"_290\":4839,\"_292\":4840,\"_294\":4841,\"_296\":4842,\"_298\":4843,\"_300\":4844,\"_302\":4845,\"_304\":4846,\"_306\":4847,\"_308\":4848,\"_310\":4849,\"_312\":4850,\"_314\":4851,\"_316\":4852,\"_318\":4853,\"_320\":4854,\"_322\":4855,\"_324\":4856,\"_326\":4857,\"_328\":4858},[],\"69ac1e9d-e8b1-4697-89cb-3df760011525\",\"8d28e1bc-ed1a-4356-a52c-0350507b6f7d\",\"1d46efca-468f-4b12-bc35-61dd2a179ccb\",{\"_197\":4880,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4879,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4878,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4877,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4876,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4875,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4874,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4873,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4872,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4871,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4870,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4869,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4868,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4867,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4866,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4865,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4864,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4863,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4862,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4861,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4860,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},{\"_197\":4859,\"_525\":14,\"_526\":14,\"_527\":-5},\"rredpg\",\"udouf3\",\"wvexau\",\"2ens8q\",\"l30352\",\"vjizio\",\"hsqfhn\",\"jb43fb\",\"j5tm1q\",\"kdc3lz\",\"pkd8fy\",\"4ka4xg\",\"i6qlkf\",\"ib84r3\",\"8nfcai\",\"f2a1hb\",\"lm70h5\",\"kdvkqa\",\"2uidmw\",\"7wckok\",\"1p6jhj\",\"dcwb5v\",[650],[4883],\"Absolutely. Since you already know **Core Java properly**, I should **not teach JavaScript from zero like a beginner**. I’ll use Java as the reference point and explicitly show you where JavaScript behaves differently.\\n\\nOur goal will be:\\n\\n\u003e **Learn JavaScript completely enough to independently build programs, understand React/Node.js code, and answer JavaScript interview questions confidently.**\\n\\nI’ll teach it **phase-by-phase**, and I’ll avoid assuming that Java concepts transfer directly.\\n\\n## The roadmap\\n\\n### Phase 0 — Java → JavaScript mental model\\nBefore syntax, we'll establish the fundamental differences:\\n\\n- Java vs JavaScript execution model\\n- Static vs dynamic typing\\n- Primitive/reference behavior\\n- `var`, `let`, `const`\\n- `==` vs `===`\\n- `null` vs `undefined`\\n- Truthy/falsy\\n- Objects vs Java classes\\n- Functions as values\\n- JavaScript's prototype model\\n- `this`\\n- Event loop\\n\\nThis phase prevents the biggest Java-programmer mistakes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Phase 1 — Core JavaScript syntax\\n\\nYou'll learn:\\n\\n- Variables\\n- Data types\\n- Operators\\n- Type conversion/coercion\\n- Strings\\n- Template literals\\n- Conditions\\n- Loops\\n- `switch`\\n- Arrays\\n- Objects\\n- Destructuring\\n- Spread/rest operators\\n\\nFor every concept I'll use:\\n\\n**Java → JavaScript → Important difference → Example → Interview questions → Practice**\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Phase 2 — Functions ⭐\\n\\nThis is where JavaScript starts becoming very different from Java.\\n\\nWe'll cover:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```js\\nconst add = (a, b) =\u003e a + b;\\n```\\n\\nAnd understand:\\n\\n- Function declarations\\n- Function expressions\\n- Arrow functions\\n- Parameters\\n- Default parameters\\n- Rest parameters\\n- Callback functions\\n- Higher-order functions\\n- Closures\\n- IIFE\\n- Scope\\n- Lexical scope\\n- Hoisting\\n\\nYou'll understand **why JavaScript can treat functions like objects/data**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Phase 3 — Arrays + Objects deeply\\n\\nNot just basic syntax.\\n\\nYou'll master:\\n\\n```js\\nmap()\\nfilter()\\nreduce()\\nfind()\\nfindIndex()\\nsome()\\nevery()\\nsort()\\nforEach()\\n```\\n\\nAnd object operations:\\n\\n```js\\nObject.keys()\\nObject.values()\\nObject.entries()\\nObject.assign()\\n```\\n\\nPlus:\\n\\n- Shallow vs deep copy\\n- Reference behavior\\n- Spread syntax\\n- Destructuring\\n- Optional chaining\\n- Nullish coalescing\\n\\nThis is extremely important for **React interviews**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Phase 4 — JavaScript OOP\\n\\nBecause you know Java, I'll explicitly compare:\\n\\n```java\\nclass Student {\\n    String name;\\n\\n    void study() {}\\n}\\n```\\n\\nwith:\\n\\n```js\\nclass Student {\\n    constructor(name) {\\n        this.name = name;\\n    }\\n\\n    study() {}\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThen we'll go deeper:\\n\\n- Classes\\n- Constructors\\n- Instance methods\\n- Static methods\\n- Inheritance\\n- `super`\\n- Getters/setters\\n- Encapsulation\\n- `extends`\\n- `instanceof`\\n\\nBut importantly, I'll explain **why JavaScript classes aren't actually the same object model as Java classes**.\\n\\nWe'll cover:\\n\\n- Prototypes\\n- Prototype chain\\n- `__proto__`\\n- `prototype`\\n- Constructor functions\\n\\nThis is an important interview area.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Phase 5 — `this` 🔥\\n\\nWe'll spend serious time here.\\n\\nYou'll learn why these behave differently:\\n\\n```js\\nobj.method()\\n```\\n\\n```js\\nconst fn = obj.method;\\nfn();\\n```\\n\\n```js\\nconst fn = () =\u003e {};\\n```\\n\\nAnd:\\n\\n```js\\ncall()\\napply()\\nbind()\\n```\\n\\nYou'll be able to answer interview questions such as:\\n\\n\u003e What determines the value of `this` in JavaScript?\\n\\n\u003e Why doesn't an arrow function have its own `this`?\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Phase 6 — Advanced JavaScript\\n\\nWe'll cover:\\n\\n- Execution context\\n- Call stack\\n- Scope chain\\n- Hoisting\\n- Temporal Dead Zone\\n- Closures\\n- Garbage collection\\n- Event loop\\n- Microtasks\\n- Macrotasks\\n- Promises\\n- Async/await\\n- Callbacks\\n- Error handling\\n\\nFor example, you'll eventually be able to explain this without memorizing:\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(\\\"A\\\");\\n\\nsetTimeout(() =\u003e {\\n    console.log(\\\"B\\\");\\n}, 0);\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(() =\u003e {\\n    console.log(\\\"C\\\");\\n});\\n\\nconsole.log(\\\"D\\\");\\n```\\n\\nand correctly predict:\\n\\n```text\\nA\\nD\\nC\\nB\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Phase 7 — Browser JavaScript\\n\\nThen we'll move from the language itself to the browser:\\n\\n- DOM\\n- BOM\\n- Events\\n- Event bubbling\\n- Event capturing\\n- Event delegation\\n- Forms\\n- LocalStorage\\n- SessionStorage\\n- Cookies\\n- Fetch API\\n- HTTP basics\\n- JSON\\n\\nYou'll learn to build things without frameworks.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Phase 8 — Modules + modern JavaScript\\n\\nYou'll master:\\n\\n```js\\nimport\\nexport\\n```\\n\\nand:\\n\\n- CommonJS\\n- ES Modules\\n- npm\\n- package.json\\n- dependencies\\n- devDependencies\\n- semantic versioning\\n- bundlers\\n- Babel\\n- Vite basics\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Phase 9 — Node.js\\n\\nOnly after JavaScript itself is solid.\\n\\nWe'll cover:\\n\\n- Node runtime\\n- npm\\n- modules\\n- filesystem\\n- HTTP\\n- Express\\n- REST APIs\\n- middleware\\n- environment variables\\n- authentication basics\\n- async backend programming\\n\\nThis will make your transition toward **Spring Boot ↔ Node/Express** much easier.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Phase 10 — Interview mastery\\n\\nFinally I'll train you on:\\n\\n**Basic questions**\\n\\n\u003e `var` vs `let` vs `const`?\\n\\n**Intermediate**\\n\\n\u003e What is a closure?\\n\\n**Advanced**\\n\\n\u003e Explain the event loop.\\n\\n**Tricky**\\n\\n\u003e What does this output?\\n\\n```js\\nconsole.log(typeof null);\\n```\\n\\n**Java-vs-JS questions**\\n\\n\u003e Is JavaScript pass-by-value or pass-by-reference?\\n\\n\u003e Is JavaScript object-oriented?\\n\\n\u003e Does JavaScript support method overloading?\\n\\n\u003e Difference between Java interfaces and JavaScript prototypes?\\n\\nAnd coding problems:\\n\\n```text\\nEasy → Medium → Hard\\n```\\n\\nusing JavaScript.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# How I will teach you\\n\\nThis is important.\\n\\nI **won't** do:\\n\\n\u003e Here's JavaScript syntax. Memorize it.\\n\\nInstead, every major topic will look roughly like:\\n\\n### 1. Java concept\\n\\n```java\\nint x = 10;\\n```\\n\\n### 2. JavaScript equivalent\\n\\n```js\\nlet x = 10;\\n```\\n\\n### 3. Critical difference\\n\\nJava:\\n\\n```java\\nint x = 10;\\nx = \\\"hello\\\"; // compile-time error\\n```\\n\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n```js\\nlet x = 10;\\nx = \\\"hello\\\"; // valid\\n```\\n\\nBecause JavaScript is dynamically typed.\\n\\n### 4. Then we go beyond Java\\n\\nFor example, Java doesn't have a direct equivalent to JavaScript's:\\n\\n```js\\nconst fn = (x) =\u003e x * 2;\\n```\\n\\nbecause **functions are first-class values** in JavaScript.\\n\\n### 5. Then interview questions\\n\\nI'll give you questions you should be able to answer.\\n\\n### 6. Then coding\\n\\nI'll make you actually write JavaScript.\\n\\n### 7. Then interview traps\\n\\nI'll specifically point out:\\n\\n\u003e ⚠️ **Java programmer trap**\\n\\nwhere your Java knowledge can lead you to the wrong JavaScript answer.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# One important rule\\n\\nWhen you ask me something like:\\n\\n\u003e \\\"Teach me objects\\\"\\n\\nI will **not jump ahead to prototypes, classes, inheritance, React state, etc.**\\n\\nWe'll progress in order.\\n\\nAnd when something requires a concept we haven't learned yet, I'll say:\\n\\n\u003e **We'll come back to this in Phase 5.**\\n\\nrather than pretending you already know it.\\n\\nThat will keep the learning path coherent.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Your final target\\n\\nBy the end, you should be able to look at:\\n\\n```js\\nconst users = data\\n    .filter(user =\u003e user.active)\\n    .map(user =\u003e ({\\n        ...user,\\n        name: user.name.toUpperCase()\\n    }));\\n```\\n\\nand understand **every single part of it**, rather than recognizing it as \\\"some JavaScript syntax.\\\"\\n\\nAnd you should be able to write something like this yourself:\\n\\n```js\\nclass Employee {\\n    constructor(name, salary) {\\n        this.name = name;\\n        this.salary = salary;\\n    }\\n\\n    getAnnualSalary() {\\n        return this.salary * 12;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nconst employees = [\\n    new Employee(\\\"A\\\", 50000),\\n    new Employee(\\\"B\\\", 70000)\\n];\\n\\nconst highEarners = employees\\n    .filter(e =\u003e e.salary \u003e 60000)\\n    .map(e =\u003e e.name);\\n\\nconsole.log(highEarners);\\n```\\n\\n**without needing me to generate it.**\\n\\nThat's the standard we'll aim for.\\n\\n## Let's start: Phase 0 — Java → JavaScript mental model\\n\\nThe **first lesson is not syntax**. We'll establish the 10 fundamental differences between Java and JavaScript first. Then we'll move into variables and data types.\",{},{\"_197\":4821,\"_204\":4888,\"_54\":4889,\"_208\":4890,\"_210\":211,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":4891,\"_217\":218},\"5e0d6c4d-680d-4a3d-9990-6f5f39acc598\",[4807],{\"_656\":657,\"_215\":4892},1789039706.0970922,{\"_651\":667,\"_668\":669},{\"_233\":234,\"_240\":4835,\"_242\":4835,\"_245\":4886,\"_248\":10,\"_666\":34},{},{\"_197\":4886,\"_204\":4896,\"_54\":4897,\"_208\":4898,\"_210\":211,\"_213\":214,\"_215\":4899,\"_217\":218},\"9ed3b4f2-d349-497d-92ff-293fd16fdd12\",[4821],{\"_656\":681,\"_215\":4902},1789039705.881758,{\"_651\":652,\"_653\":4901},{\"_678\":34,\"_246\":4900,\"_228\":67,\"_233\":234,\"_236\":4834,\"_238\":239,\"_240\":4835,\"_242\":4835,\"_243\":67,\"_66\":67,\"_245\":4894,\"_248\":10,\"_244\":14},\"b38213e8-e79e-4a98-9683-fae0992bcba9\",[669],{},{\"_197\":4894,\"_204\":4906,\"_54\":4907,\"_208\":4908,\"_210\":211,\"_212\":34,\"_213\":689,\"_215\":4909,\"_217\":218},\"67791926-88d2-4f2b-897d-a1b4de28c273\",[4886],{\"_656\":681,\"_215\":4911},1789039706.722608,{\"_651\":652,\"_653\":4910},{\"_1107\":34,\"_678\":34,\"_246\":4900,\"_228\":67,\"_233\":234,\"_236\":4834,\"_238\":239,\"_240\":4835,\"_242\":4835,\"_243\":67,\"_66\":67,\"_245\":4904,\"_248\":10,\"_244\":14},[669],{},{\"_197\":4904,\"_204\":4915,\"_54\":4916,\"_208\":4917,\"_210\":211,\"_212\":34,\"_213\":689,\"_215\":4918,\"_217\":218},\"61fa3672-8e94-4c05-bcda-9b35abc29701\",[4894],{\"_656\":681,\"_215\":4920},1789039706.704474,{\"_651\":652,\"_653\":4919},{\"_691\":34,\"_678\":34,\"_246\":4900,\"_228\":67,\"_233\":234,\"_236\":4834,\"_238\":239,\"_240\":4835,\"_242